   'One row the source object rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_source_object stores: the claims that took effect.',
   'For example the occurrence-depths stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph and one depth into graphitron_occurrence_source_object, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'Which claim takes effect, stated once over the stored claims: a claim on an occurrence holds unless one of a higher rank claims it too, ownServiceRecord over every other rule and recordParameter over the rest, as a child''s own service record and a record a service call takes override what the occurrence would otherwise read. The rank is a function of the rule and so no column of the claim.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_source', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'What one occurrence''s values come from: one row per occurrence that has a source.',
   'For example Film.title''s values are rows of film, of kind TABLE, and Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb''s are read off an instance of FilmBlurbHolder, of kind OBJECT.',
   'Every path starts from a source, so where an occurrence''s values come from is stated on the occurrence itself, rows of tables or an instance of a class, rather than as a pointer to another occurrence a reader would follow. How the writer found it is not stored. Written a depth at a time, each depth reading only what the depth above stated, and the kind is carried into each subtype''s key so the database refuses a table row under an object source.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_source_table', 'occurrence-source-table', 'graphitron',
   'One table an occurrence''s values are rows of: one row per occurrence and table.',
   'For example Film.details.length is read from film, and an argument of a field returning a union of Film and Language has a row for film and one for language.',
   'A join path starts from one of these rows, so where a parent forks each table is a row of its own. The table is referenced through a copy of its key its removal sets null, so the sweep, not a cascade, removes a row the reading no longer finds.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_source_object', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'The class an occurrence''s values are an instance of: one row per occurrence whose source is an object.',
   'For example Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb is read off the FilmBlurbHolder its parent''s service hands back.',
   'A value a producer hands back with a class but no table record and no fill is rows of no table, so its occurrences read it as an object, the start of an accessor path. The class is the parent field''s own producer''s result and nothing else, so a source never guesses among the classes a type may be backed by.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need_claim', 'path-need-claim', 'graphitron',
   'A rule''s claim that one occurrence must reach one type, with where it starts and lands: one row per occurrence, type and rule.',
   'For example a field under Query.films whose target is bound to the language table is claimed by fieldTableBound, starting on film and landing on language.',
   'The candidates the need writer evaluated as one union of arms, stored, one rule view per arm, so each is a statement H2 plans alone; the union was the costliest statement in the depth loop to plan. Which claim takes effect is the need rule view''s to state, a record a service call takes over every other rule on its occurrence. Written a depth at a time by the depth loop, every rule reading the occurrence''s own source at that depth.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need_claim_field_table_bound_rule', 'path-need-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One claim the fieldTableBound rule makes, in the shape graphitron_path_need_claim stores: a field whose target has a table of its own and is no interface reaches it, landing there.',
   'For example the occurrence-depths stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph and one depth into graphitron_path_need_claim, the rule''s own and no other.',
   'One arm of what the need writer once evaluated as a single union, kept in the catalog as a rule of its own so H2 plans it alone and the gates reading stored definitions see what it reads. It reads no reading''s mark: every stored row it reads is current when it runs.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need_claim_field_member_rule', 'path-need-claim', 'graphitron',
