   'One row the path need table rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_need_table stores: the claims that took effect.',
   'For example the occurrence-depths stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph and one depth into graphitron_path_need_table, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'Which claim takes effect, stated once over the stored claims: a claim holds unless its occurrence has a recordParameter claim and it is not one, as a record a service call takes overrides what the occurrence would otherwise reach. The rank is a function of the rule and so no column of the claim.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'One type an occurrence must reach from its source: one row per occurrence and target type, with whether the path toward it starts on a table or on an object.',
   'For example Query.search(languageId:), an argument of a field returning a union of Film and Language, needs Film and Language, each from a table.',
   'The need the paths meet, whichever side its source is on: a join path meets a need starting on a table, an accessor path one starting on an object, so graphitron_path references the need and each start kind details it in a subtype of its own, the supertype holding the key, the kind, the mark and the target type. Written by the occurrence-depths stage a depth at a time; a need a reading no longer finds is swept, taking its subtype, path, steps and stop.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need_table', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'One type an occurrence with a table source must reach: one row per occurrence and target type, with the source table it starts on and the table it lands on.',
   'For example Query.search(languageId:), an argument of a field returning a union of Film and Language, needs Film from film and Language from language, and Film.languageName, a scalar that writes a path, needs String from film with its landing not yet known.',
   'What an occurrence must reach is decided by its target types, each expanded to its members with a table, before any path is looked for, so a need no path meets is a defect at its claim rather than a row a reader fails to find. Keyed by the occurrence''s nested coordinate and the target type, as a claim by its element and target type, so a fork is a target of its own. Only an occurrence with a table source has needs, its start one of its source table rows, and an argument or input field copies its parent''s. The start, the target type and the landing are referenced through copies of their keys their removal sets null, so the sweep, not a cascade, removes a need the reading no longer finds.'),
  ('graphitron_path', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'How one need is met: one row per need a path meets, with its kind and where it came from.',
   'For example Staff.homeAddress, inherited from Person.homeAddress, meets its need for Address by a JOIN path of origin INHERITED, and Film.title by an EMPTY one ending where it starts.',
   'One path per need, keyed by the need so the two cannot disagree on what was asked, in either world: a join path from a table source, an accessor path from an object source. The kind is carried into each subtype''s key so a subtype of the other kind cannot be written, and the origin says whether it was written, inherited, inferred or empty, so an empty path is never read as a failed inference.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'The table a join path reaches: one row per path of kind JOIN.',
   'For example Customer.address reaches address, and Film.title, an empty path, reaches film, where it starts.',
   'The table side of a path: what a join path reaches, never null, its start where it is empty. The table is referenced through a copy of its key its removal sets null, so the sweep, not a cascade, removes a path the reading no longer finds.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_claimed', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'The claim a written or inherited join path follows: one row per join path of origin WRITTEN or INHERITED, the origin being the supertype''s and so out of reach of a check here.',
   'For example Staff.homeAddress, inherited from Person.homeAddress, follows the claim (Person.homeAddress, Address), which lies in another file.',
   'A written or inherited path names the claim it follows by its element and target type rather than by position, since an inherited path''s claim may lie in another file, and as a copy a swept claim sets null. An inherited path is a row per implementation, so no reader joins through implementations; an inferred one is claimed by nothing.'),
  ('graphitron_accessor_path', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'The class an accessor path reaches: one row per path of kind ACCESSOR.',
   'For example Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb, read off a FilmBlurbHolder, reaches FilmBlurb by its blurb() accessor.',
   'The object side of a path: what an accessor path reaches, written by field names and @field(name:). Declared beside the join path so the supertype''s kind has both its subtypes; its steps come when value objects are bound.'),
  ('graphitron_path_need_object', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'One type an occurrence with an object source must reach: one row per occurrence and target type, with the class it starts on.',
   'For example Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb, read off the FilmBlurbHolder its parent''s service hands back, needs FilmBlurb from FilmBlurbHolder.',
   'The object side of the need, beside graphitron_path_need_table: an accessor path meets it as a join path meets a table need, so the two sides share one need and one path supertype. Written by the occurrence-depths stage after the need, marked and swept with it.'),
  ('graphitron_accessor_path_step', 'accessor-path-step', 'graphitron',
   'One member call an accessor path makes: one row per path and position, naming the member''s code_read_slot row.',
   'For example Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb calls FilmBlurbHolder.blurb() at position 0; a path where the object passes through calls nothing and has no step.',
   'What an accessor path calls, as a join path''s steps say what it joins, so a reader renders the call off the row rather than finding the member again. One step today, a bound name being one member. Written by the occurrence-depths stage with its path.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_condition', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'A @condition applied to one table its occurrence binds: one row per occurrence carrying a @condition and table it binds whose call pinned a method.',
   'For example Query.occupantsTyped(firstName:) on customer and staff is two rows, each calling the occupantTypedNamePrefix overload taking that table.',
   'A call subtype keyed into the binding rather than a view re-deriving the table, so a condition goes when the table it was applied to does; the overload is the one whose table parameter takes the bound table, its own class over any table. Written by occurrence-bindings after the bindings, with its candidates, call, method and fills.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_condition_rule', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'One row the condition call rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_condition stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-bindings stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_condition, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_condition_candidate_rule', 'graph-occurrence-call-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the condition candidate rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_call_candidate stores for a @condition''s call: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-bindings stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_call_candidate, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The condition''s own candidate rule beside the service''s, so neither kind''s rule branches on the other; the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_condition_parameter_rule', 'graph-occurrence-call-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'One row the condition parameter rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter stores for a @condition''s call: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-bindings stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The condition''s own fill rule beside the service''s, its rules the manual''s @condition reference, so neither kind''s rule branches on the other; the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_table', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'The table an occurrence''s rows or value land on: one row per TABLE binding.',
   'For example Query.films binds public.film, its rows, and Query.films(filter:), an input object whose fields filter film, binds public.film where its need lands.',
   'The table side of a binding beside COLUMN and NODE_ID, so every occurrence that filters or is filtered binds a table and a @condition call is keyed by the binding rather than by a view re-deriving the table. Written by occurrence-bindings by a writer of its own after the node id and column bindings, reading stored rows only: the landing is the join path''s target where a path was walked, else the need''s.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_table_rule', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'One row the table binding rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_binding_table stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-bindings stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_binding and graphitron_occurrence_binding_table, which are the names every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_built_class', 'occurrence-built-class', 'graphitron',
   'The class an input object occurrence is built into: one row per occurrence and class.',
   'For example Mutation.assignFilmRecordList(in:) is built into FilmRecordListAssignment, the class its @service parameter takes, and Mutation.customerUpsert(in:).details into CustomerRecord, the record its parent is built into, as a grouping.',
   'Which class an input object is built into depends on the call that takes it, not on its type alone, so it is the occurrence''s and not graphitron_type_backing''s: a @condition''s bean belongs to its parameter and does not back the input type. Stored so the member bindings below can name their parent''s row by foreign key, a grouping''s row carrying the enclosing class its fields fill.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_built_class_rule', 'occurrence-built-class', 'graphitron',
   'The class an input object occurrence is built into: one row per occurrence and class, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_built_class stores, with the depth the writer steps through and when the parent row it reads was written.',
   'For example Mutation.assignFilmRecordList(in:) is built into FilmRecordListAssignment, the class its @service parameter takes, and Mutation.customerUpsert(in:).details into CustomerRecord, the record its parent is built into, as a grouping.',
   'The rule the stored relation is written from, depth by depth after the depth loop, a member''s or a grouping''s row reading its parent''s.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_member', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'The member of the class an input object is built into that an input field fills: one row per MEMBER binding.',
   'For example Mutation.assignFilmRecordList(in:).films fills FilmRecordListAssignment#films, the record component at position 0 of its canonical constructor.',
   'The MEMBER subtype of graphitron_occurrence_binding, the write side''s twin of VALUE: where VALUE reads a member off the object a field reads from, MEMBER fills one of the class an input object is built into. Decided here once, matched on the name the parent''s built class says, so a service''s and a condition''s bean are read alike.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_member_rule', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'The member of the class an input object is built into that an input field fills: one row per MEMBER binding, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_binding_member stores, with when the parent row it reads was written.',
   'For example Mutation.assignFilmRecordList(in:).films fills FilmRecordListAssignment#films, the record component at position 0 of its canonical constructor.',
   'The rule the stored relation is written from, with the built classes of the depth above it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_parameter', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'The call parameter an argument or input field supplies: one row per occurrence and parameter it binds.',
   'For example Query.filmsByService(ids:) binds the ids parameter of SampleQueryService.filmsByService.',
   'Every producer is a call, so what an argument does for a call is a binding like any other rather than the absence of one: a reader at an argument asks its bindings what takes it. Written from the ARGUMENT fills by the stage owning the call''s kind, the service calls'' with the producers ahead of the depth loop and the condition calls'' with the bindings after it, each sweeping its own; the fill''s landing_coordinate stays beside it until its readers move.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_parameter_rule', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'One row the parameter binding rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_binding_parameter stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-producers stage writes this view''s service rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_binding and graphitron_occurrence_binding_parameter, which are the names every reader spells.',
   'One rule for every kind of call, each stage writing the rows of its own calls'' kind, so neither stage restates it. Kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_value', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'The member an occurrence''s value is read by off the object it reads from: one row per VALUE binding, the class read from, the class of the value and the member''s code_read_slot row, none where the object passes through.',
   'For example Query.filmBlurbHolder.blurb binds FilmBlurbHolder#blurb, a FilmBlurb, and a payload''s data element that its producer''s class backs binds that class itself.',
   'The object side of a binding, beside COLUMN and NODE_ID on the table side: what an occurrence takes is the value at its accessor path''s end, so a reader of the object side, the outbox''s $result over a service''s value and the service flip''s object reads, reads one binding rather than walking the object again. Written by the occurrence-values stage from graphitron_occurrence_value_rule, under a VALUE row of graphitron_occurrence_binding, marked and swept by kind.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_value_rule', 'graph-occurrence-binding', 'graphitron',
   'One row the value rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_binding_value stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-values stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_binding_value, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step', 'path-step', 'graphitron',
   'One step a join path takes: one row per need and position the path toward it stepped through, from one table to the next, with the place of the written element it resolved where one did.',
   'For example Customer.city over @reference(path: [{key: "customer_address_id_fkey"}, {key: "address_city_id_fkey"}]) is two steps, customer to address and address to city.',
   'The path as steps between tables, keyed by the need so a stopped need keeps the steps it took beside its stop, each naming the element it resolved by its place in the claim''s written list rather than by where it was written, so a step does not move when the file around its element is edited. How the step joins is its children''s.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_column', 'path-step-column', 'graphitron',
   'A column pair a step of a join path joins on: one row per step and pair.',
   'For example the step from customer to address joins customer.address_id to address.address_id, one pair.',
   'A join is column pairs, and the key the step rode is where they came from, so a foreign key ridden either way and a function''s result matched by name are one join form.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_key', 'path-step-key', 'graphitron',
   'The written foreign key a step of a join path rode: one row per step whose claim names its key.',
   'For example {key: "customer_address_id_fkey"} from customer rides customer_address_id_fkey; a key the step inferred is graphitron_join_path_step_inferred_key''s.',
   'Provenance for the editor and the messages, which name the key a step rode where the author wrote it; the columns are the join. A key the step inferred is its sibling''s, so a reader tells a written key from an inferred one by which relation holds it.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_inferred_key', 'path-step-key', 'graphitron',
   'The one foreign key between a step''s two tables, which the step rode where nothing named a key: one row per step whose key is inferred.',
   'For example the step from customer to address where nothing is written, or {table: "address"} is, rides customer_address_id_fkey, the one key joining them.',
   'Inference is a step''s, never a path''s: where an element names only a table or nothing is written, the step rides the one foreign key between its two tables, and that key is this row. No shared key or several is a defect at the claim, so a step with an inferred key always has exactly one.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_primary_key', 'path-step-key', 'graphitron',
   'The primary key a step of a join path rode to join a row to itself: one row per step whose {key:} names its table''s own primary key.',
   'For example Film.sameRow over @reference(path: [{key: "film_pkey"}]) is one step from film back to film on film_pkey, reading the parent''s own row.',
   'Provenance for the editor and the messages, which name the key a step rode; the columns, each primary-key column paired with itself, are the join. A sibling of graphitron_join_path_step_key, whose foreign key to sql_referential_constraint would refuse a primary key.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_matched_key', 'path-step-matched-key', 'graphitron',
   'The primary key a step from a routine''s derived table to a table rode, its columns matched by name among the derived table''s: one row per such step.',
   'For example Actor.films over @routine(name: "films_for_actor") returning [Film] steps from the function table films_for_actor to film on film_pkey, film_id matched by name.',
   'A function''s derived table has no foreign key, so a step leaving it reaches its table by that table''s primary key, each primary-key column matched by name among the derived table''s columns: an inferred routine result, a written {table:} element from a function, and the step after a path ending on a @routine alike. A sibling of graphitron_join_path_step_primary_key, which is the {key:} self-join only; the pairs are graphitron_join_path_step_column''s.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_method', 'path-step-method', 'graphitron',
   'The condition method a step of a join path joins or filters by: one row per step, the one overload whose two table parameters take the step''s from and to table.',
   'For example Customer.homeAddress, inherited from Person.homeAddress, calls homeAddress(Customer, Address) where Staff.homeAddress calls homeAddress(Staff, Address).',
   'Each path calls the overload whose two table parameters take its step''s two tables, so one written condition resolved from several starting tables is a row per path, and a step no overload takes, or two take with neither more specific, keeps no row and draws a defect instead.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_routine', 'path-step-routine', 'graphitron',
   'The routine a step of a join path calls: one row per step a @routine element took.',
   'For example Actor.films over @routine(name: "films_for_actor") is one step from actor to films_for_actor, calling public.films_for_actor.',
   'A @routine is a step like any other, from the table the path stands at to its function''s result; what the step calls is this row, and how it is called its parameter rows.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_parameter', 'path-step-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'Where a routine step''s parameter takes its value: one row per step and parameter, a column of the table the step departs or an argument of the field.',
   'For example Actor.films over @routine(name: "films_for_actor", argMapping: "pActorId: $source.actor_id, pMinLength: minLength") binds pActorId from actor.actor_id and pMinLength from the argument minLength.',
   'The routine step''s join, as a key step''s is its column pairs: a parameter bound from a column correlates the call with the row the path stands at, one bound from an argument does not.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_stop', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'One need whose join path stopped short: one row per need whose path met a step it could not take, with where and how many moves it found there.',
   'For example {key: "film_actor_actor_id_fkey"} written on a Film field stops the path from film at position 0, no move leaving film.',
   'A stop is a step not taken, which graphitron_join_path_step cannot hold, a row there being a step; a stopped need has its steps and its stop and no path row, a met need its path and its steps. It is the break the editor and the messages name, at the written element the author edits where there is one.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_claimed_rule', 'path-need', 'graphitron',
   'The claim a need follows: one row per need of an occurrence that a claim covers and walks to, with the claim and whether it is the element''s own or inherited.',
   'For example Staff.homeAddress, writing nothing, follows (Person.homeAddress, Address) as INHERITED, Store.people''s need for Staff follows its own @referenceFor, (Store.people, Staff), and an argument writing @reference follows its own claim for every type its field reaches.',
   'The rule for graphitron_join_path_claimed, kept in the catalog where the gates reading stored definitions see what it reads. The precedence is the manual''s: the field''s own @referenceFor naming the type, then its own claim, then exactly one inherited, an extending interface overriding the one it extends and @referenceFor inherited member by member. An argument or input field follows its own @referenceFor naming the type, else its own claim matched by the element alone, and inherits nothing. The step stage walks each row and writes it beside the path once the walk reaches the target.'),
  ('graphitron_join_path_step_rule', 'path-step-move', 'graphitron',
   'A move one written element of a claim allows from a table: one row per claim, position, departure and move, and one row per element with no destination.',
   'For example {key: "customer_address_id_fkey"} on Customer.address allows customer to address, and {table: "language"} on a Film field allows film to language over each of film''s two keys.',
   'The walk as a flat rule over base tables: what one element allows from a table, independent of any need, so the step stage reads it once per position with the graph, the position and the instant bound and counts the moves each need finds. The row with no destination is what lets a need finding no move still name the element it stopped at.'),
  ('build_warning_stale_module_hint', 'stale-module-hint', 'derivation',
   'One graph whose run read a workspace module stale, and the clause its errors carry for it.',
   'For example a graph whose run read opptak-service stale, whose errors then end saying that one module was read stale and may be why.',
   'Every error a stale module may have caused says so, because a name added to a module since it compiled reads as an author error at every site that names it, and one cause behind many errors is what a developer reading the errors one at a time cannot see. Per graph and not per error: which module a given error needed is not something the errors record, so the clause counts the stale modules, says may, and points at the warnings that name them one row each. Its own relation rather than written into each arm, so the diagnostic union spells the clause once.'),
  ('build_warning_stale_module', 'stale-module', 'derivation',
   'One workspace module a graph''s run read stale, with why and the two times that say so.',
   'For example a service module whose sources were saved after its target/classes was written, or a module with no output read from the copy last installed.',
   'The schema is checked against the classes a run reads, so a module read stale makes every name added to it since read as an author error, and one warning naming the module is what turns many errors into one cause. A rule over captured facts and evaluated when read rather than stored, because its source half is written by the dev loop''s source walk, after a capture''s derivations have run: the configuration says which module each entry is, the classpath reading the output''s class times, and the source walk the sources'' newest. Advisory, so the comparison may be approximate: times rather than content, Java sources only, and a module''s build directory matched by path prefix.'),
  ('java_source_root', 'source-root', 'java-source',
   'One walked source root, and when the newest .java file under it was last modified.',
   'For example a service module''s src/main/java, whose newest file was saved after the module last compiled.',
   'The source half of whether a workspace module was compiled since it was edited, beside the class files'' times store_class_file holds for the output half. Per root and not per file, the question being only whether anything under it changed, and per root rather than per module because a root is what the walk covers. A time and not a stamp, because what is asked is when, and content cannot say whether a compile came after an edit; the family''s refusal to trust times stands for what it governs, deciding whether a file is read again.'),
  ('store_graph_module', 'graph-module', 'store',
   'One module of the workspace whose output or a copy of it is on the classpath the build handed this graph''s run, with where it lives and where its build writes.',
   'For example the consumer''s service module, at its own directory, writing its classes to target/classes and its jar to target.',
   'Configuration the plugin resolves rather than a reading: the workspace is every module under the outermost aggregator, and which of them the run reads is what its classpath holds. Keyed by coordinate, which is the module''s identity across the forms Maven hands a run, its output directory, its packaged jar or its installed jar, where store_graph_dependency is keyed by the path of whichever form it was. Only the modules the run reads: a module the classpath does not hold is no question this graph''s run can be asked. What a reader wants of a module is whether the run read it current, which needs where it lives and where its build writes.'),
  ('store_graph_module_entry', 'graph-module-entry', 'store',
   'One classpath entry of this graph''s run, and the workspace module it is a form of.',
   'For example the installed service-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar Maven resolved for the service module, or that module''s target/classes read in its place.',
   'The link between the two identities a module has in a run, the path the classpath holds and the coordinate the module is, held apart so that neither relation carries a column empty on most of its rows: most entries are no module''s, and a module is told by its coordinate whatever form it arrived in.'),
  ('federation_version_definition', 'federation-version-definition', 'federation',
   'One definition a federation version brings into a schema that links it.',
   'For example @key under v2.10, a repeatable directive a link importing it names key and one that does not names federation__key, and FieldSet, a scalar.',
   'What a link brings into the schema is the library''s to say, the imported and the unimported alike, and the editor has to know it to judge a directive the generator accepts. Captured with the name the library gives a definition no import names, so graphitron never restates the specification''s namespacing.'),
  ('federation_version_directive_argument', 'federation-version-directive-argument', 'federation',
   'One argument a federation version''s directive declares.',
   'For example fields: FieldSet! of @key under v2.10, its type named by the specification.',
   'On graphql_directive_argument''s terms, so the vocabulary graphitron derives reads the two the same way; its named type is the specification''s, the graph''s name for it being graphitron''s rule.'),
  ('federation_written_version', 'federation-written-version', 'federation',
   'The federation version a link graphitron adds names, under the library it is read against.',
   'For example v2.10, which a graph federated only through a configured tag links to.',
   'Graphitron''s own choice rather than the author''s or the library''s, recorded beside the versions so the configured link reads a row rather than a literal, and so the database refuses a version the library does not support. Not the newest version the library offers, which would change whenever the library did.'),
  ('federation_version', 'federation-version', 'federation',
   'One federation specification version the federation library supports.',
   'For example v2.10, a link whose url ends in v2.10 being one the library on graphitron''s classpath accepts.',
   'Which versions a link may name is federation-jvm''s to say, and it says so only by accepting or refusing one. Read once per library jar rather than per link, so a graph''s link is checked against a captured fact and a version no graph names yet is there for the editor to offer.'),
  ('federation_version_import', 'federation-version-import', 'federation',
   'One name a federation version''s link may import.',
   'For example @key and FieldSet under v2.10, and @context only from the version that introduced it.',
   'What graphitron may add to a link''s imports, and what an author may write there, is the library''s to decide, a directive being importable from its own minimum version whatever file its definition sits in. Captured as the library answers, by trying each name as an import, so nothing graphitron maintains lists federation''s vocabulary.'),
  ('store_graph_progress', 'gatherer-progress', 'capture',
   'How far a capture of one graph got: one row per gatherer that reads it, and one for the capture as a whole.',
   'For example a capture that hung in the graphitron gatherer leaves its row started after it last completed, every gatherer before it completed with this reading''s instants, and the capture row incomplete.',
   'A capture commits gatherer by gatherer, so the store says how far a reading got; this is where it says it, read by a person or a tool looking at a store rather than by the generator. Written by each gatherer around its own run, through the sink package''s Progress, and by ModelCapture for its own row. Reporting only, never a transaction boundary: the gatherer commits its work in whatever transactions suit it. The start is written before the gatherer touches anything, so one that never returns has already named itself, and the completion after the gatherer has returned, so a finished row means its work is committed; one that throws leaves its row unfinished. Wall-clock instants rather than the reading''s, which every gatherer shares; the reading''s instant stays on store_graph.last_captured and on every row it marks. No sweep: a row is the gatherer''s last reading and the next one overwrites it, and a graph or gatherer that goes takes its rows by cascade.'),
  ('store_graph_write', 'gatherer-table-write', 'capture',
   'How long one finished capture of a graph spent writing one table: one row per table each gatherer wrote, every capture keeping its own.',
   'For example graphitron_occurrence_column written by the graphitron gatherer in two statements, its rule evaluated inside the insert.',
   'Every capture-time regression has been one stage''s write, its rule evaluated inside the insert, and the per-gatherer times in store_graph_progress say which gatherer but not which table. Timed by the sink package''s WriteTimes, a listener on the writing connection that adds each insert, merge, update and delete to the table it names and the gatherer Progress last started, so no stage times itself. Written when the capture finishes, so a capture that stops partway writes nothing, its row in store_graph_progress saying where. No sweep: each capture keeps its rows beside the ones before it, so a slow round is read against the rounds before it, and a graph that goes takes its rows by cascade. Read by MCP status and the dev loop, which name the latest capture''s slowest.'),
  ('store_graph_write_stage', 'stage-table-write', 'capture',
   'One table one stage of a gatherer''s anchor phase wrote in one finished capture of a graph, and how long that stage spent on it.',
   'For example graphitron_path_need written by the occurrence-depths stage of the graphitron gatherer, once per depth.',
   'store_graph_write says which table a capture''s time went to and which gatherer wrote it, and a gatherer''s anchor phase is a sequence of stages, so this says which stage. Written by WriteTimes beside the row it details, from the stage AnchorStage names while it runs, and only for writes inside a stage. Two readers: the gate holding each stage to the relations meta_stage_write declares for it, which reads every fact document''s captures, and whoever prices a stage, the per-stage time being what a split of one stage''s work is measured by. No sweep: it goes with its store_graph_write row.'),
  ('store_graph_read', 'gatherer-relation-read', 'capture',
   'How long one finished capture of a graph spent reading one relation: one row per relation each gatherer spent a millisecond or more preparing and reading, every capture keeping its own.',
   'For example a rule view read by a stage that fetches it into Java and inserts the rows, the view evaluated in the read.',
   'A stage that fetches its rule into Java and inserts the rows evaluates the rule in the read, which store_graph_write never sees: one such rule went from 7.7 s to 37.9 s on sis with nothing recording it. Timed by the same WriteTimes listener as the writes, through the fetch, under the gatherer Progress last started, named by the first relation the query reads from. A relation read for under a millisecond in a capture is not recorded, so the small lookups every capture makes do not multiply its rows. Written when the capture finishes, beside its writes and under the same instant; no sweep, every capture keeping its rows as store_graph_write does, and a graph that goes takes them by cascade. Read with the writes by MCP status and the dev loop.'),
  ('store_graph_dependency', 'graph-dependency', 'store',
   'One entry of the compile classpath the build handed this graph''s run, with what the build resolved it as.',
   'For example the consumer''s own target/classes is a DIRECTORY of origin PROJECT, and jooq-3.20.11.jar a RELEASE the pom declares.',
   'Configuration rather than a reading: what a module depends on is something its build declares, so the row is transcribed as the producer resolved it and nothing here is read off the entry. Per graph, because how an entry reached the classpath is a fact about one module''s build and not about the bytes: one jar is declared by one module and dragged in by another. Stored rather than held in the Java that wrote it because a graph''s claim on an entry can only reference a dependency that is a row, and that reference is what lets a dependency the build stops declaring release its claim by cascade.'),
  ('store_graph_outbox', 'graph-outbox', 'store',
   'The <outbox> block of one graph''s build: where the messages of its @outbox fields go, as authored.',
   'For example a build configuring <outbox><table>outbox</table></outbox> is one row naming the table, and a build with no block has no row.',
   'Configuration rather than a reading, transcribed as written and resolved nowhere: the table and function are names the derivation resolves against the catalog, and a block naming both targets or neither is recorded and reported at each @outbox it affects, because a defect has to sit at an SDL position and the configuration has none. Row presence is the fact, as for the maximum page size: no block, no row.'),
  ('store_graph_outbox_role', 'graph-outbox-role', 'store',
   'One role the <outbox> block binds to a name of its target: a column of the outbox table or a parameter of the outbox function.',
   'For example <columns><type>event_type</type></columns> is one row binding the role type to the column event_type.',
   'Kept apart from the block because the roles are a set of bindings, not a column each: the table target binds columns and the function target binds parameters, under one key. The kind says which element wrote it. Defaults the target applies where a role is not bound are a resolution and live in the derivation.'),
  ('store_graph_outbox_payload_entry', 'graph-outbox-payload-position', 'store',
   'One entry of the <outbox> <payload> mapping every message carries, at its place in it.',
   'For example <payload>actor: $session.userId, operation: $scope.operationName</payload> gives two rows, actor bound to $session.userId at position 0 and operation to $scope.operationName at 1.',
   'The decode of the common payload, written by capture the way the generic argMapping writer decodes a mapping the SDL carries: the entries are the same sentence about a different corpus, and the configuration has no SDL position for the generic writer to key them by. A string that does not parse writes no rows and is kept verbatim in the block row.'),
  ('store_graph_max_allowed_page_size', 'graph-max-page-size', 'store',
   'The largest page any connection of one graph returns, as its build declared it in <maxAllowedPageSize>.',
   'For example a build setting the parameter to 1000 is one row reading 1000, and a build that sets none has no row.',
   'Configuration rather than a reading, and generation policy the store reads: the minted first argument''s default is lowered to it and an authored default above it is a defect, both of which are views, so the maximum is a row they can join rather than a value only the generator holds. Its own graph-keyed relation, on store_graph_tenant_column''s terms: single-valued, optional and run-owned, so row presence is the fact and a build with no maximum has no row.'),
  ('store_graph_session_supplied', 'graph-session-supplied', 'store',
   'One graph whose session identity is supplied per request, as its build declared it with a <sessionState> block.',
   'For example a build with <sessionState/> in its POM is one row, and a build with no block has none.',
   'Configuration rather than a reading, and the fact every question about session identity starts from: the generators emit the session types, the factory parameter and the connection hook only for a graph with a row, and the derived rejections of a $session binding anti-join against it. Row presence is the fact, per the family''s absence rule: no row means no identity is mounted. Its own graph-keyed relation, on store_graph_tenant_column''s terms: single-valued, optional and run-owned.'),
  ('store_graph_session_handle', 'graph-session-handle', 'store',
   'The class a graph''s session mount returns, as its build named it in <sessionState><handle>.',
   'For example a build naming com.example.Identity is one row reading that name, and a build whose mount returns nothing has none.',
   'Configuration rather than a reading: only the authored name lands here, and what the class offers is derived from the classpath corpus, so the members a $session binding may name are rows that cite the class files and not a value only the generator holds. A child of store_graph_session_supplied rather than a nullable column on it, after the unmount relation it replaces: row presence is the fact, and a handle without the switch is unwritable by the foreign key.'),
  ('graphitron_session_handle_member', 'session-handle-member', 'graphitron',
   'One accessor of the class a graph names as its session handle, under the property name an author writes after $session.',
   'For example a handle record with components brukerId and sokerId draws two rows, and a class offering getTitle and isTitle draws two rows under one name.',
   'Derived from the configured handle row and the class the classpath corpus read, so what $session.<member> may name is a row an author''s binding can be checked against rather than a reflection result only the generator holds. The members are the slots the class declares itself, not the ones it inherits: a jOOQ record handle offers the two accessors its generated class declares, not the Record API beneath them. Kept as one row per accessor, so two accessors for one property are visible as such and the binding that names it is the defect, instead of the class being refused for a member nobody reads.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_parameter', 'argmapping-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'The Java parameter one written argMapping entry binds: the entry, and the parameter of the method its code reference resolved to that carries the name written on its left.',
   'For example the entry brukerId: $session.brukerId on a @service resolves to the parameter named brukerId of the one method the service names, and an entry whose method is overloaded, missing or compiled without -parameters has no row.',
   'The join a check of an argMapping''s left side needs and every such check used to rebuild: the entry''s string sits under a code reference, the reference resolves to one method, and the method''s parameters carry the names an entry targets. An entry that does not resolve has no row, which is what a defect arm anti-joins on, and a parameter name the class file does not carry resolves nowhere rather than guessing, on the parameter column''s own terms.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_pair_defect_rule', 'argmapping-pair-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the argMapping-entry defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_pair_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_pair_defect, and graphitron_entry_defect_rule rolls them up to the string.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it. It sits at the entry because that is where each offence is: a string carries several entries, and judging them at the string would force the offences into one value, which the fact schema refuses for a set. The string-grain verdict is read off these rows, so there is one statement of the rule and two grains of its answer.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_pair_defect', 'argmapping-pair-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One written argMapping entry a rule refuses, under the code that names the rule: the entry-grain detail of the defect graphitron_entry_defect states once for the whole string.',
   'For example brukerId: $session.nope on a handle with no such member is one row reading SESSION_MEMBER_UNKNOWN and the member written, under the string''s one row.',
   'The child of graphitron_entry_defect at the grain a mapping string is made of. The defect relation is keyed by the written position of a directive, which for an argMapping is the string; a string holds several entries and a message names one, so the specifics of a defect are rows under the string''s row and the string''s row is the verdict. Keeping them apart is what lets every offending entry be reported at once without collapsing a set into one value, and the foreign key to the string''s row is what makes a sweep of the verdict collect its details. Named for the grain and not for a rule: the session rules are the first to judge an entry, not the only ones that could.'),
  ('graphql_schema_problem', 'graph-schema-problem', 'graphql-assembly',
   'Reading this graph''s documents and making a schema of them produced this problem: one of the refusals the toolchain this graph is built with raised, whichever of the four stages raised it.',
   'For example a file that will not parse is one row naming that file, a second file declaring a type an earlier file already declares is another, and a second federation @link is a third.',
   'The toolchain decides this and the store records it rather than deciding again: graphql-java, and at REWRITE the loading rewrites the generator composes the corpus with, so a directive the build accepts is never reported undeclared here. Making a schema of the documents checks whether they work together, around thirty checks deep, from references resolving and interface contracts to directive locations and the schema''s own shape. Re-deriving it from the entries would be a second implementation of a validation the toolchain already runs, and the two would disagree once the library moved. One relation for every stage: parsing, combining, composing and building a schema are not four questions a reader has, and the stage is a column so one who does care can ask. A file that will not parse contributes no entries, which is why the site columns are here: this is the only relation that can say the file was read. They are nullable because the later stages point at a declaration where they can and nowhere where they cannot. Absence is success: a graph whose documents made a schema has no rows here, so asking whether the corpus is sound is a count. Keyed at the graph, the stage and the order raised within it, not at a site, a problem need not report one. The stage is in the key so each stage numbers its own and each stage''s writer stands alone; an ordinal dense across every stage let only one writer hold them, which constrains the gatherers rather than describing the corpus.'),
  ('graphql_ast_type_declaration_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A type declaration as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares or extends a named type of this kind and this name.',
   'For example type Film { title: String } is one row saying OBJECT, and extend type Film { rating: Rating } is another saying OBJECT and an extension.',
   'One relation for all six declaration forms and their extensions, the form being a column, because a child names its parent by position and a reader following that reference would otherwise union six relations to find what is there. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_field_definition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A FieldDefinition as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares a field of this name on the declaration it was written inside.',
   'For example the title: String inside type Film { title: String } is one row, naming the position that type declaration was written at.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_field_argument_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An argument of a field, as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares an argument of this name on the field it was written inside.',
   'For example the lang: Lang = NB inside title(lang: Lang = NB): String.',
   'A relation per site, because the parent is the thing that differs. The parser gives an input value one shape wherever it is written, but a field''s argument, an input object''s field and a directive definition''s argument sit inside three different kinds of node, and a key names one table: at one relation for the node kind the parent position is a number a reader has to resolve. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. The parent position is a key into graphql_ast_field_definition_entry: both rows come out of one parse of one file and one method writes both, so nothing a document can say breaks it. What the names on this row refer to is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_input_field_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A field of an input object, as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares an input field of this name on the declaration it was written inside.',
   'For example the q: String inside input Filter { q: String }.',
   'A relation per site, because the parent is the thing that differs. The parser gives an input value one shape wherever it is written, but a field''s argument, an input object''s field and a directive definition''s argument sit inside three different kinds of node, and a key names one table: at one relation for the node kind the parent position is a number a reader has to resolve. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. The parent position is a key into graphql_ast_type_declaration_entry: both rows come out of one parse of one file and one method writes both, so nothing a document can say breaks it. What the names on this row refer to is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_directive_argument_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An argument a directive definition declares, as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares an argument of this name on the definition it was written inside.',
   'For example the fields: String! inside directive @key(fields: String!) on OBJECT.',
   'A relation per site, because the parent is the thing that differs. The parser gives an input value one shape wherever it is written, but a field''s argument, an input object''s field and a directive definition''s argument sit inside three different kinds of node, and a key names one table: at one relation for the node kind the parent position is a number a reader has to resolve. Named for the definition that declares it, where graphql_ast_applied_argument_entry is named for the application that passes one. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. The parent position is a key into graphql_ast_directive_definition_entry: both rows come out of one parse of one file and one method writes both, so nothing a document can say breaks it. What the names on this row refer to is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_enum_value_definition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An EnumValueDefinition as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares an enum value of this name on the declaration it was written inside.',
   'For example the G inside enum Rating { G PG } is one row, at ordinal 0 of that declaration''s values.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_implements_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An interface named in an implements clause as one document wrote it: this position in this file says the declaration it was written inside implements an interface of this name.',
   'For example type Film implements Node & Aged draws two rows, Node at ordinal 0 and Aged at ordinal 1.',
   'The name written here need not be declared anywhere in the corpus. Holding a reference to something absent is the point: that is an author error some detection reports, and a relation that refused the row could not report it. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_union_member_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A member named in a union as one document wrote it: this position in this file says the union it was written inside has a member of this name.',
   'For example union Media = Film | Show draws two rows, Film at ordinal 0 and Show at ordinal 1.',
   'The name written here need not be declared anywhere in the corpus. Holding a reference to something absent is the point: that is an author error some detection reports, and a relation that refused the row could not report it. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_directive_definition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A DirectiveDefinition as one document wrote it: this position in this file defines a directive of this name.',
   'For example directive @key(fields: String!) repeatable on OBJECT is one row, its argument and its two locations being rows of their own that name this position.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_directive_location_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A DirectiveLocation as one document wrote it: this position in this file names a place the directive defined above it may be applied.',
   'For example the OBJECT and the INTERFACE of directive @key on OBJECT | INTERFACE are two rows.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_schema_definition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A SchemaDefinition as one document wrote it: this position in this file declares or extends the schema''s own node.',
   'For example schema { query: Query } is one row, and extend schema { subscription: Sub } is another marked an extension.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_operation_type_definition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An OperationTypeDefinition as one document wrote it: this position in this file binds one root operation to one type name.',
   'For example the query: Query inside schema { query: Query } is one row.',
   'The name written here need not be declared anywhere in the corpus. Holding a reference to something absent is the point: that is an author error some detection reports, and a relation that refused the row could not report it. Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_applied_argument_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'An argument of a directive application as one document wrote it: this position in this file passes a value of this name to the application it was written inside.',
   'For example the fields: "id" inside @key(fields: "id") is one row, carrying the value as it was written.',
   'Keyed by the position the node was written at, so two documents writing one name are two rows and nothing here chooses between them. Neither the parent reference nor the names this row carries are keys: following them means resolving, which is the anchors'' work, not a transcription''s.'),
  ('graphql_ast_value_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphql-ast',
   'A value as one document wrote it: this position in this file wrote a value of this kind, inside the value that encloses it where one does, for the node that holds the whole expression.',
   'For example @reference(path: [{table: "film_actor"}]) writes three rows, the list, the object at index 0, and the string at the object field table, under the applied argument that holds them.',
   'One relation for a recursive thing, because a value is one kind of node however deep it sits: a string written at the top of an argument and a string written inside an object two levels down state the same fact, that this slot was written with this literal, and splitting them by depth or by kind would be splitting on an attribute. The nine kinds are a column for that reason, with a check naming them, and the leaf text is one column rather than one per type because coercing a written literal is the work of whoever anchors it against the declared type. The parent reference is a key back into this relation and the holder reference is not: an enclosing value is always a value, while a holder is an applied argument or one of three kinds of declaration carrying a default, and picking which relation holds it is a resolution. Keyed by the position the node was written at, like everything else in this family, so two documents writing one expression are two trees. The holder is repeated on every node rather than held at the root because the alternative is a recursive read on a family whose whole argument is that readers should not be doing recursive work; the same choice graphitron_argmapping_candidate already made. This is the decomposition graphql_ast_applied_argument_entry.value_sdl and the three default_value_sdl columns hold as one string, and those columns are owed a removal once nothing reads them.'),
  ('graphql_ast_entry', 'sdl-entry-position', 'graphql-ast',
   'A written position exists in this document: the supertype of the nineteen entry relations, keyed by the position the parse read it at, and carrying which of them holds the rest of it.',
   'For example the @field on name: String @field(name: "title") is one row of kind FIELD_DIRECTIVE, written inside the field declaration whose position it names as its parent.',
   'Nineteen entry relations share a primary key and nothing else, so a reader holding a position had to know which of them to look in before it could ask anything, and a relation naming a position had nothing to reference. Written by the arms themselves as each writes its own rows, which is what lets them key into it and what keeps the two from disagreeing: one reading of one node produces the supertype row and the subtype row together. It carries the tree as well as the position, because three of the nineteen have a parent whose relation is not fixed and could otherwise reference nothing at all. What it does not carry is any name resolved against a later cadence: this is written while one document is being read, and the anchors that settle what the corpus honours do not exist yet.'),
  ('graphql_ast_touched_coordinate', 'touched-coordinate', 'graphql-ast',
   'A coordinate the latest reading of a graph''s documents touched: one a document the reading rewrote named before the rewrite or after it.',
   'For example renaming Film.title to Film.name in film.graphqls touches Film.title, Film.name and every other coordinate film.graphqls names, and nothing another file alone names.',
   'Where re-deriving what an edit reaches starts. Captured at the write rather than derived after it, because the rewrite deletes the half that says what the document named before, and a coordinate the edit removed is exactly one the anchors have to revisit. Every coordinate a changed document names counts, its unchanged ones too: the anchors stop where a row they re-derive comes out the same, so being coarse here costs a check and never an answer. A reading that rewrote no document touches nothing, which is what lets a round with no change reach nothing.'),
  ('graphql_ast_element_entry', 'sdl-entry-position', 'graphql-ast',
   'This written position declares a schema element, this is the coordinate it declares, and this is the declaration it was written inside: the eight entry kinds that name one, under the nineteen that need not.',
   'For example type Widget declares Widget, the field under it declares Widget.name, and a directive definition declares @widget and @widget(arg:), while the directive applied to that field declares nothing and has no row here. A schema block declares $schema, which is ours.',
   'The middle of the entry hierarchy, and why the coordinate is stated once rather than seven times: it was a generated column on each kind that declares one, so every reader wanting a coordinate without knowing which kind declared it unioned all seven, which capture did twice and the lint rules did again. Seven and not five because the specification''s coordinate grammar spells a directive and an argument of one, and graphitron keys deprecations on exactly those; the narrower five are what graphql_element held while a walk wrote it, the walk having no arm for a directive coordinate. Keyed by the position and not by the coordinate, two documents declaring one type being two rows here and one row in graphql_element, which is the whole difference between what a document wrote and what the corpus settled on. No key into graphql_element, for the reason nothing in this family has one: that relation is written long after this, and a coordinate here is a string the document''s own names compose rather than a reference to anything.'),
  ('graphql_ast_directive_application_entry', 'sdl-entry-position', 'graphql-ast',
   'This written position applies a directive, and this is the name it applies: the five entry kinds that are an application, under the nineteen that need not be.',
   'For example the @key on type Widget @key(fields: "id") is one row here, and the declaration it was written on is one row of graphql_ast_element_entry beside it.',
   'The other middle of the entry hierarchy, and the counterpart of the element one: the pair divide the same nineteen kinds along two different questions, so a directive written on a field has a row here and none there while the field itself has the reverse. The same argument as the element one makes about coordinates: the name a directive applies was a column on each of the five sites, identical in all five, so a reader asking where a directive was written unioned five relations that differ only in what the application sits inside. Capture did that union, the lint rules did it again, and the five relations remain because each is the parent of a different decode and carries the site as a foreign key a collapsed relation could not state. What they no longer carry alone is the name, which is the one thing they share and is now said once here. The site stays a join rather than becoming a column: an application is written directly inside what it applies to, so the parent hop graphql_ast_entry already carries answers it, and adding a column saying which of five things a row is about would state twice what the tree states once.'),
  ('graphql_directive_element', 'directive-name', 'graphql-ast',
   'A directive coordinate exists in this graph: the decomposition of the coordinate graphql_element holds for a directive definition, which is the at sign and this name.',
   'For example directive @paged on FIELD_DEFINITION is the row (paged, @paged).',
   'The specification''s coordinate grammar has five productions and two of them open with an at sign, so a directive and a formal argument of one are schema elements exactly as a type and a field are. graphql_element has held all five productions since it stopped being written by a walk, which had no arm for either; what was missing was the pair of relations decomposing those two coordinates into the parts a reader joins by, so a reader holding a directive coordinate had to take the string apart. The split from graphql_directive is graphql_type_element''s split from graphql_type and has its reason: what a definition says about itself is one fact, and that its coordinate exists is another.'),
  ('graphql_directive_argument_element', 'directive-argument-name', 'graphql-ast',
   'A directive argument coordinate exists on a directive: the decomposition of the coordinate graphql_element holds for a formal argument of a directive definition.',
   'For example the pagedName of directive @paged(pagedName: String) is the row (paged, pagedName, @paged(pagedName:)).',
   'The other of the two, and the one with a reader already written: a deprecation keys on a coordinate like @paged(pagedName:), and before this the only way back to the directive and the argument was to find the parentheses and the colon and cut. The unique on the coordinate makes that join total in both directions, the two keys being two spellings of one thing.'),
  ('lint_violation', 'lint-violation', 'derivation',
   'One rule broken at one written position: this rule has something to say about what this file wrote here.',
   'For example an input object named WidgetFilter draws a row of input-object-name-suffix at the position its declaration was written at.',
   'Keyed on the position rather than on the coordinate, because a lint finding is something an author goes to and fixes and a coordinate assembled from several declaration sites names no single place to go. That decides more than the key: a name spelled wrongly at a base declaration and at two extensions is three rows, one per place the author has to edit, where a coordinate-keyed relation would have said it once and pointed at whichever site capture met first. A row asserts a defect, so a rule''s population is the positions where the author could have written it otherwise and not every position of the right kind: a type extension carries no description slot at all, so an undescribed one is not an undocumented type but a place where documenting is not a thing that can be done, and a row there would be a false fact rather than a noisy one. A view and not a table, which is what a derivation gets when nothing forces storage: no corpus is read, no key is established here that its own references do not cover, and no read cost has been measured. What that buys beyond the rule being stated once is that the integrity a stored version would need foreign keys to defend is structural instead, every row being constructed by joining the relations it cites.'),
  ('graphitron_deprecated', 'schema-element', 'graphitron',
   'This schema element is deprecated, and this is the replacement hint the author gave: whatever the corpus retired, under the coordinate that names it.',
   'For example a retired directive draws the row @asConnection and a retired argument of one draws @asConnection(connectionName:).',
   'One relation where there were three, which is what keying on a coordinate buys. They were a relation per shape of key, a directive under its name, an argument of one under two names and an input field under a type and a field, each carrying the same reason under a differently spelled key, and a reader asking whether something was deprecated had to know which of the three to ask before it could ask. The coordinate is the specification''s own answer to what names an element, and the two directive forms were anchored so that this relation could use it rather than spelling its keys out again.'),
  ('graphql_element', 'schema-element', 'graphql-ast',
   'A schema element exists in this graph: the supertype of the four element relations beside it, keyed by the schema coordinate the GraphQL specification spells for it.',
   'For example the input argument of Mutation.rentFilm is the row Mutation.rentFilm(input:), the field it sits on is Mutation.rentFilm, and the type declaring that field is Mutation.',
   'The element family states an element''s existence at four grains and states nowhere that an element exists, so a relation naming any coordinate has nothing to reference and renders one into a string instead, where no foreign key reaches it. This is the supertype those four have always implied, written by capture beside the anchors it generalises rather than stated as a union over them, which is what makes a reference to any coordinate one column and one key. The four carry the spelling and a foreign key back here, which is the join down to the parts and what makes an anchor with no coordinate impossible; one call writes both rows from one string, so there is no second rendering for a constraint to have to check. Keyed by the spelling and not by a decomposition, because the decompositions are exactly what differ between the four and the specification has already settled the grammar. The element kind names the row in the specification''s own vocabulary, so a reader wanting the parts joins the relation for it instead of splitting the spelling, FIELD and INPUT_FIELD sharing one because they share a coordinate form and are told apart by the parent''s kind.'),
  ('graphql_directive_application', 'directive-application', 'graphql-ast',
   'One directive an author applied, at the coordinate they applied it to: one row per application, however many sites the corpus writes them at.',
   'For example type Film @key(fields: "id") is one row at coordinate Film, and the @external on Film.title is another at Film.title.',
   'One relation where there were five, and the five were not five facts. Each stated that a directive was applied somewhere and keyed it by its own site decomposed, three columns at the schema block and seven at a field argument; none of them carried the coordinate, which is the one thing every site has. A reader therefore chose a relation by where the author happened to write, and a reader wanting all of them unioned five. graphql_element is a table rather than a union, so this keys into it and the choosing stops. The schema block was the site that made the collapse look impossible, having no coordinate in the specification''s grammar; it has ours, $schema, which a GraphQL name cannot spell. What orders a repeat is the merge order of the declaration the application sits inside, which the element entry it sits on states, and the schema block sorts by its file''s age instead, the same ORDER BY rather than a second arm because its merge order is null and its coordinate shares a partition with nothing.'),
  ('graphql_directive_application_arg', 'directive-application-argument', 'graphql-ast',
   'One argument an author passed to an application: the application''s own key and the formal argument the value binds.',
   'For example the fields: "id" of type Film @key(fields: "id") is one row.',
   'One relation for the reason the applications above are one, and by an easier argument: the five this replaces were identical but for the site key they copied down from their parent, so there was never a fact here that differed by site. The value is the rendered SDL literal rather than a parse of it, which is what makes an application legible without knowing what the directive means and what lets a consumer that does know read the same string the author typed. Nothing is ranked, an argument being named once inside one application; a document naming one twice is a schema problem and the upsert keeps the later of the two.'),
  ('graphql_type_element', 'graph-type', 'graphql-ast',
   'A type name exists in this graph: the anchor every type-keyed fact hangs off, carrying the key and nothing else.',
   'For example type Film { title: String } in one file and extend type Film { year: Int } in another are one row, Film.',
   'Existence is the whole assertion, and every attribute of the type is a fact some other relation owns at that fact''s own cadence. Split out from graphql_type so the reference web anchors on the earliest cadence an SDL fact can have: a name exists because some declaration site declares or extends it, which the per-file parse settles before anything is composed or judged, so a relation on a later cadence can be rewritten, withheld or emptied without the facts hanging off the coordinate following it. Anchoring on a composed relation instead would make every per-site fact''s existence conditional on the whole corpus assembling.'),
  ('graphql_type', 'graph-type', 'graphql-ast',
   'What a named type is: its kind and its description, hanging off graphql_type_element.',
   'For example type Film { title: String } is the row (Film, OBJECT).',
   'Read from whichever site capture meets first in merge order, macro-contributed sites included, which on a well-formed schema is the base definition; graphql_type_declaration carries every site and the kind each one wrote. Nothing references this relation, deliberately: the split from graphql_type_element exists so that what a later cadence owns cannot drag the reference web with it, and holding the reference web off the attribute relation is that split doing its job.'),
  ('graphql_type_declaration', 'type-declaration-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'A declaration site of a type: the base definition or one extension, at the position it was written.',
   'For example type Film in film.graphqls and extend type Film in rental.graphqls are two rows, the first with is_extension false.',
   'All five extension kinds are live, so a type''s effective shape may be assembled from several files; this relation records which site contributed what, in merge order, and is the unit an incremental refresh asks about: which types a file touches. The built-in scalars are declared in the bundled specification.graphqls, which sorts after every file, so an author''s declaration of one is the base and an authored extension merges after it. Keyed by the site, so two files declaring one name are two rows and each keeps its own position.'),
  ('graphql_field_element', 'graph-field', 'graphql-ast',
   'A field coordinate exists on a type: the anchor for the field-keyed half of the reference web.',
   'For example title: String on type Film is the row (Film, title, Film.title).',
   'On graphql_type_element''s terms and for its reason. Written from the declaration sites, so the coordinate exists as soon as one site declares the field, and a coordinate two sites declare is one row; the losing declaration is the duplicate quarantine''s business, not this relation''s. Object and interface parents make it an output field and input object parents an input field, which the specification counts as two kinds of schema element sharing one coordinate form; this relation holds both, the two differing in nothing it carries, and graphql_element says which.'),
  ('graphql_field', 'graph-field', 'graphql-ast',
   'What a field at a coordinate is: its type expression, its description, its default and the site that contributed it.',
   'For example title: String! on type Film is the row (Film, title) with type_sdl String! and non_null true.',
   'Hangs off graphql_field_element. Object and interface parents make it an output field, input object parents an input field, and the join decides. Nothing references this relation, on graphql_type''s terms: the field-keyed reference web anchors on the coordinate instead, so an attribute rewritten at a later cadence drags nothing with it.'),
  ('graphql_argument_element', 'field-argument', 'graphql-ast',
   'An argument coordinate exists on a field: the anchor for the argument-keyed decode relations.',
   'For example films(rating: String) on Query is the row (Query, films, rating, Query.films(rating:)).',
   'On graphql_type_element''s terms and for its reason: existence is the assertion, and what the argument is lives in graphql_argument at its own cadence.'),
  ('graphql_argument', 'field-argument', 'graphql-ast',
   'What an argument on a field is: its type expression, its default, its description and the site that declared it.',
   'For example films(rating: String = "G") on Query is the row (Query, films, rating) with default_value_sdl "G".',
   'Hangs off graphql_argument_element. Arguments were once classified per field and mostly projected away, with no position kept; this keeps each with the site that declared it. Nothing references this relation, on graphql_type''s terms.'),
  ('graphql_enum_value_element', 'enum-value', 'graphql-ast',
   'An enum value coordinate exists on a type: the anchor for the value-keyed decode relations.',
   'For example the G of enum Rating { G PG } is the row (Rating, G, Rating.G).',
   'On graphql_type_element''s terms and for its reason: existence is the assertion, and what the value is lives in graphql_enum_value.'),
  ('graphql_enum_value', 'enum-value', 'graphql-ast',
   'What a value an enum declares is: its place in the enum, its description and the site that declared it.',
   'For example the PG of enum Rating { G PG } is the row (Rating, PG) at ordinal 1.',
   'Hangs off graphql_enum_value_element. Deprecation is not a column because @deprecated is an ordinary applied directive, read from graphql_directive_application like any other. Nothing references this relation, on graphql_type''s terms.'),
  ('graphql_implements_interface', 'implemented-interface', 'graphql-ast',
   'A type answers to an interface: one row per interface the type declares it implements.',
   'For example type Film implements Node & Named is two rows, (Film, Node) and (Film, Named).',
   'The declaring end is the type, which is why this keys on the type and graphql_union_member keys on the union: a union declares who it admits and a type declares what it answers to, so the two are not one fact wearing two hats. The interface may be one no document declares, which assembly refuses and this relation records rather than refusing itself. graphql_poly_member unions the two for the readers that take either.'),
  ('graphql_union_member', 'union-member', 'graphql-ast',
   'A union names a member: one row per member type the union declares, in the order it wrote them.',
   'For example union Result = Film | Actor is two rows, (Result, Film) at position 0 and (Result, Actor) at 1.',
   'Separate from graphql_implements_interface because a union declares who it admits and a type declares which interfaces it answers to, so the declaring end differs and with it the key, the position base and every foreign key. graphql_poly_member unions the two for the readers that genuinely take either.'),
  ('graphql_root_operation', 'root-operation', 'graphql-ast',
   'The schema binds a root operation to a type: one row per operation it names.',
   'For example schema { query: Query mutation: Mutation } is two rows, (QUERY, Query) and (MUTATION, Mutation).',
   'These rows are the seeds the reachability derivation grows from. The binding is an author-spelled reference, so its dangling case mints a located diagnostic, and the position columns are what it locates from. A double binding cannot reach capture: a schema extension re-binding an operation throws at parse.'),
  ('graphql_directive', 'directive-name', 'graphql-ast',
   'A directive is defined: whether it repeats, its description and the site that declared it.',
   'For example directive @paged repeatable on FIELD_DEFINITION is the row (paged) with repeatable true.',
   'What a definition says about itself is one fact and that its coordinate exists is another, which graphql_directive_element states; the split is graphql_type''s from graphql_type_element. The author''s directives and the bundled vocabulary both land here, a name declared twice settled by the order every declaration is ranked in.'),
  ('graphql_directive_location', 'directive-location', 'graphql-ast',
   'A directive definition names a permitted location.',
   'For example directive @paged on FIELD_DEFINITION | ARGUMENT_DEFINITION is two rows.',
   'One row per location rather than a list column, so whether a directive may sit somewhere is a key lookup, and a location a definition stopped naming leaves as a row.'),
  ('graphql_directive_argument', 'directive-argument-name', 'graphql-ast',
   'A directive definition declares a formal argument: its type expression, its default, its description and the site that declared it.',
   'For example the pagedName of directive @paged(pagedName: String) is the row (paged, pagedName) with type_sdl String.',
   'Carries the same wrapping decode as graphql_field, so the list-ness of a directive argument is a column read rather than a string parse. graphql_directive_argument_element is the decomposition of its coordinate, on graphql_type''s split from graphql_type_element.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_input_value_deprecated_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a deprecation marker on an input value says: the replacement hint the author gave, decoded.',
   'For example a connectionName argument marked with the reason "own your Connection type" gives one row carrying that text.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the input value it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. @deprecated is not graphitron''s directive, and it is decoded here for the reason federation''s @key is: graphitron gives it a meaning GraphQL does not, unifying it with a docstring convention the specification has no room for, and a consumer asking whether something is deprecated should not have to know which of the two marked it. The decode is why this sits beside the applied-argument row rather than being read off it: that row carries the rendered literal, quotes and all, and recovering the text from it would mean re-reading SDL at every read.'),

  ('graphitron_ast_table_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @table application says: the table this declaration is bound to, as written.',
   'For example type Film @table(name: "film") gives one row reading film.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. Nothing positional is duplicated: the type this is written on, the file, and whether the declaration was an extension are all one join away on the key, so a reader that wants them reads the AST row and this relation says only what the arguments meant. Two documents applying @table to one type are two rows and neither is refused, which is the whole difference from a relation keyed by the type name: a collision is a query over these rows, resolved oldest source first, rather than a first-write-wins the writer had to arbitrate. The split of the written name is a cut of the string and nothing else; whether the table exists is a question for the catalog and belongs where the resolving happens.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_scalar_type_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @scalarType application says: the constant holding the scalar this declaration is bound to, as written.',
   'For example scalar Money @scalarType(scalar: "com.example.Scalars.MONEY") gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the type it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. The payload columns are nullable where the directive definition marks the argument required: a required argument the author left out is a schema problem the toolchain reports, and refusing the row here would lose the transcription of what they did write. Whether the named class holds such a field is a question for the classpath census and belongs where the resolving happens.'),
  ('graphitron_code_reference_site', 'sdl-code-reference', 'graphitron',
   'Where one written Java code reference sits: the directive that wrote it, the element it is on, and the method it names once that site''s default applies.',
   'For example isEnglish: Boolean @externalField(reference: {className: "no.example.FilmExtensions"}) sits at Film.isEnglish under externalField and names isEnglish.',
   'What both the resolution and the defects that report it need to know about a reference and the written relation does not say: which directive wrote it and on what. Both are a fixed climb through keys the transcription declares, the holder of a value being the applied argument at any depth and an argument''s parent its application, so it is a view and not a grain. The @externalField default is stated here because both readers apply it; stated twice, the two could disagree about which method a reference names.'),
  ('graphitron_code_reference', 'graph-code-reference', 'graphitron',
   'The one method a written Java code reference names, where the graph''s classpath answers with exactly one.',
   'For example @service(service: {className: "no.example.CityService", method: "cityUppercase"}) draws one row naming the one cityUppercase that class declares, and a misspelt method draws none.',
   'The written reference met with the classpath, once for every site, as a reference into code_method rather than as the two strings again. Only a settled resolution is a row, on graphitron_tabletype''s terms: a reference naming nothing and one naming two overloads both draw none, so the anti-join against the written reference is every one that does not resolve, and graphitron_entry_defect says which of the three ways it failed. Resolved against every public method rather than a directive''s admission arm, because a method a site may not name is a different fault from one that does not exist. A reference naming a class only is not in this population; its resolution is to a class. Written by the graphitron anchor, which runs after the classpath is read.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_pair', 'graph-argmapping-pair', 'graphitron',
   'One pair of a written argMapping, named by its mapping and the parameter it binds: one row per mapping and parameter.',
   'For example input.ids on @service at Query.filmsByPath is the pair (Query.filmsByPath, service, 0, service, ids).',
   'The pair a reader means when it asks what a call''s parameter is bound to, by the call''s names rather than by where the string sits, so the landing, the steps and the fills key by what was written and an edit above it in the file changes no row. Stored as the target the landing and the steps reference, cascading from the mapping and from the entry, whose position rides along for a defect.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_pair_rule', 'graph-argmapping-pair', 'graphitron',
   'One row the argMapping pair rule computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_pair stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_pair, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_site', 'graph-argmapping-site', 'graphitron',
   'Where one written argMapping sits: the directive and its repeat, the element it is written on, the code reference it is a field of and the path element that reference sits in.',
   'For example @reference(path: [{table: "film", condition: {className: "no.example.Conditions", method: "byActor", argMapping: "actorId: id"}}]) on Actor.films sits under reference at ordinal 0, on Actor.films, in the condition''s reference, at step 0.',
   'What a reader of the pair relation needs to know about a mapping and the string does not say. The decode the pair relation replaces carried it as one site literal welding the directive, the element kind and the path step together, and a reader filtered on nine spellings of it; these are the parts. Each is a fixed hop through keys the transcription declares, the holder of a value being its applied argument at any depth. Stored rather than left a view because every reader of a mapping joins it into a stack of views of its own, and H2 climbed it again inside each: the argMapping key-projection relations ran an order of magnitude slower over the view than over this table. The code reference is what the method a mapping binds into is resolved through, graphitron_code_reference holding it, so no reader matches a method by name again.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_site_rule', 'graph-argmapping-site', 'graphitron',
   'One row the climb from a written argMapping to where it sits computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_site stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_site, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the anchor step that runs it, on the terms every stage rule is kept on: the EXCEPT between this view and the table it fills stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_service_holder', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'The constructor that makes the object an instance @service method runs on: one row per service producer whose method is an instance method and whose class has a buildable constructor, the widest.',
   'For example Query.title @service(service: {className: "SampleInstanceService", method: "titleByFilmId"}) is made by SampleInstanceService(DSLContext).',
   'What the service call reads to make its object, where the generator reflected on the class to choose. Keyed by the producer, since each call makes its own object, and keyed into code_constructor so that a constructor gone from the classpath takes it along. Stored rather than read through the rule because the call that reads it is emitted per field and the rule climbs the producer, the method and every constructor of the class. A class with no buildable constructor has no row; SERVICE_HOLDER_UNBUILDABLE says so.'),
  ('graphitron_service_holder_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the holder rule computes, in the shape graphitron_service_holder stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron assembly inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_service_holder, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_call_context_key', 'graph-call-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'The context key that fills one parameter of one call: one row per code reference and parameter a $context pair or a contextArguments element names once.',
   'For example contextArguments: ["tenantId"] on a @service fills the parameter tenantId of the method its service names with the value the request context carries under tenantId.',
   'What a call''s parameter row reads where a context value fills it, and what decides whether the context can fill a parameter at all. contextArguments is the pair naming its own parameter, so both spellings are one fact here and nothing downstream knows which was written. Keyed by the code reference as written rather than by the method it resolves to, because a key is written whether or not the method is found. Stored rather than read through the rule because its readers join it into the fill and constructor rules, and the climb from a contextArguments element to its reference crosses five relations. A parameter written twice has no row: which of the two to believe is not the store''s to pick, and CONTEXT_KEY_DUPLICATE says so where each was written.'),
  ('graphitron_call_context_key_rule', 'graph-call-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'One row the context-key rule computes, in the shape graphitron_call_context_key stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_call_context_key, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the anchor step that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_call_context_key_written', 'sdl-context-key-writing', 'graphitron',
   'One context key written for one parameter of one call, at the place it was written: a $context pair, or a contextArguments element read as the pair naming its own parameter.',
   'For example contextArguments: ["tenantId"] on a @service writes tenantId for the parameter tenantId of the method its service names.',
   'Every writing, twice-written parameters included, which the rule and the duplicate defect both read so that the two cannot disagree about what was written. A view because nothing constrains it and its only readers are those two, each filtered to one graph.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_call', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call', 'graphitron',
   'One written argMapping entry binding a parameter to an argument path, under one occurrence of the field, argument or input field its directive is written on.',
   'For example filter.minLength on @routine at Actor.filmsNested, under Actor.filmsNested.',
   'The population the landing rule and its defects share, so that the two cannot disagree about which entries owe a landing: a mapping string is written once on a declared element and taken under every place that element is reached. A view, its readers each joining it on its full key.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_step', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call-step', 'graphitron',
   'One segment a written argMapping path consumes under one occurrence of its call, and the occurrence it reaches.',
   'For example filter.minLength on @routine at Actor.filmsNested reaches Actor.filmsNested(filter:) at 1 and Actor.filmsNested(filter.minLength:) at 2.',
   'The path taken a name at a time over the occurrences, one keyed join per name on their parent and name, which is what lets the landing and the defect telling a misspelt name from a cycle read the same steps. Stored rather than a recursive view, because H2 evaluates a recursive view whole, every graph''s paths with it, before a reader''s graph reaches it; written a count at a time, so each statement is over one graph''s rows, and it ends because each step consumes a name, whatever the input types do.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_step_head_rule', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call-step', 'graphitron',
   'One head step the argMapping step rule computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_step stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron assembly inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_step at 1, before the next steps.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_step_next_rule', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call-step', 'graphitron',
   'One next step the argMapping step rule computes from a stored step, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_step stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron assembly inserts this view''s rows for one graph and one count into graphitron_argmapping_step, a count at a time.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it. It reads the stored steps rather than itself, so the graph a writer pins reaches every join.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_landing_key_column', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call-node', 'graphitron',
   'The key column a written argMapping path projects out of the node id it lands on: one row per landing and node binding it projects from.',
   'For example filmId: pick.languageRef.language_id under Query.filmsByOptionalLanguage takes position 0 of the language node''s key, language_id.',
   'A fact of the landing, not of the fills that take it: which column is decided by the written pair, the call occurrence and the node binding there, never by the method or by which of a condition''s per-table calls takes it. Keyed by the landing and the use-site node binding, with foreign keys to both, so a binding that changes takes it. Written by occurrence-bindings after the node ids.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_landing_key_column_rule', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call-node', 'graphitron',
   'One row the landing key column rule computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_landing_key_column stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-bindings stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_landing_key_column, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter_pair', 'graph-occurrence-call-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'The written argMapping pair a fill came from: one row per fill a pair wrote.',
   'For example the filmId parameter of Query.filmsByOptionalLanguage''s condition takes the pair filmId: pick.languageRef.language_id, and a parameter its name or its type filled has no row.',
   'The provenance of a fill, stated on the derived fact: whether a written pair answered decides whether the fill can project a node id''s key column, and a reader reaches the pair''s landing by keys rather than joining back on a nullable parameter name. Written with the fills of each call kind, by the stage owning it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter_pair_rule', 'graph-occurrence-call-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'One row the fill pair rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter_pair stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-producers stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter_pair after the fills, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_landing', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call', 'graphitron',
   'Where one written argMapping entry''s argument path lands under one occurrence of its call: one row per entry and call occurrence whose path resolves.',
   'For example input.ids on @service at Query.filmsByPath lands on Query.filmsByPath(input.ids:).',
   'Where an author''s argument path goes, stated once per place its call is reached and nothing more: what the landing is, a column, a table or a node identity, is its occurrence''s own binding, so a reader joins the occurrence rather than finding a copy here. It replaces the candidate set, which enumerated every path an author might write at every coordinate to serve the few written. A path that does not land has no row and a defect at its mapping string.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_landing_rule', 'graph-argmapping-pair-call', 'graphitron',
   'One row the argMapping landing rule computes, in the shape graphitron_argmapping_landing stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron assembly inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_argmapping_landing, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_argmapping_pair_entry', 'sdl-argmapping-pair', 'graphitron',
   'One entry of an argMapping as one document wrote it: at this position in this mapping, this parameter is bound to this.',
   'For example argMapping: "customerId: input.customerId, session: $session" gives two rows, customerId bound to input.customerId at position 0 and session to $session at 1.',
   'The mapping @routine, @service, @externalField and @condition all take, read once and one way. Each site used to read the string for itself, so what one site supported another did not. The string is the transcription''s already, verbatim at its own position, so this holds what a parse of it says and keys to that position: which directive or reference wrote it is a join, and the raw text is not repeated. Which strings are mappings is the vocabulary''s answer, by name, any argument or input field it calls argMapping. A sigil is recorded as written, admission being a rule about the site. A string that does not parse writes no rows, the transcription keeping it.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_code_reference_entry', 'sdl-code-reference', 'graphitron',
   'A Java code reference as one document wrote it: this position names this class and, where it says, this method.',
   'For example @service(service: {className: "no.example.FilmService", method: "films"}) gives one row at the object value, and @enum(enumReference: {className: "no.example.Rating"}) gives one naming no method.',
   'The fact every site that names Java states, written once. Nine relations carried a class and a method under keys of one shape, one per site, and each was a decode a site wrote for itself, so a reader asking what Java a graph names unioned them. Which values are references is the vocabulary''s answer, read by type: anything it declares ExternalCodeReference, at whatever depth. A site the vocabulary gains therefore writes rows here without new code. Keyed by the node the reference was written as, so the site, the directive and the argument are joins rather than columns, and the reference''s argMapping is the application''s fact and not this one''s.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_enum_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @enum application says: the external code reference this enum declaration is bound to, as written.',
   'For example enum Rating @enum(enumReference: {className: "com.example.Ratings"}) gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the type it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. The reference''s three fields are lifted into columns because an object literal is not a value a reader can query, and each is kept exactly as typed: whether the class is on the classpath and whether it declares that method are questions for the classpath census, which is a different relation and a different reading.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the graphitron gatherer''s entry-defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_field_target_rule''s terms and for its reasons. Stored at all because the read that matters is interactive: the editor asks for one file''s diagnostics, and the chain arms of this rule once reached a relation whose two recursive walks ran over every chain in the graph. A per-file predicate cannot push into a recursion behind a view boundary, so as a view the whole walk ran on every keystroke''s worth of diagnostics, and the LSP''s scan-count gate is what caught it. What the gate measured is not the argument, and the measurement is recorded where measurements live rather than here, because a ceiling moves and a rationale that cites one goes stale the day it does. The argument is that the walk already runs once per capture, Steps storing every step it takes and every place it stops, so a chain defect is read off that record and computing it again per read is a second evaluation of a rule the capture has already performed. The write arms are cheap and are stored with the rest rather than split out, one relation being the name every consumer already spells and the split buying a second relation to keep in agreement with this one.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'A defect a schema has, one row per defect at the written position an author edits to fix it, under the code naming the rule and the stage raising it: the supertype, which a gatherer is encouraged to extend with subtype and detail relations where a defect has more to say than its code and detail.',
   'For example an @orderBy on a @routine mutation field draws one row at the @orderBy''s line, reading READ_SURFACE_ON_WRITE; a detail relation such as graphitron_argmapping_pair_defect is keyed by this key, with a foreign key to it ON DELETE CASCADE, so a swept defect takes its details with it.',
   'The key is the entry, not the coordinate. A coordinate is an aggregate over the sites that declare it, so keying a defect there loses which of two offending applications to fix and reintroduces by the key the ranking this relation exists without. The entry is what the author edits. One relation and not one per defect: eleven sibling relations differed almost only in payload, and every payload column went into a rendered sentence, a prose coordinate or a file position, with nothing computing on them. The family follows the grain rather than being chosen: a defect registers at an entry, this family owns those entries, so it is filed here. A defect at a grain this family does not own belongs with whoever owns it, which is why a generated class publishing malformed constants is not in this population. What the position is about is not stored: graphitron_entry_defect_site reaches the element in fixed hops. Many stages write it, each the rows it raised and under its own name, nobody owning a code, and what one defect has to say beyond its code and detail is a relation of its writer''s keyed by this key, whose foreign key collects it when the stage sweeps the defect.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_claim', 'entry-defect-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One claim a claim defect is about: one row per defect and claim in conflict.',
   'For example a field carrying both @service and @mutation draws a CLAIMS_CONFLICT at the field with a SERVICE row and an INSERT row under it, each claim pointing at where it was written.',
   'One defect per defect, its claims a relation beneath it: a defect''s detail is one string, and the claims an author has to see to fix a conflict are a set, each with a position. Keyed by the defect and the claim, with a foreign key to each, so a swept defect or a claim the reading no longer makes takes the row with it. What a report calls each claim is its reader''s to compose.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_member', 'entry-defect-member', 'graphitron',
   'One member a polymorphic @nodeId''s defect names: one row per defect and member.',
   'For example a union of Film and Actor whose @nodeId lands on a parameter taking FilmRecord draws a NODE_ID_SLOT_NOT_SUPERTYPE_OF_MEMBER defect with an Actor row under it.',
   'One defect per defect, its members a relation beneath it: a verdict about each member of a container is one defect at the @nodeId the author edits, and the members it names are a set. Keyed by the defect and the member, with a foreign key to the defect, so a swept defect takes the rows with it.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_member_rule', 'entry-defect-member', 'graphitron',
   'One member a @discriminate''s defect names, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect_member stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a @discriminate interface whose member Missing carries no @discriminator draws a DISCRIMINATOR_MISSING row naming Missing.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms; the entry defect rule reads it for the two per-member codes of a @discriminate, so one statement says which members a defect names.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_build_entry_defect_member_rule', 'entry-defect-member', 'graphitron',
   'One member a polymorphic @nodeId''s defect names, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect_member stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a union of two node types whose @nodeId lands on a parameter taking one of their records draws a NODE_ID_SLOT_NOT_SUPERTYPE_OF_MEMBER row for the other.',
   'The node id half of the member rule, owned by occurrence-builds as its codes are: the build defect rule reads it for the two per-member codes, and occurrence-builds writes the members it names.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_key_column', 'entry-defect-key-column', 'graphitron',
   'One key column an unresolved projection could have meant, under its defect: one row per defect, landing, node binding and key position.',
   'For example filmId: pick.filmActor onto a FilmActor id names nothing past it, and its ARGMAPPING_KEY_COLUMN_UNRESOLVED defect has a row for each of the two key columns, actor_id and film_id.',
   'One defect per written argMapping string, the key columns it could have meant a relation beneath it rather than a detail string, keyed to the node binding at the landing so a reader names them without parsing. With a foreign key to the defect, so a swept defect takes the rows with it.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_key_column_rule', 'entry-defect-key-column', 'graphitron',
   'One row the unresolved projection detail rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect_key_column stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the entry-defects stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect_key_column under the defects it marked, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms; the entry defect rule reads it for the code, so one statement says where a defect is and which key columns it names.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_list_element_defect', 'occurrence-list-element-defect', 'graphitron',
   'A list of input objects a filter compares as rows that cannot be compared so, at one occurrence: one row per occurrence and rule it breaks there, an element field that cannot be one cell of its row or a @condition taking a value read per element.',
   'For example Query.filmsByCells(cells.filmId:), a node id read per element of cells, draws a LIST_ELEMENT_NODE_ID row.',
   'The predicate the list element defects share, which list an occurrence is read through, whether its field filters and whether its argument is a lookup key, stated once at the occurrence and stored, so the defect rule groups its rows to the declaration over one table rather than repeating the joins per code. Written by occurrence-depth-defects before its defect rule, which reads it, and swept by its mark.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_list_element_defect_rule', 'occurrence-list-element-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the list element defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_list_element_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example occurrence-depth-defects inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_list_element_defect, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_parameter_rule', 'entry-defect-parameter', 'graphitron',
   'One call parameter a parameter defect names, under the defect: one row per defect, occurrence, call and position.',
   'For example a @condition on a field returning a union of customer and staff whose method leaves name unfilled is one PARAMETER_UNFILLED defect at the condition with a row for each table''s call.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms; the entry defect rule reads it for the three parameter codes, so one statement says where a defect is and which parameters it names. A defect''s key is its position and code, so the calls one directive makes, under several fields or for several tables, are named here rather than packed into the detail string.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_table', 'entry-defect-table', 'graphitron',
   'One table a defect names: one row per defect and candidate table.',
   'For example @table(name: "note") where multischema_a and multischema_b both declare note draws a TABLE_AMBIGUOUS defect with a row for each.',
   'One defect per ambiguous @table, its candidates a relation beneath it: the author edits one @table, and the tables its spelling could mean are a set. Keyed by the defect and the table, with a foreign key to the defect, so a swept defect takes the rows with it, and one to sql_table, so a table the catalog loses is named by nothing.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_table_rule', 'entry-defect-table', 'graphitron',
   'One table a defect names, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect_table stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a TABLE_AMBIGUOUS defect whose spelling two schemas answer draws a row for each schema''s table.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the defect''s own detail is the spelling it matched, so the candidates are read from the defect rather than resolved a second time.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_path_step', 'entry-defect-path-step', 'graphitron',
   'One @routine a field''s written path steps through, under the claim defect it conflicts in: one row per defect and step.',
   'For example a field carrying @service and a @routine below a root without @splitQuery draws a CLAIMS_CONFLICT with a SERVICE claim and this row for the routine.',
   'A routine below a root and not split is a step of the field''s path and no claim, yet no claim can share a field with it, so the conflict it is in names it beside the claims. Keyed by the defect and the step''s written position, with a foreign key to each.'),
  ('graphitron_entry_defect_site', 'graphitron-entry-defect-finding', 'graphitron',
   'One finding an author reads, with the element its position is about: one row per position and code over the stages that raised it, and the coordinate, type and field an editor or a report names it by.',
   'For example a READ_SURFACE_ON_WRITE row at the @orderBy written on Mutation.rentFilm reads Mutation.rentFilm, Mutation and rentFilm here.',
   'A view and not columns on the defect, the element being a fact of the position: a declaration is its own element, a directive application''s is its parent, and a value''s is reached through the node holding it, the climb graphitron_code_reference_site already takes. Every hop is a key the transcription declares, so the view states no decision of its own and a reader that wants the element joins it rather than the rule restating it per arm. It collapses the stage (Alf, 2026-11-13): an author sees one finding per position and code, two stages raising one rule wording it alike.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_key', 'outbox-message-key', 'graphitron',
   'One key of one @outbox message''s payload, as the parameter of the message''s call it is, by what fills it.',
   'For example @outbox(argMapping: "filmId: $result.id, title: in.title") on Mutation.createFilm gives two keys, filmId filled by the result''s node id and title by the argument path in.title, and a configured common payload entry adds its own key to every message.',
   'A message is a call its field''s occurrence makes, and a key the parameter of it a fill reaches, in graphitron_occurrence_call_parameter''s vocabulary: an argument or a $result path lands under the occurrence as a service''s argument path does, and the key reads its landing''s binding, a column or a node id table-side and a member''s value object-side, rather than walking the result again. A key no fill reaches has no row and is a defect at its @outbox. Written by the outbox stage from graphitron_outbox_key_rule, marked and swept; read by OutboxQuery and the outbox''s defects.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_key_rule', 'outbox-message-key', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outbox key rule computes, in the shape graphitron_outbox_key stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the outbox stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_outbox_key, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_message', 'outbox-message', 'graphitron',
   'One @outbox message: one application of @outbox on a field, the message the field''s call writes when it succeeds.',
   'For example @outbox(event: "FilmCreated") on Mutation.createFilm is the message at ordinal 0 of Mutation.createFilm, with the event FilmCreated, and a second @outbox beside it a second message.',
   'The application stated once as the message it is, so the outbox''s rules and its emitter read a row rather than joining the application, the field element and the decoded entry again, which the outbox views did three times over. Keyed by the application, the field and the repeat, since a message is what was written, so one written on a field no query reaches is still a message and its defects still land at its position; the field''s occurrence is referenced where there is one, the call whose producer writes the message, which is how placement is judged against the occurrence model rather than against a field-keyed producer. Written by the outbox stage from graphitron_outbox_message_rule, marked and swept.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_message_rule', 'outbox-message', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outbox message rule computes, in the shape graphitron_outbox_message stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the outbox stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_outbox_message, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_target', 'graph-outbox', 'graphitron',
   'The outbox table of one graph, resolved: the table the configuration names and the columns its two roles land in, the event''s type and its payload.',
   'For example <outbox><table>outbox</table></outbox> over a table with a primary key, a type and a payload column, and defaults for its other columns gives one row naming it and the two columns; a configuration that resolves to no one such table has no row and is a defect at each @outbox.',
   'Stored by the outbox stage from graphitron_outbox_target_rule, one row per graph, so its readers, the outbox defects and OutboxQuery, read a row rather than resolving the configuration against the catalog again. A view over the configuration and the catalog, both read before the graphitron gatherer anchors, so the rule is stated once and nothing stores its answer: one row per graph, the key the configuration already has. The table resolves through the graph''s own sources, so a sibling graph''s tables never answer, and an unqualified name must be unambiguous. A role the configuration does not bind takes its own name. A message is a business event, so graphitron fills its type and its payload and nothing else: the table needs a primary key, which is the id a consumer drops a second delivery by, and every other NOT NULL column needs a default, the event''s id and timestamp being the database''s to fill. Routing and keying are the relay''s.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_target_rule', 'graph-outbox', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outbox target rule computes, in the shape graphitron_outbox_target stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the outbox stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_outbox_target, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_routine_target', 'graph-outbox', 'graphitron',
   'The outbox routine of one graph, resolved: the procedure or function the configuration names and the parameter each role lands in.',
   'For example <outbox><function>outbox_emit</function></outbox> over a routine taking type and payload gives one row naming it and the two positions; a configuration that resolves to no one such routine has no row and is a defect at each @outbox.',
   'Stored by the outbox stage from graphitron_outbox_routine_target_rule, one row per graph, as the table target is. Derived from the configuration and the catalog, as the table target is, and beside it: a graph configures one or the other. The routine resolves through the graph''s own sources and must be one the catalog has no table for, since it is called through its Configuration-first method; an unqualified name must be unambiguous. A role answers to the parameter whose name, underscores and case aside, the configuration binds it to, else to the role''s own name, and every parameter must answer to a role.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_routine_target_rule', 'graph-outbox', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outbox routine target rule computes, in the shape graphitron_outbox_routine_target stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the outbox stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_outbox_routine_target, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_key_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the minted-keys stage''s entry-defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example an @issue in a graph with no federation @link gives one VOUCHER_WITHOUT_FEDERATION_LINK row at the @issue.',
   'Owned by the stage that already reads the federation link for the keys it mints, so "this graph is federated" is read in one place for the key and the refusal alike.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the voucher stage''s entry-defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example an @issue whose claims name a field the type does not declare gives one VOUCHER_CLAIM_NOT_ABSORBED row at the @issue.',
   'The voucher stage''s own defects, decided from the transcription, the capture entries, the membership and what the stage has just written, so each reads only what is upstream of it and none waits for a later stage''s facts.'),
  ('graphitron_entity_key_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the entity key stage''s entry-defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a @key(fields: "titel") on a film type whose table has no such column gives one KEY_FIELD_NOT_A_COLUMN row at the @key.',
   'The entity key stage''s own defects, judged from how each key field binds at its entity''s table, which the occurrence bindings state, so the rule reads stored bindings rather than the classified fields the refusals were once made from.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_binding_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the binding defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example occurrence-bindings inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect, a bare @nodeId landing on a table two nodes are bound to giving one NODE_ID_TARGET_AMBIGUOUS row at that @nodeId.',
   'The defects of a node id inferred from the one node over a table where no node or several are, written by the occurrence-bindings stage that writes graphitron_occurrence_node_id, whose missing row they judge, so graphitron_entry_defect_rule does not carry them.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_build_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the build defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example occurrence-builds inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect, an input field filling no member of the bean its input object is built into giving one MEMBER_UNKNOWN row at that field.',
   'The defects of what an input object is built into, written by the occurrence-builds stage that writes the built classes and member bindings they read, as the outbox''s are, so the gatherer''s one rule over every arm, which H2 parses whole wherever it is named, does not carry them.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_depth_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the occurrence defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example occurrence-depth-defects inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect, a field under an object with no key to take off it giving one NO_KEY_FROM_PARENT row at the field.',
   'The defects judging the facts occurrence-bindings and occurrence-builds write, a child producer''s lift and the column a field reads or writes, by a stage of their own after them, so neither the depth loop nor the gatherer''s one rule over every arm carries them.'),
  ('graphitron_outbox_entry_defect_rule', 'graphitron-entry-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outbox stage''s entry-defect rule computes, in the shape graphitron_entry_defect stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the outbox stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_entry_defect, an @outbox on a field that writes nothing giving one OUTBOX_NOT_A_WRITE row at the application.',
   'The outbox stage''s own defects, written as its last statement from the relations it has just written, so the rule reaches the messages and the targets as stored rows rather than through the gatherer''s one rule over every arm.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_error_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'One @error application, at its @ token.',
   'For example type FilmNotFound @error(handlers: [{handler: VALIDATION}]) gives one row, and its one handler is a row of graphitron_ast_error_handler_entry hanging off it.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. The directive''s only argument is the handler list, so the row carries its key and nothing else; it is stored because the handlers are a child relation and a child hangs off the decode of the application it was written inside, which is what lets a reader go from an application to its handlers by key and lets deleting the application take them. An application writing an empty list is a row with no handlers under it.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_error_handler_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One element of an @error application''s handlers list, with the kind it names.',
   'For example handlers: [{handler: DATABASE, sqlState: "23503"}, {handler: VALIDATION}] gives two rows, one of each kind, under the one application.',
   'The supertype of the per-kind relations: what every handler has whatever its kind, which is the element it decodes, the application holding it and the kind, so a reader wanting the handlers of an application, in order, joins one relation rather than unioning several. The kind is stored as written rather than read off which subtype holds a row: a VALIDATION element carries nothing else, and a DATABASE element''s fields stand in graphql_ast_applied_argument_entry, so neither has a subtype of its own. Every object element of an admitted list is a row, and every GENERIC element is a row of its kind''s relation too, whatever it carries. The kind carries a CHECK that states, rather than enforces, what the legality gate already guarantees: only the three declared kinds reach this writer, and the constraint makes a drift in that gate fail at the row. The index is part of the element''s natural key, so it is copied here with the application and made unique beside it; it is a copy rather than a reference to graphql_ast_value_entry, whose position is NULL where a lone object was written for the list, an element this relation places at 0.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_error_generic_handler_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'A GENERIC handler of an @error application, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example {handler: GENERIC, className: "java.lang.IllegalStateException"} at position 0.',
   'One relation per handler kind, because the kind decides which fields mean anything. A GENERIC handler matches by class identity, so it carries a class name and nothing about SQL; the directive rejects sqlState and code on it outright. Every GENERIC element is a row whatever it carries, the class name NULL where none was written: entries capture rather than validate, and a handler naming no class is a defect for a detection to state. Merged with its siblings this would be six nullable columns of which a row fills three, and which three is a rule no constraint over that shape can state. A field an author wrote where their handler''s kind rejects it has no column here and is not lost: the whole handler list stands verbatim in graphql_ast_applied_argument_entry, which is where a reader reporting the rejection finds it.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_binding_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @field application says: the name written, wherever it was written.',
   'For example title: String @field(name: "title") on an output field, films(titleFilter: String @field(name: "title")) on an argument and TITLE @field(name: "title") on an enum value give one row each, all reading title.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the element it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. One relation for every site the definition admits, because the name written is one fact wherever it is written: the key already reaches the application and through it the element and its kind, so a relation per site would be told apart by what the key reaches anyway, and every reader wanting the name at more than one site would put them back together. What the name refers to, a column the element reads or binds, or the sort column an enum value under @order names, follows from the site and is settled where the catalog is read.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_node_id_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @nodeId application says: the node type the identifier belongs to, as written, wherever it was written.',
   'For example id: ID! @nodeId(typeName: "Film") on an output field and actorId: ID @nodeId on an input field give one row each, the first reading Film and the second naming no type.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, on graphitron_ast_binding_entry''s terms: one relation for every site, the site being one join away. Whether an identifier is encoded or decoded follows from that site and is the binding''s question, not a property of what was written. The type name is nullable and nothing here supplies what an omitted one deduces to, the containing type on a plain field and the table''s node type on a referenced one being resolutions over other relations; a NULL is what says the author left the deduction to the build.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_field_condition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @condition application on an output field says: the external method constraining the field''s query, as written.',
   'For example summary: String @condition(condition: {className: "no.example.Conditions", method: "visibleFilm"}) gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the field it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. The reference''s three fields are lifted into columns because an object literal is not a value a reader can query, and each is kept exactly as typed. The override flag is nullable and the definition''s own default is not filled in: what an omitted argument falls back to already stands in graphql_directive_argument.default_value_sdl, and supplying it here would lose the difference between a default taken and a value written, which is exactly the difference a reader reporting on an author''s intent needs. Whether the named class declares that method is a question for the classpath census, which is a different relation and a different reading.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'One application of a directive that carries a path, @reference or @referenceFor, at whichever site it was written.',
   'For example @reference(path: [{table: "film_actor"}, {table: "actor"}]) on Film.actors gives one row at its at sign.',
   'A key-only decode, and not empty for being one: a path''s elements hang from it and cascade with it, so a path has an application to belong to rather than reaching one through the value tree''s holder chain. Both directives carry the same path and are written at a field, an argument and an input field alike, so one relation serves the six combinations, the directive and the site being the applied-directive row''s to say.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_element_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One element of the path an application of @reference or @referenceFor wrote, at its index in the list, whatever it says.',
   'For example @reference(path: [{table: "film_actor"}, {}, {key: "film_actor_actor_id_fk"}]) gives three rows at positions 0, 1 and 2, the second naming nothing.',
   'Entries capture and do not validate, so an element exists whatever it names: one naming none of a table, a key and a condition, which the element type allows, is a row here and a defect for a detection to state, where before it was in no relation at all and a path read through the step relations had a hole at its index. A field the element type does not declare is not admitted by the definition, so assembly reports it and DirectiveLegality keeps the application out of the entries. The step relations are its subtypes, one per thing an element can name.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_field_path_entry', 'sdl-field-path-position', 'graphitron',
   'One element of the path a field declaration wrote, at its place in that path: one row per element of the field''s @routine and @reference applications, composed in the order they were written.',
   'For example hopped @routine(name: "films_for_actor") @reference(path: [{table: "film"}]) gives two rows, the routine at 0 and the element at 1.',
   'The path a field wrote, as a path rather than as the directive applications that spell it: capture sees every directive on the field at once, so it states the composed order once, and the walk and every reader asking what a field wrote read it by the field rather than climbing from each application to its declaration and ranking them.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_path_entry', 'sdl-path-position', 'graphitron',
   'One element of one written path, at its place under the anchor that wrote it: a field declaration''s @routine and @reference applications composed in order, a @referenceFor application, an argument or an input field.',
   'For example Store.people @referenceFor(type: "Staff", path: [{key: "staff_store_id_fkey"}]) gives one row, the key element at place 0 under the @referenceFor application.',
   'Every written path as a path, whatever wrote it: graphitron_ast_field_path_entry holds a field declaration''s alone, so a @referenceFor application, an argument and an input field had nowhere to state their elements in order. Keyed by the anchor''s position within its file, an entry''s own key; a reference from another file reaches an element by its anchor''s name in graphitron_join_path_claim and its place here.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_for_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @referenceFor application says, at any site: the participant type whose join path this application replaces, as written.',
   'For example media: [Media!] @referenceFor(type: "Book", path: [{table: "book"}]) gives one row reading Book.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. Unlike @reference this directive names something besides its path, so its application adds the participant to the path family''s application decode, at whichever site it was written; its path elements are the shared element and step entries, so a participant name is read without touching the steps. Repeated applications are independent, one per participant, where repeated @reference applications concatenate: that difference is a fact about what the two directives mean and shows up here only as a relation the steps reference. Declaring one participant twice is a build error and no key here states it, two documents naming one participant being two rows this relation transcribes and a detection reports.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_table_step_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'The table a path element of a @reference or @referenceFor application names, at any site, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example @reference(path: [{table: "film_actor"}, {table: "actor", key: "film_actor_actor_id_fk"}]) gives two rows.',
   'A path element states a table, a key, a condition, or several of those at once, and each stated fact is a row of its own relation at the element''s shared position. That is what makes every column here total: the alternative is one relation of nine nullable columns whose legal combinations no constraint over that shape could state, which is the shape the @error handlers were split to avoid. A reader wanting the whole element joins the three on the position; a reader wanting only the tables a path names reads one relation and no predicate. Both directives that carry a path, at every site they are written at, write here: an element is one input type wherever it is spelled, and which directive and which site it sits under are facts of its application, reached through the element''s value entry, rather than of the element. So a rule over path elements is written once per fact and reads one relation. The rows hang off the element''s entry, which every element has whatever it says, so the application and the index it was written at are its columns and an element writing none of the three is still an element.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_key_step_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'The foreign key a path element of a @reference or @referenceFor application names, at any site, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example the second element of @reference(path: [{table: "film_actor"}, {table: "actor", key: "film_actor_actor_id_fk"}]) gives one row.',
   'A path element states a table, a key, a condition, or several of those at once, and each stated fact is a row of its own relation at the element''s shared position. That is what makes every column here total: the alternative is one relation of nine nullable columns whose legal combinations no constraint over that shape could state, which is the shape the @error handlers were split to avoid. A reader wanting the whole element joins the three on the position; a reader wanting only the tables a path names reads one relation and no predicate. Both directives that carry a path, at every site they are written at, write here: an element is one input type wherever it is spelled, and which directive and which site it sits under are facts of its application, reached through the element''s value entry, rather than of the element. So a rule over path elements is written once per fact and reads one relation. Whether the named constraint exists is a question for the catalog and belongs where the resolving happens.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_reference_condition_step_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'The condition a path element of a @reference or @referenceFor application names, at any site, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example {table: "actor", condition: {className: "no.example.Conditions", method: "liveActor"}} gives one row here and one in the table relation.',
   'A path element states a table, a key, a condition, or several of those at once, and each stated fact is a row of its own relation at the element''s shared position. That is what makes every column here total: the alternative is one relation of nine nullable columns whose legal combinations no constraint over that shape could state, which is the shape the @error handlers were split to avoid. A reader wanting the whole element joins the three on the position; a reader wanting only the tables a path names reads one relation and no predicate. Both directives that carry a path, at every site they are written at, write here: an element is one input type wherever it is spelled, and which directive and which site it sits under are facts of its application, reached through the element''s value entry, rather than of the element. So a rule over path elements is written once per fact and reads one relation. This is the one arm of an element that reaches the classpath where the others reach the catalog, which is why it carries a class and a method rather than a qualified name.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_connection_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @asConnection application says: the page size the field defaults to and the Connection type name it overrides, as written.',
   'For example films: [Film!] @asConnection(defaultFirstValue: 25) gives one row reading 25.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. Both columns are nullable and the definition''s own defaults are not filled in, so a bare @asConnection is a row of two nulls: that row is what says the directive was applied with nothing said, which is a different fact from an application naming the default page size explicitly, and the fallback itself already stands in graphql_directive_argument.default_value_sdl. The connection name argument is deprecated and the deprecation is the directive definition''s to state; a transcription records what was written and says nothing about whether it should have been.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_issue_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @issue application says: the scope, the lifetime and the claims of the vouchers it issues, as written.',
   'For example voucher: String! @issue(scope: "results", ttl: "PT5M", claims: "ownerId") gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the field it was written on is one join away rather than a column here. The arguments are kept as written: the duration is parsed and the claims resolved to fields by the voucher stage, where a malformed value can be a defect at the application.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_issue_claim_entry', 'sdl-parsed-selection', 'graphitron',
   'One selection an @issue''s claims field set parsed into, at its index in that parse.',
   'For example claims: "eierId" gives one row, eierId at position 0, not nested.',
   'The parse of the claims argument, on the federation key selection''s terms, keyed by the application and the index: what the author wrote, each selection''s first name and whether it nests. Which member a claim names is the voucher stage''s decision, read into graphitron_voucher_claim.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_accept_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @accept application says: the scope of the vouchers it accepts, the authored key they decode to and the claims they carry, as written.',
   'For example voucher: String! @accept(scope: "results", claims: "ownerId") gives one row with no key.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the field it was written on is one join away rather than a column here. The arguments are kept as written: the claims are resolved to fields by the voucher stage and the key to an authored key by the entity key stage, where a malformed value can be a defect at the application. An omitted key is a null, not the one authored key filled in: which key a bare @accept decodes to is the entity key stage''s decision.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_accept_key_entry', 'sdl-parsed-selection', 'graphitron',
   'One selection an @accept''s key field set parsed into, at its index in that parse.',
   'For example key: "id" gives one row, id at position 0, not nested; an @accept with no key gives none.',
   'The parse of the key argument, on graphitron_ast_issue_claim_entry''s terms: what the author wrote, each selection''s first name and whether it nests. Which authored key it names is the entity key stage''s decision, read into graphitron_accept_key, field set against field set.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_accept_claim_entry', 'sdl-parsed-selection', 'graphitron',
   'One selection an @accept''s claims field set parsed into, at its index in that parse.',
   'For example claims: "eierId" gives one row, eierId at position 0, not nested.',
   'The parse of the claims argument, on graphitron_ast_issue_claim_entry''s terms: what the author wrote, each selection''s first name and whether it nests. Which field a claim names is the voucher stage''s decision. A relation of its own rather than a marker column on the issue''s parse, since each references its own application entry.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_component', 'voucher-component', 'graphitron',
   'One voucher member whose column and scalar resolved, the value the voucher carries for it.',
   'For example VgsResultat.id over @field(name: "vgs_resultat_id") reads vgs_resultat_id on the issuing table and serializes as an ID.',
   'The issue emitter''s input: which column each member is read from and which scalar serializes it. A relation apart from the membership because it needs the issuing type''s table, which exists only after the population is built, while the membership must exist before; and because a member whose column fails to resolve is still a member, withheld and refused, never a row here and never published.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_component_rule', 'voucher-component', 'graphitron',
   'One voucher component the component rule computes, in the shape graphitron_voucher_component stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the vouchers stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_voucher_component, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_claim', 'voucher-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One claim of an @issue, resolved to the voucher member it names.',
   'For example claims: "eierId" on a VgsResultat''s voucher gives one row naming the member VgsResultat.eierId.',
   'The claims are decided into rows rather than re-parsed by each reader: the codec''s claims section, the connection key''s typing and the refusals all read which member a claim names. Keyed by the application and the claim''s place, referencing the member, so a claim is an absorbed field by construction and a claim naming anything else draws no row, which is the refusal.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_claim_rule', 'voucher-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One voucher claim the claim rule computes, in the shape graphitron_voucher_claim stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the vouchers stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_voucher_claim, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_accept_claim', 'accept-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One claim of an @accept, resolved to the field of its type it names.',
   'For example claims: "eierId" on a VgsResultat''s voucher gives one row naming the field VgsResultat.eierId.',
   'The claims are decided into rows rather than re-parsed by each reader: the connection key''s typing, the mount''s typed claims and the refusals all read which field a claim names. Keyed by the application and the claim''s place, referencing the field''s graphitron_field row, so a claim is a field of the accepting type by construction and a claim naming anything else draws no row, which is the refusal. Not graphitron_voucher_claim with a marker column: an @issue''s claim references a member, which is no field, and an @accept''s a field, which is no member, so one relation would carry a foreign key null on every row of the other marker. Whether the field reads a column is not decided here: that is how the field binds, which the entity key stage reads.'),
  ('graphitron_accept_claim_rule', 'accept-claim', 'graphitron',
   'One accept claim the claim rule computes, in the shape graphitron_accept_claim stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the vouchers stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_accept_claim, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_member', 'voucher-member', 'graphitron',
   'One field declaration an @issue absorbs into its voucher, withheld from the emitted population.',
   'For example a VgsResultat whose voucher field carries @issue absorbs its id and eierId, two rows, while the voucher field itself is no member.',
   'An absorbed field is not a field classified and then hidden: the store keeps no binding, claim or occurrence for a coordinate that is no graphitron_element row, so the membership is decided before the population is built and the authored sets anti-join it. Decided from structure alone, the issue application and the type''s transcribed fields, and independently of every refusal, so a resolution failure or a refused @issue is a defect and never a field A publishes. Written by its own stage ahead of macro-expansion, since it has to exist before the population does.'),
  ('graphitron_voucher_member_rule', 'voucher-member', 'graphitron',
   'One voucher member the membership rule computes, in the shape graphitron_voucher_member stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the voucher-members stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_voucher_member, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_outbox_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @outbox application says: the event it names and the mapping that builds its payload, as written.',
   'For example createFilm(in: FilmCreateInput!): Film @outbox(event: "FilmCreated", argMapping: "filmId: $result.id") gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the field it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. Both arguments are kept as typed and nothing is checked: an omitted event defaults to the field''s coordinate in the anchor, and the mapping string is read by the generic argMapping writer into its pair entries, which is where its entries are decoded.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_mutation_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @mutation application says: which write the field performs, over how many rows, against which table, as written.',
   'For example deleteFilm(input:): Boolean @mutation(typeName: DELETE, multiRow: true, table: "film") gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, so the field it was written on and the file are one join away rather than columns here. The operation is kept as the token the author typed and never checked against the vocabulary the definition declares: nothing upstream has validated it, so a CHECK would turn a typo into a constraint violation, and membership is a detection over this column. The table name is split by the grammar that cuts it and nothing more; what an omitted table resolves to is a resolution over the field''s own chain and belongs where that chain is walked.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_routine_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @routine application says: the database routine backing the field and the mapping binding its parameters, as written.',
   'For example reportedFilms: [Film!] @routine(name: "public.reported_films", argMapping: "pEnv: env") gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, which is also what tells two applications on one field apart: the directive repeats and no ordinal is assigned here, numbering the applications being the anchors'' work over rows that carry the position the numbering sorts by. The routine name is split by the grammar that cuts a qualified name and nothing more. Both mappings are kept as the strings the author typed: they share a grammar and not a subject, one binding a parameter to an input path and the other to a column of the previous chain node, which is why they are columns beside each other rather than one, and cutting either into segments is the argument-mapping derivation''s work.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_federation_key_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a federation @key application says: the field set naming the entity''s key, and whether the subgraph resolves it, as written.',
   'For example type Film @key(fields: "id reviews { id }") gives one row carrying that field set verbatim.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, which is also what tells two applications on one type apart: the directive repeats and no ordinal is assigned here. The field set is kept whole in a column and parsed into the two relations below it, which is this family''s only grammar whose output is rows. The string stays because the parse is a reading of it and a re-emitter wants what was read. The rows exist because the anchors this derives into have held selections and segments since before the entry relations did: the shape is transcribed rather than chosen here, and a decode that rendered the parse back into a dotted name would hand that derivation a string to take apart again. No reader outside those anchors consumes a key today, so what the rows are worth is a question the first one will settle.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_federation_key_selection_entry', 'sdl-parsed-selection', 'graphitron',
   'One leaf selection the field set parsed into, at its index in that parse.',
   'For example "id reviews { id }" gives two rows, one for id and one for the id inside reviews.',
   'A grammar yields a list and a relation holds a set, so the order is a column. That is all the ordinal is. A decoded element that is a node the author wrote carries no such column, being identified by the position it was written at and ordered by the value entry relation that records it; a field set is one string, so its selections have no position of their own and the ordinal is what makes them a list rather than a bag. A malformed field set yields whatever prefix parsed, so a selection missing from here is a defect a detection reports over the column beside it rather than an absence this relation asserts.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_federation_key_segment_entry', 'sdl-parsed-selection-segment', 'graphitron',
   'One segment of one parsed selection, at its depth from the outermost inward.',
   'For example the selection reached by "reviews { id }" gives two rows, reviews then id.',
   'Nesting is part of federation''s grammar and survives the decode as segments, rather than being flattened into a dotted string. Two relations rather than one because a selection exists whether or not it nests: the selection row is what a reader counts and joins on, and the segments are how deep it went. That today''s generator rejects a nested key is a detection''s business and not a limit on what capture records, which is the same rule every decode in this family takes about input it is handed.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_discriminate_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @discriminate application says: the column whose value decides which subtype a row is, as written.',
   'For example interface Media @discriminate(on: "media_type") gives one row whose on_column is media_type.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. One end of a two-directive mechanism, and the relations are apart because the ends are: this one is written on the interface or union that has subtypes, and its counterpart on each object that is one, so they sit at different declarations and a reader joining them is asking a question about the corpus rather than reading one row. The column name is kept as the author typed it and resolves against nothing here; that the named column exists, and that every subtype claims a distinct value of it, are questions a detection asks of these rows together.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_discriminator_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @discriminator application says: the value of the discriminating column that means a row is this subtype, as written.',
   'For example type Book implements Media @discriminator(value: "BOOK") gives one row whose discriminator_value is BOOK.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, and the subtype end of the mechanism graphitron_ast_discriminate_entry opens. The value is a string whatever the SQL type of the column it will be compared against, because that is what an author can write in SDL and this relation states what was written; coercing it here would need the catalog, which no decode reads. An object implementing two discriminated interfaces writes one row per application, so the value is not assumed to be the object''s only one.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_node_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @node application says: that the type is addressable by an opaque id, and what it calls itself inside one, as written.',
   'For example type Film @node(typeId: "F") gives one row whose type_id is F.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. A row is written for every application rather than only for those that wrote an argument, both arguments being optional and the key-column list being the child relation its elements hang off. The type id is kept as written and never defaulted: the directive documents an omitted typeId as meaning the type''s own name, and that substitution is a reading of the document rather than a fact of it, so a reader wanting the effective value joins the declaration and a reader wanting to know what the author chose asks this column.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_node_keycolumn_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One keyColumns element of a @node application, as written.',
   'For example keyColumns: ["film_id"] gives one row, keyed where film_id was written.',
   'A list argument becomes rows rather than a column, and the row names the element it decodes rather than the application and an index into it. The order matters here as much as anywhere in the family, the directive stating that the positions are the order the columns take inside an id, and that is exactly why this relation does not restate it: the element''s own row carries its index already, and a second numbering would be a number a reader could only trust by reconstructing the list it came from. The list is optional, and its absence is the author asking for the database''s own primary key in the order the database has it, which is why an omitted list writes no row rather than a defaulted set. Every element is a string because the definition admits nothing else, an application writing anything else being refused before it reaches a decode at all.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_node_legacykeycolumn_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One legacyKeyColumns element of a @node application, as written.',
   'For example legacyKeyColumns: ["customer_id", "rental_id", "rental_date"] gives three rows, each keyed where its column was written.',
   'The twin of graphitron_ast_node_keycolumn_entry for the second list argument of @node, decoded the same way so the two arguments are captured alike: the row names the element it decodes, whose own row carries its index. An omitted list writes no row.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_link_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a federation @link application says: the specification the schema opts in to, as written.',
   'For example a schema extension applying @link with federation v2.10 as its url argument gives one row carrying that string.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, which is also what tells two applications apart: the directive repeats and no ordinal is assigned here, numbering the applications being the anchors'' work over rows that carry the position the numbering sorts by. The URL is kept as the string the author typed and nothing is read out of it, the specification it names and the version in its last segment both being questions for a reader that knows what federation versions mean. A row is written for every application rather than only for those that wrote a URL, because the import list is the child relation and its elements need the parent to hang off.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_federation_link_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'A @link application whose url names the federation specification, and the version it names.',
   'For example a schema extension applying @link with federation v2.10 as its url gives one row carrying v2.10, and a @link to any other specification gives none.',
   'The decode of what the url means, made where the specification''s prefix is a constant, so a reader asking whether a graph is federated joins a row rather than filtering urls with a literal of its own. A child of the link entry at the same key, the url being any specification''s and this the one the generator acts on.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_link_import_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One import element of a @link application, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example import: ["@key", {name: "@shareable", as: "@federatedShareable"}] gives two rows, the second carrying an alias.',
   'A list argument becomes rows rather than a column, and this list is the one place in the family where an element admits two spellings: federation writes an import as a bare string or as an object binding it to a local name, and both are the same fact with the alias absent in the first. So the two spellings are one relation with a nullable alias rather than two relations or a column saying which was written, the spelling being syntax and the import being what a reader wants. An element that names nothing writes no row, the name being the import''s whole identity, and it costs its neighbours nothing: every element keys by where it was written rather than by a count, so a row missing here removes a row and not a number. @link is federation''s vocabulary and this generator declares no definition for it, so the legality rule does not reach these elements and that gap is the ordinary one a NOT NULL column makes.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_compose_directive_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a schema-level @composeDirective application says: the directive the schema asks the supergraph to carry, as written.',
   'For example extend schema @composeDirective(name: "@nodeType") gives one row whose directive_name is @nodeType.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, which is also what tells two applications apart: the directive repeats and no ordinal is assigned here. Federation spells the directive two ways, @composeDirective where the schema''s @link imports it and @federation__composeDirective where it does not, and both are the same fact, so the writer reads both into this one relation rather than leaving every reader to ask twice. A row is written only for an application that wrote its name as a string: the argument is required, so an application without one asserts nothing a row can hold, and the applied-directive row already says it was applied. The question it answers is which directives a graph composes, which graphitron_synthesized_node_type_id asks of @nodeType; an import under an alias is not resolved and so is not recognised.'),

  ('graphitron_ast_index_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @index application says: the database index the enum value names, as written.',
   'For example TITLE_INDEX @index(name: "idx_film_title") gives one row whose index_ref is idx_film_title.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. The one argument the directive declares is its whole payload, so the column is NOT NULL and an application that wrote no name writes no row: such a row would carry its key and nothing else, which is what the applied-directive row already says, and that is the rule every single-argument decode in this family takes. The index name resolves against the catalog and not against anything here; an @index naming an index the database does not have is a document this relation transcribes and a detection reports.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_order_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What an @order application says: which of the three sorting surfaces an enum value selects, as written.',
   'For example BY_TITLE @order(fields: [{name: "title"}]) gives one row whose two columns are both NULL, the sort being the child rows.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position, and the enum-value twin of a @defaultOrder application. The directive admits exactly one of an index, a field list and the primary key, and no constraint here states that: an application naming two is a document this relation transcribes and a detection reports. The field list is the child relation rather than a rendered literal, so an application that took that surface is a row here whose payload columns are both null and whose sort is the rows beside it, which is also why a row is written for every application rather than only for those that wrote an argument. The primary-key column is null where the author wrote nothing, the directive''s declared default being a generator constant rather than a fact about the document.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_order_field_entry', 'sdl-written-value', 'graphitron',
   'One fields element of an @order application, at its position in the list, as written.',
   'For example fields: [{name: "title", collate: "xdanish_ai"}] gives one row at position 0.',
   'A list argument becomes rows rather than a column, and here the order is the answer: the position an element was written at is the sort order the author asked for, so a reader takes it from the key rather than from parsing a literal. FieldSort is the element type the directive definition declares, so an element of some other shape makes the whole application one @order does not admit and none of it is transcribed. Each field of the element becomes a column, all three nullable because an element may write only its name and because a NULL direction is what says the element takes the enclosing application''s, filling one in being a loss of which of the two the author wrote.'),
  ('graphitron_ast_input_value_condition_entry', 'sdl-declaration-site', 'graphitron',
   'What a @condition application on an input value says: the external method deciding the predicate, as written.',
   'For example scope: ID @condition(condition: {className: "no.example.Conditions", method: "inScope"}) gives one row.',
   'A decode of one directive application, keyed by the application''s own position. The reference''s three fields are lifted into columns because an object literal is not a value a reader can query, and each is kept exactly as typed: whether the class is on the classpath and whether it declares that method are questions for the classpath census. The override argument is NULL where the author wrote none rather than carrying the definition''s false, which keeps the difference between a default taken and a value written; the default itself stands in graphql_directive_argument.default_value_sdl. The context arguments are a relation of their own, one row per list position, so deleting this row takes them.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_conflict', 'minted-conflict', 'graphitron',
   'A coordinate several macro applications would mint and disagree about: one row per contested coordinate in the graph.',
   'For example two carriers naming one connection through connectionName over different element types disagree about that connection''s own nodes field, which draws a row here and no row at all in graphitron_field.',
   'Two applications minting one coordinate the same way are the ordinary case and the whole reason the minted relations key by their source, shared machinery being stated whole by every carrier. Two that disagree are not, and neither of them may win. Picking one would put a shape in the emitted population that no application asked for and that nothing records, which is the class of silence this line of work exists to remove; refusing outright would be worse, because the schema is one an author can write and capture runs before assembly and for readers that never run it, so the store has to exist for the diagnostic to sit in. So the coordinate is withheld and this row is what says why, on the claim conflict''s terms, which is the same shape one increment earlier: a contested coordinate, neither claimant, and a relation naming it. What each application would have written is not copied here; the minted relations keep it, keyed by the coordinate that coined each, so the readings are a join away rather than a duplicate.'),
  ('graphitron_element', 'expanded-element', 'graphitron',
   'A schema element the generator emits exists in this graph, whether an author declared it or macro expansion minted it: the supertype of the four element relations beside it, keyed by the schema coordinate the GraphQL specification spells for it.',
   'For example a QueryFilmsConnection no author wrote is the row QueryFilmsConnection here and no row at all in graphql_element, and the field carrying the macro is one row in each.',
   'A graphitron relation naming a coordinate has nowhere to key. graphql_element holds what the document declares, so a foreign key there excludes exactly the coordinates macro expansion minted, which is what a connection is made of; the relation that shipped as graphitron_field was written with that key and the build failed on a minted connection''s own field. The union views that answered for the expanded population could not stand in, a view being no key''s target. So this is that population written down, at the grain the specification already gives it, with the same spelling and the same kind vocabulary graphql_element uses so that a reader holding a coordinate from either family holds the same string. The three subtypes beside it carry the parts and the payload together, which is where this family parts company with the transcription: an element the expansion minted has no twin to join for its details, so an anchor that carried only a key would send every reader back through a union.'),
  ('graphitron_facet_binding', 'facet-binding', 'graphitron',
   'What one @asFacet application binds, at the applications that are well formed: the column its counts group by, the named type those counts are keyed on, and whether such a key may be null.',
   'For example @asFacet on FilmFilter.rating is one row naming the rating column and the type its values carry.',
   'Moved out of the intent_ family by the arc that owns macro expansion. That family has no owning gatherer and is being retired, and what this derives from is graphitron''s own vocabulary decoded, so this is where the fact belongs and the graphitron gatherer, which runs the expansion, is who answers for it. The definition-keyed half of the facet reading, keyed on the input field the directive sits on and resolving nothing about who consumes it; graphitron_connection_facet is the use-keyed half and reads this one. The split is the resolver''s own rather than a convenience, the well-formedness predicate being stated once here instead of at each consumer. A malformed application contributes no row, which is what makes the relation safe to read without re-checking it: the detection that reports the malformation reads the entries, not this.'),
  ('graphitron_connection_carrier', 'connection-carrier', 'graphitron',
   'A field @asConnection expands, with the names and wrapping its expansion needs: one row per carrier.',
   'For example films: [Film!]! @asConnection on Query is one row naming QueryFilmsConnection, QueryFilmsConnectionEdge and the element Film!, where the same directive on a films: Film field is no row at all.',
   'The admission test and the naming rule are one decision each, and this is where each is made. Every arm of the mint reads it, so an arm cannot disagree with its siblings about which fields are carriers or what a carrier''s connection is called, which is what happens when the join is repeated per arm. Admission is a column test rather than a parse: a bare list of a named type is is_list with list_depth 1, and the expansion used to read that off type_sdl by hand because the anchor dropped the column the entry had already captured. The position travels with the row because the anchor resolving two carriers onto one connection name has to say which application it honoured, and a coordinate cannot say that. Stored, by macro expansion before anything it expands, and keyed into the authored field it describes: the authored fields exclude carriers by anti-join, and as a view joining four relations it was re-evaluated once per field, which on a large consumer schema took the field write from half a second to forty.'),
  ('graphitron_connection_carrier_rule', 'connection-carrier', 'graphitron',
   'One row the connection carrier rule computes, in the shape graphitron_connection_carrier stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_connection_carrier, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_connection_facet', 'carrier-facet', 'graphitron',
   'Which facets one connection carrier surfaces, and in which order: one row per @asFacet application reachable from the carrier''s own filter arguments.',
   'For example films(filter: FilmFilter) @asConnection surfaces every facet FilmFilter declares, in emission order.',
   'Moved out of the intent_ family by the arc that owns macro expansion. That family has no owning gatherer and is being retired, and what this derives from is graphitron''s own vocabulary decoded, so this is where the fact belongs and the graphitron gatherer, which runs the expansion, is who answers for it. The carrier population is the expansion rather than the directive: graphitron_connection_carrier holds a row exactly where @asConnection expands, so an application that expanded nothing carries no facets here and neither does a structural Connection return type. Reachability is one hop, the facet field being declared on a type an argument of the carrier names, which is the expansion''s own walk and not a closure through nested inputs. position is dense per carrier and is the emission order, which is what lets a consumer fold these rows into a file and get the same bytes twice. A name repeated across one carrier''s filters collapses to its first occurrence, which is a backstop rather than a rule: a duplicate is rejected with a named diagnostic before it can reach an accepted schema.'),
  ('graphitron_argument_reachable_input', 'reachable-input-type', 'graphitron',
   'Which input types the emitted schema can reach from a field argument, directly or through the input fields of one it reaches.',
   'For example a FilmFilter named by Query.films(filter:) is one row, and a RatingFilter that FilmFilter declares a field of is another, while an input type nothing names is absent.',
   'A view because nothing in the model derives from it. A consumer''s query reads it and that is all it is for, so it carries the rule and no table carries the rows. The moment another derivation builds on it, it is an anchor and owes a table: an anchor is what other derivations stand on, and a table is what holds a key, a foreign key and a check so an illegal state is unwritable rather than merely unexpected. That is the condition to test when adding the second reader, and it is not a question of how many consumers ask: a hundred queries reading it directly leave it a view, and one derivation standing on it does not. Stated over the emitted family rather than the transcription, because a macro mints arguments and an input reached only through a minted one is reachable in the schema the generator emits and absent from the one the author wrote. The recursion carries a path and unions all, rather than unioning distinct, because an input type may declare a field of its own type and the cycle has to be stopped as it is walked; the path is what the recursion consumes and the grain reports neither it nor how many ways a type was reached. What is deliberately not here is any bound by classification: the Java fold this replaces closed through classified input types and stopped where classification had yielded none, which narrowed it on schemas that were failing for other reasons, and nothing states that bound as intended.'),
  ('graphitron_synthesized_node_type_id', 'synthesized-node-type-id', 'graphitron',
   'Which node types publish their wire type id in the emitted schema as @nodeType, because the graph composes that directive.',
   'For example a graph whose schema applies @composeDirective(name: "@nodeType") gets one row per node type, Film''s carrying the id its @node declares, its class publishes, or its own name. A stub, @node(resolvable: false), gets none: the subgraph owning the entity publishes its id.',
   'A derivation and not a capture, on the minted key''s terms: a node subgraph resolving Query.node(id) has to learn which type an id belongs to, and the supergraph can only carry that if the owning subgraph publishes it, so the emitted schema gains a directive no author wrote. The type id is graphitron_node.type_id as resolved, so nothing here restates the three-tier rule or the node population, which is that relation''s, table-bound by its key. The condition is the author''s own composition of the directive, read from graphitron_ast_compose_directive_entry: composing it is the opt-in, so no setting exists and a graph that does not compose it publishes what it published before. This relation is its own provenance, which is what lets the synthesized application stay out of the transcription families entirely.'),

  ('graphitron_element_authored', 'expanded-element', 'graphitron',
   'One of the four sets graphitron_element is the union of: an element an author declared, at the coordinate the transcription spells for it, of a kind this family anchors.',
   'For example the Film in type Film { title: String } is one row, and the Film.title written inside it is another.',
   'Named rather than left inside the anchor''s writer, where it was one arm of a four-arm insert whose admission rules lived in Java predicates a reader of the schema could not find. The five kinds it claims are listed rather than the other two excluded, so a kind added to graphql_element arrives refused rather than uninvited, and the refusal is a row missing from the schema rather than a silent widening. This set alone carries no precedence: an author''s declaration is the thing the minted sets stand down to, so what admits a row here is only that the transcription anchors it.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_coinage', 'minted-coinage', 'graphitron',
   'Which coordinate coined each type the expansion mints: one row per minted type name and the application it was derived from.',
   'For example films: [Film!]! @asConnection with a title facet coins QueryFilmsConnection, QueryFilmsConnectionEdge, PageInfo, QueryFilmsConnectionFacets and StringFacetValue, all at Query.films.',
   'Named because the pairing was being restated by each reader that wanted it, once as a staging table''s column and then as an enumeration of arms in Java. Distinct because shared machinery is coined by every carrier that wants it and a name coined twice at one coordinate is one row; not distinct across coordinates, because which application coined a name is the fact this relation exists to carry.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_coinage_rule', 'minted-coinage', 'graphitron',
   'One row the coinage rule computes, in the shape graphitron_minted_coinage stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_minted_coinage, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, on graphitron_type_backing_rule''s terms. Stored by MacroExpansion before the directive anchor reads it, because H2 evaluates a view again for every row joined to it and the federation chain joins these three to each other: on a consumer schema configuring 7734 tags over 654 coinages the directive upsert did not finish, and with the three stored it reads in a fifth of a second.'),
  ('graphitron_element_minted_type', 'expanded-element', 'graphitron',
   'One of the four sets graphitron_element is the union of: a type macro expansion adds to the schema, which no author declared and no two applications disagree about.',
   'For example an author writing films: [Film!]! @asConnection and nothing else gets QueryFilmsConnection here, where an author who also wrote their own QueryFilmsConnection gets no row.',
   'Precedence decides nothing at this grain, and saying so once here is clearer than carrying a disjunction that cannot fire. An element is a coordinate, so a mint at a coordinate the transcription already anchors is excluded outright and the authored set has it; there is no reading under which a REPLACE would be the reason a row survived. Distinct because shared machinery is stated whole by every carrier that wants it: several rows arrive for one PageInfo differing in nothing but which carrier wrote them, and the anchor is one row per coordinate because that is what a coordinate is.'),
  ('graphitron_element_minted_field', 'expanded-element', 'graphitron',
   'One of the four sets graphitron_element is the union of: a field macro expansion adds to the schema, on the minted type set''s terms and one more.',
   'For example the edges and pageInfo on a minted QueryFilmsConnection, and nothing at all where the author declared that type themselves.',
   'The extra rule is the one worth reading twice: a field is excluded where the type it sits on was minted by the same application and lost to an author''s declaration of that name. Machinery fields collide with nothing, so a rule stated at the field grain alone would land edges and pageInfo on the author''s type and fuse two types nobody asked to merge. Losing the type takes its fields with it, and the coordinate that coined both is what ties them together. Distinct on the minted type set''s terms, and for its reason.'),
  ('graphitron_element_minted_argument', 'expanded-element', 'graphitron',
   'One of the four sets graphitron_element is the union of: a field argument macro expansion adds to the schema, which today is the pagination a connection carrier gets where its author wrote none.',
   'For example the first and after on Query.films(first:) where the author wrote films: [Film!]! @asConnection with no arguments.',
   'On the minted type set''s terms and no more: an argument the transcription already anchors is not minted over, and a contested coordinate belongs to nobody. No rule of the field set''s second kind is owed here, every argument minted today landing on the carrier the directive sits on, and that field surviving whether the expansion rewrote it or left it, so there is no owning element whose fate an argument could have to share.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_definition', 'federation-definition', 'graphitron',
   'One federation definition a graph''s link brings in, under the name the graph sees it by.',
   'For example @key as @key where the link imports it, as @federation__key where it does not, and FieldSet as federation__FieldSet unless imported.',
   'The one place a federation definition''s local name is decided, from the link''s imports and the library''s name for what it leaves out, so the directives, their argument types and the types themselves read one answer.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_definition_rule', 'federation-definition', 'graphitron',
   'The rule graphitron_federation_definition stores: one row per federation definition a graph''s link brings in.',
   'For example every definition of the version the link names, under the name its imports give it.',
   'The rule, on graphitron_federation_link_rule''s terms. It reads the stored link and imports, which the anchor fills first.'),
  ('graphitron_directive', 'expanded-directive', 'graphitron',
   'A directive the graph''s schema declares, written or brought in by its federation link.',
   'For example a graph linking federation and importing @key holds key here as FEDERATION beside every directive its documents define, and federation__shareable where the link leaves @shareable out.',
   'graphql_directive holds what documents define, which is not the vocabulary the composed schema has: the link brings federation''s in, and an editor judging against the written ones alone calls a directive the generator accepts unknown. This is the composed vocabulary, which the editor and the hover read.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_rule', 'expanded-directive', 'graphitron',
   'The rule graphitron_directive stores: one row per directive a graph declares.',
   'For example what its documents define, and the federation directives its link brings in under the names it brings them in by.',
   'The rule, on graphitron_federation_link_rule''s terms. A name a document defines and the link brings in is the document''s, the composition refusing the collision.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_argument', 'expanded-directive-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One argument a directive the graph declares takes.',
   'For example fields: federation__FieldSet! of @key in a graph whose link imports @key and not FieldSet, its type under the name the graph sees.',
   'On graphitron_directive''s terms, at the argument grain, which is what the editor''s argument checks read.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_argument_rule', 'expanded-directive-argument', 'graphitron',
   'The rule graphitron_directive_argument stores: one row per argument of a directive a graph declares.',
   'For example the arguments its documents write, and those of the federation directives its link brings in.',
   'The rule, on graphitron_directive_rule''s terms, mapping a federation argument''s type through graphitron_federation_definition.'),
  ('graphitron_type_federation', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One of the sets graphitron_type is the union of: a type a graph''s federation link brings in.',
   'For example federation__FieldSet, the scalar @key''s fields: argument takes, in a graph whose link does not import FieldSet.',
   'The composed schema holds the types the link brings in, and a reader asking what a federation argument''s type is asks graphitron_type like any other. Written after the link''s imports, a name an author or the mint already holds staying theirs.'),
  ('graphitron_type', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'Every type the generator works with, the author''s and the ones macro expansion minted, under one key: one row per type name in the graph.',
   'For example a Connection type the connection macro minted sits here beside the type whose field carried the macro. Total from federation-vocabulary on, the stage adding the last population, so a consumer from there joins this rather than left-joining it.',
   'The type grain of the emitted population, as a table where it was a view. Two things follow from being a table and neither is available to a union: a relation about a minted type can key here, and a reader can be given an index. The payload is carried rather than joined from graphql_type because a minted type has no row there, so the join would be a left join whose null arm is the whole reason this relation exists. What it does not carry is the declaration site, that being a fact only the transcription can assert. The supertype every type-grained relation keys into by type and kind: a table is a type, and a node is a table.'),
  ('graphitron_type_authored', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_type is the union of: a type an author declared, carried across whole.',
   'For example type Film { title: String } is the row Film here, with the kind and docstring the author wrote.',
   'Named rather than left as an arm of the anchor''s writer, where it was one half of a union whose admission lived in a Java predicate. It excludes nothing, and that absence is the minting rule showing through: no arm of the type mint replaces, so an author''s declaration always survives, and an exclusion that excludes nothing would read as though something could.'),
  ('graphitron_type_minted_candidate', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'What the arms of the type mint state before any disagreement between them is resolved.',
   'For example two carriers landing on one connection name are two rows here and none in graphitron_type_minted.',
   'The population graphitron_minted_conflict is counted over, and so the one relation of the pair that may hold two rows for one coordinate. Its sibling cannot: a set that excluded the contested coordinates would have to be read to find them, which is the circle this pair opens. It also answers the reader that wants what the expansion states rather than what survived, which is how a stood-down mint still carries its edge.'),
  ('graphitron_field_minted_candidate', 'expanded-field', 'graphitron',
   'What the arms of the field mint state before any disagreement between them is resolved.',
   'For example two carriers disagreeing about one connection''s nodes field are two rows here and none in graphitron_field_minted.',
   'On graphitron_type_minted_candidate''s terms. It carries the rewrite flag its set does not need to expose, because whether a row restates an authored field or adds a new one decides which arm of the element anchor owns its coordinate.'),
  ('graphitron_argument_minted_candidate', 'expanded-argument', 'graphitron',
   'What the arms of the argument mint state before any disagreement between them is resolved.',
   'For example a carrier whose author already wrote first is one row here and none in graphitron_argument_minted.',
   'On graphitron_type_minted_candidate''s terms.'),
  ('graphitron_carrier_facet_mint', 'carrier-facet', 'graphitron',
   'What one connection carrier''s facets add to the schema: one row per facet the carrier surfaces, carrying the container and value-type names the mint derives.',
   'For example films(filter: FilmFilter) @asConnection with a title facet is one row naming QueryFilmsConnectionFacets and StringFacetValue.',
   'The naming rule for the facet triad, stated once where the carrier and its facets meet. Three arms of the mint read it, so none of them spells the container''s name or a bucket''s from the scalar a second time, which is how the two came to disagree before. At the carrier-facet grain rather than the carrier''s, because what it adds is per facet: one container per carrier but a bucket type and a container field per facet on it.'),
  ('graphitron_type_minted', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_type is the union of: a type macro expansion adds, filling a name no author took.',
   'For example QueryFilmsConnection where the author wrote only films: [Film!]! @asConnection.',
   'The complement of graphitron_type_authored, and disjoint from it by the same constraint: a mint fills a name nobody took, so no coordinate is in both. Distinct because shared machinery is stated whole by every carrier that wants it, the rows differing in nothing but which carrier wrote them. A coordinate the applications disagree about is in neither set, graphitron_minted_conflict holding the reason.'),
  ('graphitron_field_authored', 'expanded-field', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_field is the union of: a field an author declared that no expansion rewrote, carried across whole.',
   'For example the title in type Film { title: String }, where films: [Film!]! @asConnection is not here, the expansion having restated it.',
   'The one authored set that excludes anything, and the exclusion is real here: a rewritten carrier is restated by the expansion, so the author''s row is not what the schema emits. This is where precedence is load-bearing, and the only grain where it is.'),
  ('graphitron_field_minted', 'expanded-field', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_field is the union of: a field macro expansion states, either adding one where the author wrote none or restating the carrier the directive sits on.',
   'For example the edges on a minted QueryFilmsConnection, and the rewritten films whose author wrote it as a plain list.',
   'The complement of graphitron_field_authored, and the one set that overlaps the element family: a rewritten carrier is an authored element and a minted field, because an element is a coordinate and the coordinate did not change. That disagreement is why the part sets are stated rather than derived from the element sets, which would be right at five of the six and silently wrong here. A field also stands down where the type it sits on was minted by the same application and lost to an author''s declaration of that name; losing the type takes its fields with it, and the coordinate that coined both is what ties them together.'),
  ('graphitron_argument_authored', 'expanded-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_argument is the union of: an argument an author declared, carried across whole.',
   'For example the lang in title(lang: Lang = NB): String, default included.',
   'On graphitron_type_authored''s terms and for its reason: no minted argument replaces, the pagination arms adding names rather than taking them, so an author''s argument always survives and this set excludes nothing.'),
  ('graphitron_argument_minted', 'expanded-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One of the two sets graphitron_argument is the union of: an argument macro expansion adds where the author wrote none, which today is a connection carrier''s pagination.',
   'For example the first and after on Query.films(first:).',
   'The complement of graphitron_argument_authored. No rule of the field set''s second kind is owed: every argument minted today lands on the carrier the directive sits on, and that field survives whether the expansion rewrote it or left it, so there is no owning element whose fate an argument could have to share.'),
  ('graphitron_field', 'expanded-field', 'graphitron',
   'Every field the generator works with, at the type expression it works with: one row per field coordinate, output and input alike, carrying the wrapping columns graphql_field carries.',
   'For example a field the connection macro rewrote reads here as the Connection it returns, where graphql_field is where a reader goes for what the author wrote instead.',
   'The field grain of graphitron_type''s argument, with one difference worth stating. A type is minted or authored and never both, so that population is a disjoint union; a field can be authored at a coordinate whose type expression a macro then rewrote, which is a disagreement at a coordinate both populations hold rather than a row only one of them has. This relation states the generator''s reading at such a coordinate and graphql_field states the author''s, so the two are recoverable separately instead of the authored expression surviving only in a provenance record no anti-join reaches.'),
  ('store_graph_source_input', 'graph-source-input', 'graphql-source',
   'What a graph''s configuration says about one schema file it reads: one row per file the recipe matched, with the tag and description note its entry carries.',
   'For example a file matched by an entry configured with tag stable is one row carrying stable, and a file matched by an untagged entry is a row carrying none.',
   'The configuration''s tags and notes reached the schema the run emits and no relation said which element they reached, so a store-sourced reader dropped them: the tag a carrier takes from its file never reached the type it coined. The recipe entry''s own row cannot answer it, a pattern entry matching many files and the match being made only where the files are read; so it is recorded there, at the file grain the elements already carry. A store_ relation because it is configuration rather than a reading of the file: a store_graph_schema_input entry, matched to a file the graph claims in store_graph_source.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application', 'expanded-directive-application', 'graphitron',
   'Every directive application the generator emits, the author''s and the ones macro expansion minted, at the coordinate it is applied to: one row per application.',
   'For example the @key(fields: "id") federation synthesises for a node type sits here beside the @table its author wrote on it, at the same grain and answering the same questions.',
   'The directive grain of the emitted population, which the anchors lacked: a macro that applies a directive rather than minting an element had nowhere to put it, so the federation key rule stayed a view outside the anchor and the renderer patched it in. Three sets, told apart by origin: what a document states, carried across from graphql_directive_application, what the configuration applies to what a document declares, and what a macro adds. Marked and swept by the reading after the element sweep, as the element anchors are, so an application the reading still derives keeps its row.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_arg', 'expanded-directive-application-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One argument of an application graphitron_directive_application holds: the application''s own key and the formal argument the value binds.',
   'For example the fields: "id" of a synthesised @key is one row, its value the literal an author would have typed.',
   'On graphql_directive_application_arg''s terms, so a reader renders every origin the same way.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_authored', 'expanded-directive-application', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application is the union of: an application an author wrote at a coordinate the emitted population holds, carried across whole.',
   'For example the @table on type Film @table(name: "film") is a row at Film here, with the position the author wrote it at.',
   'Restricted to the coordinates graphitron_element holds, which is the boundary that relation''s CHECK states: an application on an enum value or on the schema block stays in graphql_directive_application until an emitter wants the whole schema from this family.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_configured', 'expanded-directive-application', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application is the union of: an application the configuration adds to what a document declares.',
   'For example the @tag(name: "stable") a field takes from the entry its file was matched by.',
   'The loading rewrites apply it and no document states it, so it is a set of its own rather than authored; graphitron_configured_tag states the rule.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_minted', 'expanded-directive-application', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application is the union of: an application macro expansion adds.',
   'For example @key(fields: "id") on a node type in a federation-linked graph whose author declared no id key, or the @tag a minted Connection inherits from its carrier.',
   'One arm per macro that applies a directive: federation key synthesis, and the federation directives a minted type inherits. Numbered after every authored application of its directive at its coordinate, so the sets cannot collide.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_arg_authored', 'expanded-directive-application-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application_arg is the union of: an argument an author passed to an application the authored set carries.',
   'For example the name: "film" of type Film @table(name: "film") is one row here.',
   'On graphitron_directive_application_authored''s terms, at the argument grain.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_arg_configured', 'expanded-directive-application-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application_arg is the union of: an argument the configuration passes to an application it adds.',
   'For example the name: "stable" of a configured tag is one row here.',
   'On graphitron_directive_application_configured''s terms, at the argument grain.'),
  ('graphitron_directive_application_arg_minted', 'expanded-directive-application-argument', 'graphitron',
   'One of the three sets graphitron_directive_application_arg is the union of: an argument macro expansion passes to an application it adds.',
   'For example the fields: "id" and resolvable: true of a synthesised @key are two rows here.',
   'Stated as the SDL an author would have written, so the renderer needs no rule for what a macro means by its arguments.'),
  ('graphitron_configured_tag', 'configured-tag', 'graphitron',
   'The rule <schemaInput tag> states, as a relation: one row per @tag the configuration applies to an element of the emitted population.',
   'For example a field declared in a file whose entry carries tag stable, and which carries no @tag of its own, is one row naming stable.',
   'The one statement of the rule: the emitted schema applies these rows, and inheritance reads them, so a carrier''s tags and the ones its minted types inherit cannot disagree. Fields, input fields, arguments and enum values take their file''s tag; a union takes one per declaration site, each site judged on its own applications.'),
  ('graphitron_configured_tag_rule', 'configured-tag', 'graphitron',
   'One row the configured-tag rule computes, in the shape graphitron_configured_tag stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_configured_tag, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, on graphitron_type_backing_rule''s terms. Stored by MacroExpansion before the directive anchor reads it, because H2 evaluates a view again for every row joined to it and the federation chain joins these three to each other: on a consumer schema configuring 7734 tags over 654 coinages the directive upsert did not finish, and with the three stored it reads in a fifth of a second.'),
  ('graphitron_enum_value', 'expanded-enum-value', 'graphitron',
   'Every enum value the generator emits: one row per enum value coordinate.',
   'For example enum Rating { G PG } is the rows Rating.G and Rating.PG. Authored only, the macros minting no enum.',
   'The fourth part relation under graphitron_element, so a reader holding an enum value''s coordinate reaches its type and name by a join rather than by splitting the spelling, and the description the generator emits for it is stated beside the other three kinds''.'),
  ('graphitron_enum_value_authored', 'expanded-enum-value', 'graphitron',
   'The one set graphitron_enum_value is filled from: an enum value an author declared, with the note its file configures.',
   'For example the G in enum Rating { G PG }.',
   'On graphitron_type_authored''s terms. One set rather than two, nothing minting an enum value.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_link', 'federation-link', 'graphitron',
   'A graph opts in to Apollo Federation, and how: one row per federated graph.',
   'For example a schema extension applying @link with a federation url is one AUTHORED row naming that application, and a graph whose only opt-in is a <schemaInput tag> is one CONFIGURED row naming none.',
   'The one place the opt-in is stated. It came to a reader two ways, an authored link read from the decode and a link the composition synthesised for a configured tag, which the assembly recorded as a fact about the composition rather than about the corpus. Here both are a rule over what capture read: the authored link graphitron_ast_federation_link_entry decodes, the first by position where several do, and the configured one where none does and an input carries a tag.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_link_import', 'federation-link-import', 'graphitron',
   'One name a graph''s federation link imports, in the order it is written: one row per import.',
   'For example import: ["@key", {name: "@shareable", as: "@federatedShareable"}] gives two rows, and a third importing @tag where a tag is configured.',
   'What a link brings into scope, which is what decides whether a name the schema uses is declared. The authored imports come from the decode, and graphitron adds what it applies itself, its mint and the configured tags, as the link''s version allows, so its own output is never undeclared. The composition builds the link it hands federation-jvm from these rows, authored and configured alike.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_link_rule', 'federation-link', 'graphitron',
   'The rule graphitron_federation_link stores: one row per graph that opts in to federation, the way it opts in.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_federation_link, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the anchor step that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_federation_link_import_rule', 'federation-link-import', 'graphitron',
   'The rule graphitron_federation_link_import stores: one row per name a graph''s federation link imports.',
   'For example an authored link''s imports come across in the order written, and after them each directive graphitron applies that the link''s version lets it import.',
   'The rule, on graphitron_federation_link_rule''s terms. It reads the stored opt-in and the stored applications, which the anchor fills first, rather than their rules, so no view is evaluated inside another. The order is the list''s as written, reached from the import through the value it is and the argument holding it to the application.'),
  ('graphitron_carrier_directive', 'carrier-directive', 'graphitron',
   'The federation directives on the fields that coined a minted type: one row per @tag or @shareable a carrier carries, authored or configured.',
   'For example films: [Film!]! @asConnection @tag(name: "public") gives QueryFilmsConnection a row naming the public tag on Query.films.',
   'The input to inheritance and to the narrowing finding, stated once so both read one population. Only types the mint did not stand down for, an author''s declaration inheriting nothing.'),
  ('graphitron_carrier_directive_rule', 'carrier-directive', 'graphitron',
   'One row the carrier-directive rule computes, in the shape graphitron_carrier_directive stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_carrier_directive, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, on graphitron_type_backing_rule''s terms. Stored by MacroExpansion before the directive anchor reads it, because H2 evaluates a view again for every row joined to it and the federation chain joins these three to each other: on a consumer schema configuring 7734 tags over 654 coinages the directive upsert did not finish, and with the three stored it reads in a fifth of a second.'),
  ('graphitron_inherited_directive', 'inherited-directive', 'graphitron',
   'The federation directives a minted type inherits from its carriers: the tags every carrier carries and @shareable when any carrier is.',
   'For example a PageInfo two carriers share carries only the tag both carry, and is shareable because one of them is.',
   'Apollo''s contract rule read over the carriers: a tag on a type also belongs on every field returning it, so the tags are the intersection, a relational division over type, carrier and tag. @shareable is a composition requirement rather than a filter, so it is the union.'),
  ('graphitron_argument', 'expanded-argument', 'graphitron',
   'Every field argument the generator works with, the author''s and the ones macro expansion minted: one row per argument coordinate, carrying the wrapping columns graphql_argument carries.',
   'For example the first and after arguments a connection carrier gets when its author wrote no pagination argument sit here, where graphql_argument holds only what the document declares and therefore holds no row for them.',
   'The argument grain, and the one this family gained rather than inherited. The connection expansion mints two arguments on a carrier whose author wrote no pagination argument, and until this relation the store recorded nothing about them: the expansion has two halves in two modules, one writing facts and one building schema objects, and only the second knew the arguments existed. So an argument the generator emits was reachable from the emitted schema and from no relation, which is the gap this closes. Total over the emitted population on graphitron_type''s terms.'),
  ('graphitron_type_member', 'type-member', 'graphitron',
   'A member of a polymorphic type: one row per union or interface and each type it admits.',
   'For example Searchable = Film | Language draws Film and Language under Searchable, and type Language implements Node draws Language under Node.',
   'The polymorphic subtype of graphitron_type: both ends key into the supertype with their kinds pinned, so a member is a type and a container a union or an interface by the database''s own key. Stored, beside the types macro expansion writes, so a rule asking a container''s members joins one table on its key rather than the graphql_poly_member view, a union of two transcription relations a rule view may not read. What a member is bound to, a table or none, is each reader''s join to graphitron_tabletype, so no caller''s filter is baked into the grain.'),
  ('graphitron_type_member_rule', 'type-member', 'graphitron',
   'One row the type member rule computes, in the shape graphitron_type_member stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_type_member, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_scalar_class', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'The Java class a scalar''s values arrive as: one row per scalar whose class is known.',
   'For example Int arrives as java.lang.Integer, and scalar Money @scalarType(scalar: "com.example.Scalars.MONEY") as the input type of the constant''s Coercing.',
   'A subtype of graphitron_type pinned to SCALAR. The built-in classes and the @scalarType resolution were spelled out in three rules, each re-deriving them on every evaluation and none scoping the constant to the graph''s classpath entries; stored here, by the gatherer that holds the document, the catalog of constants and the graph''s sources, the rules join one keyed table. The class graphql-java delivers is the fact: what a parameter of a primitive or another type accepts stays each reader''s rule, so no reader''s leniency becomes the fact. A scalar whose class is unknown, or whose constant two entries coerce differently, has no row.'),
  ('graphitron_scalar_class_rule', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One row the scalar class rule computes, in the shape graphitron_scalar_class stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_scalar_class, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_input_type', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'The type an argument or input field occurrence takes, and the Java class its values arrive as: one row per such occurrence.',
   'For example Query.films(filter:) takes the input object FilmFilter and has no class, and Query.films(filter.releaseYear:) takes Int, arriving as java.lang.Integer.',
   'What a call''s parameter binding asks of every element it could bind: its type, the type''s kind, whether it is a list and the class it arrives as. The condition and producer parameter rules each derived it inline from the declared argument or field, the type and the scalar''s class, and read that derivation inside correlated subqueries, so it was evaluated once per probing row; on a large consumer schema that was most of a six-second rule evaluated once per occurrence depth. Written by the occurrences stage beside the occurrences it keys into, ahead of the depth loop that reads it. Whether a search may enter an input object, never through a list, is the reader''s predicate over two columns of the row.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_input_type_rule', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'One row the occurrence input type rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_input_type stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrences stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_occurrence_input_type, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_edge', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'An edge of a Relay connection and the item its node names: one row per edge type.',
   'For example FilmsEdge, whose node field names Film, draws one row naming Film, however many connections page through it.',
   'A subtype of graphitron_type pinned to OBJECT, so an edge is a type of its kind by the database''s own key. Structural and not directive-driven: an edge is an object some connection''s edges field names and that declares node, whether @asConnection minted it or the author wrote it. The item is stated once, here, and a connection reaches it through its edge, so two connections sharing an edge cannot disagree about what it carries. A name and not a binding: whether the item is bound to a table is graphitron_tabletype''s answer.'),
  ('graphitron_edge_rule', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One row the edge rule computes, in the shape graphitron_edge stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_edge, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_connection', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'A Relay connection type and the edge it pages through: one row per type whose shape is a connection.',
   'For example a FilmsConnection declaring edges: [FilmsEdge] draws one row naming FilmsEdge, and a type that is not a connection draws none, so a reader left-joins this rather than joining it.',
   'A subtype of graphitron_type pinned to OBJECT, keyed into graphitron_edge for what it pages. Structural and not directive-driven: a connection synthesised from @asConnection and one the author wrote out have the same shape and so the same row. The shape test is the spec''s own and its names literal, edges then an OBJECT edge then node; field names being unique within a type, it never fans out. Written by macro expansion beside the types it reads, ahead of the field targets that unwrap it, so no rule reads the shape as a view or re-spells it as anti-joins.'),
  ('graphitron_connection_rule', 'expanded-type', 'graphitron',
   'One row the connection rule computes, in the shape graphitron_connection stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_connection, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_field_position', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A field that fills a position of the connection shape: one row per field.',
   'For example FilmsConnection.totalCount fills TOTAL_COUNT, and PageInfo.endCursor fills END_CURSOR however many connections share PageInfo; a field filling no position has no row.',
   'Which position of the connection shape a field fills, stated once at the field''s grain so a rule asking whether a field is a connection''s totalCount, facets or page info joins a key instead of spelling the field''s name. A connection''s and its edge''s fields fill the CONNECTION and EDGE positions, and the fields of the type a connection''s pageInfo field names fill the PAGE_INFO ones; a type shared by several connections fills each position once. Recognition of the shape itself is graphitron_connection''s and graphitron_edge''s; this states only the positions a recognised type''s fields fill.'),
  ('graphitron_field_position_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the field position rule computes, in the shape graphitron_field_position stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the macro-expansion stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_field_position, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphql_element_field', 'element-field-site', 'graphql-ast',
   'The field a schema element sits on, for the element kinds whose coordinate names one: one row per field coordinate, output and input alike, and one per field-argument coordinate, carrying the parts the spelling is built from.',
   'For example Query.films draws a row naming the type and the field, and Query.films(filter:) draws another naming those and the argument, so a reader holding either spelling reaches the field with one join.',
   'What a reader joins when it holds a coordinate and needs the field, which before this was two left joins and a COALESCE spelled once per reader, and wrongly available to a reader that forgot the second arm. Named-type and enum-value elements are absent and that is the population rather than a gap: neither sits on a field, so a reader asking this question of one is asking something with no answer and gets no row instead of a half-populated one. The argument component is NULL on a field coordinate, which is the one nullness here and is the difference between the two arms rather than a fact withheld. Not columns on graphql_element itself, because a type coordinate has no field and an enum value''s name is not one, so carrying them up would put two nullnesses on the supertype to serve half its subtypes. It reads as a reconstruction and is not one: the supertype exists and every member points at it, so this unions a hierarchy that is already written rather than standing in for one nobody wrote, which is the distinction SupertypeSignatureGateTest draws before it counts a union as debt.'),
  ('graphitron_tabletype', 'bound-table-type', 'graphitron',
   'A graph type that resolved to exactly one catalog table: one row per type whose @table spelling the catalog answered unambiguously.',
   'For example a Film type over @table(name: "film") draws a row naming the film table in the schema that declares it, while a spelling two schemas both declare draws none and is found by anti-join against the entry the author wrote.',
   'The settled half of the type-to-table binding, and the whole of the relation rather than a column beside an unsettled one. A spelling two schemas both declare resolves to two tables and to no row here; which types those are is the anti-join against the @table decode, so ambiguity stays answerable and is answered where the author''s writing lives. Storing both populations with a count is what the relation this replaces does, and the cost of that is measurable: eleven of its readers spell the same candidates = 1 predicate and four forget to, which on a multi-schema catalog multiplies their rows silently. The key is the graph and the type, and it is the settledness itself; a relation admitting an ambiguous binding could not carry it. It carries a foreign key into sql_table too, cascading on delete, and is the first graph-keyed relation here to point into the source-keyed catalog: the cascade is what makes that sound, a shared source being recrawled whole and taking the bindings that named it with it, where without one it could not be recrawled at all while a graph still referenced it. Written as a stage of the graphitron gatherer because both sides are already in hand there: the catalog crawler runs before it by declared dependency and the @table decode is the gatherer''s own, one flush earlier. The type-name fallback and the exclusion of the three root operation types are resolution rules and live here, the decode being what the author wrote and nothing more.'),
  ('graphitron_node', 'node-type', 'graphitron',
   'Which of a graph''s types are nodes, with the wire type id each answers to: one row per node type, from the authored population and the published one alike.',
   'For example a Customer carrying @node over @table draws a row, a type merely implementing Node over a table whose class publishes __NODE_TYPE_ID draws one beside it, and a @node on a type with no @table draws none.',
   'Nodehood as a resolved fact rather than a membership view over two arms, which is the difference between what an author wrote and what took effect. @node only takes effect on a type that also carries @table, so the table binding is the precondition on both arms and not a filter a reader remembers; a @node on an unbound type stays in the decode, and the anti-join between the two is where an author is told the directive did nothing. The type id rides along because it is total over this population and over no other: three tiers answer, the author''s declaration, the backing class''s published constant and the type''s own name, and the last always answers, so a reader encoding an id needs no fallback and the column needs no nullness. Which tier answered is a column because a diagnostic about two types colliding on one id has to say where each came from. The key columns are not here and that is deliberate: an author may declare the id and leave the columns to the catalog or the reverse, so the two axes resolve separately and a single declared-versus-inferred flag over the node would be wrong for half the schemas that have one. Written as a stage of the graphitron gatherer after the binding it stands on, both sources being in hand there: the catalog crawler has flushed and the @node decode is the gatherer''s own.'),
  ('graphitron_node_table', 'node-table', 'graphitron',
   'A table at least one node of a graph declares itself over: one row per graph and table, with how many nodes do and, where one alone does, which.',
   'For example a film table only Film is bound to draws (film, 1, Film), and one Film and FilmAlias are both bound to draws (film, 2) with no node.',
   'The one node over a table stated once: the node id binding''s inferring rules, the target defects and the outbox''s node table each counted the nodes over a table themselves and probed for a second. Keyed by the table, which no reference it reads covers, so it is stored rather than a view the rules would read.'),
  ('graphitron_node_table_rule', 'node-table', 'graphitron',
   'A table at least one node of a graph declares itself over: one row per graph and table, with how many nodes do and, where one alone does, which, in the shape graphitron_node_table stores.',
   'For example a film table only Film is bound to draws (film, 1, Film).',
   'The rule the stored relation is written from, after the nodes it counts.'),
  ('graphitron_key_field_binding', 'key-field', 'graphitron',
   'One field a federation key selects on a table-bound object type, authored or synthesised, and how it binds at that type''s own table.',
   'For example @key(fields: "tenantId sku") on a Product draws two COLUMN rows, the @key(fields: "id") synthesised for a node type one NODE_ID row, or a COLUMN row where its id carries @field, and the @key(fields: "voucher") an @accept mints one VOUCHER row.',
   'The one fact an entity key alternative needs that no other relation states: which of the type''s occurrence bindings each key field resolves to at the entity''s table. Everything else an alternative carries, the column, the node''s type id and key columns, the resolvability, is stated elsewhere, so only the binding is stored and a reader composes the rest from the relations that state it. Keyed by the key''s directive application and the field''s place in the parse, since a key on an extension can name a field declared in another file; referencing the application, the occurrence binding (which carries the kind) and the type''s table, so a row is a binding at the type''s own table by construction. A selection with no row binds nothing there, which is the entity key stage''s refusal.'),
  ('graphitron_accept_key', 'accept-key', 'graphitron',
   'The authored federation key one @accept''s vouchers decode to.',
   'For example voucher: String! @accept(scope: "results", claims: "ownerId") on a VgsResultat whose one authored key is @key(fields: "id") gives one row naming that key.',
   'The one fact the voucher alternative needs beyond how the minted key''s field binds, which graphitron_key_field_binding states as a VOUCHER row: which authored key the lookup part decodes to, decided once from key: or the type''s one authored resolvable key. The alternative''s columns are that key''s, read through its own key field bindings, so nothing about the columns is restated here. Keyed by the @accept application, referencing the key''s application, so a decode onto a key that is gone cascades away. An @accept no key answers draws no row, which is the refusal.'),
  ('graphitron_accept_key_rule', 'accept-key', 'graphitron',
   'One accept key the rule computes, in the shape graphitron_accept_key stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the entity key stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_accept_key, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_key_field_binding_rule', 'key-field', 'graphitron',
   'One key field binding the entity key rule computes, in the shape graphitron_key_field_binding stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the entity key stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_key_field_binding, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_key', 'minted-key', 'graphitron',
   'One federation key a graph mints on one of its table-bound types, with the rule that minted it.',
   'For example a node type of a federation-linked graph whose author wrote no id key carries one NODE row, fields id and resolvable.',
   'Which keys a macro adds was a membership rule inside the minted application view, and the argument view read that view and hardcoded what every minted key said, because the entity rule was the only one. A relation of its own lets more than one rule mint a key, each a role with its own field set and resolvability, and lets both sets that render the application read the same row, so no view reads another and no argument is assumed. Decided upstream of the emitted population, from graphitron_node, the federation link and the authored keys, so the stage that writes it runs before emitted; the application sets join graphitron_element as they did, so a key is rendered only on a type the graph emits. The ordinal is stated here for the same reason: the application and its arguments name one @key between them.'),
  ('graphitron_minted_key_rule', 'minted-key', 'graphitron',
   'One key the minting rules compute, in the shape graphitron_minted_key stores: the rules themselves, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the minted-keys stage writes this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_minted_key, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_node_keycolumn', 'node-key-position', 'graphitron',
   'A node type''s key columns, in key order and resolved against the table it is bound to: one row per position, naming a column the catalog has.',
   'For example a Customer over the customer table draws its primary key columns, an author pinning keyColumns: ["email"] draws that column instead, and a pinned name the table does not have draws nothing at all.',
   'Half of a node''s identity as a resolved fact, beside graphitron_node which carries the other. Three populations answer and one wins: what the author pinned, what the bound table''s generated class publishes, and the table''s own primary key. Standing on the nodehood anchor is what makes resolution possible at all. The relation this replaces reaches the pinned tier through a decode keyed by graph and type alone, so that tier had no table to resolve against and forwarded the authored spelling untouched; a node is table bound unambiguously by construction, so the table is in hand and the column is a reference rather than a name. Both authored tiers fold to resolve, against the column''s SQL name and its generated field name alike, and neither spelling survives into the row. A tier that applies and fails to resolve yields nothing rather than falling through: an author who pinned a column the table does not have has made an error, and encoding ids against the primary key instead would publish a wire format nobody asked for, silently. The same holds within a tier, a key list with a hole decoding values into the wrong positions. The bound table rides along in three columns so the row can reference both the type''s binding and the column, which together make a row naming another table''s column impossible. The order is why this is a relation rather than a list: an id encoded against one order and decoded against another finds the wrong row rather than failing.'),
  ('graphitron_node_legacy_keycolumn', 'node-legacy-key-position', 'graphitron',
   'One position of the legacy key shape a wire type id answers to in a graph, resolved against the table the type id''s ids are rows of.',
   'For example type id RentalItem with keyColumns ["rental_id"] and legacyKeyColumns ["customer_id", "rental_id", "rental_date"] draws three rows, rental_id at position 1 with current position 0.',
   'A legacy key shape is the column list ids of a type were encoded from before its key was narrowed, and the generated runtime converts such an id to the current shape before reading it. It is a fact of the wire type id rather than of a GraphQL type, because the conversion runs where an id is decoded and knows only the type id it expects; keying it so also reaches the decode callers no type-level fact reaches. One shape per type id is the primary key, no repeated column the unique key beside it. A shape is anchored only where every rule holds, all positions or none: a shape that misses a current key column, reorders the current key, names a column twice or the table lacks, disagrees between the schema and the catalog, or belongs to a type id two tables or two current keys answer to, is absent here and present as a defect.'),
  ('graphitron_node_legacy_key_defect', 'node-legacy-key-defect', 'graphitron',
   'One rule a table-bound type''s legacy key shape breaks, in one graph, which is why the shape its type id answers to is not anchored.',
   'For example an InventoryItem whose legacyKeyColumns leave out its current key column inventory_id gives one NODE_LEGACY_KEY_MISSES_KEY_COLUMN row whose detail is inventory_id.',
   'The negative half of the stage that writes graphitron_node_legacy_keycolumn, written by the same evaluation of the rules so the two halves cannot disagree: a type id gets a shape exactly when none of the types answering to it has a row here. The rule view reads these rows to place each defect at the @node or @table the author edits, rather than restating the rules in SQL beside the stage that already applied them.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_key', 'path-element-key-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'A foreign key a path element names: one row per element and candidate key.',
   'For example {key: "film_actor_actor_id_fkey"} names public.film_actor''s key to actor, and an unqualified {key: "note_event_fk"} names both schemas'' note_event_fk.',
   'What an element names, resolved from its spelling once so spelling lives only in the entry, and in the author''s terms, a key being what the author named. One row per candidate rather than a decline: the generator narrows a name two schemas share by the table its walk stands at, so the walk narrows here too, and none or several left is a defect at the step. Keyed by the element, so it is the same at every site.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_key_rule', 'path-element-key-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the path element key rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_element_key stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_path_element_key, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_primary_key', 'path-element-primary-key-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'A table''s own primary key a path element names: one row per element whose key: spelling names a table''s primary key, per candidate key.',
   'For example {key: "film_pkey"} names public.film''s primary key, which joins each film row to itself.',
   'The key: spelling a field writes to read its parent''s own row through a @table return type on the same table: the identity join. A sibling of graphitron_path_element_key rather than a widening of it, because that relation''s foreign key to sql_referential_constraint is the integrity rule that makes its rows foreign keys, and a kind column would trade it away. Keyed by the element and the table, the constraint name being sql_primary_key''s to say. Whether the walk may ride it is the walk''s question: only as a path''s only element on a field reading one row.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_primary_key_rule', 'path-element-primary-key-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the path element primary key rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_element_primary_key stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_path_element_primary_key, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_table', 'path-element-table-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'A table a path element names: one row per element and candidate table.',
   'For example {table: "film_actor"} names public.film_actor, and {table: "multischema_a.note"} names that schema''s note alone.',
   'What an element names, resolved from its spelling once and keyed by the element rather than by the spelling as graphitron_spelled_table is, so a reader starts from the element it holds. One row per candidate; the walk takes the one key from where it stands to the named table, and an element naming no table or several is a defect.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_table_rule', 'path-element-table-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the path element table rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_element_table stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_path_element_table, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_routine_result', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'Where a field''s path ends on a @routine: one row per field whose last written element is a @routine naming one routine, with the function whose result the rows of the type it returns are.',
   'For example Actor.films over @routine(name: "films_for_actor") returns ActorFilm, whose rows are films_for_actor''s result; a mutation''s @routine is a write and draws no row.',
   'A @routine binds no table, so what such a type''s rows are is a fact about the field that calls the routine, stated once here from the written path for every reader that asks where the type is read: the result rule, the source table, the column claims. It needs no walk, the function being where any path ending on it lands. Stored, because those readers ask it as a probe per type, which a view answers by evaluating its whole rule again.'),
  ('graphitron_routine_result_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the routine result rule computes, in the shape graphitron_routine_result stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_routine_result, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_routine', 'path-element-routine-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'A routine a path element names: one row per @routine element and candidate routine with a generated call.',
   'For example @routine(name: "films_for_actor") names public.films_for_actor, whose call is the Routines method filmsForActor.',
   'What a @routine element names, resolved from its spelling once and keyed by the element, as a table element is in graphitron_path_element_table, so every reader of a path starts from the element it holds. One row per candidate; none or several is a defect at the element.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_routine_rule', 'path-element-routine-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the path element routine rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_element_routine stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_path_element_routine, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_method', 'path-element-method-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'A condition method a path element names: one row per element and candidate method.',
   'For example {condition: {className: "no.example.Conditions", method: "liveActor"}} names that class''s liveActor, one row per overload.',
   'What an element names, resolved once against the condition methods the graph''s classpath declares. A method alone is the steps join, its signature saying which tables it joins; beside a key or a table it is a filter on that step. One row per overload, the generator choosing among them by the arguments it maps.'),
  ('graphitron_path_element_method_rule', 'path-element-method-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'One row the path element method rule computes, in the shape graphitron_path_element_method stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_path_element_method, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_spelled_table', 'spelled-table-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'How a written table name resolves against the catalog census: one row per candidate table, keyed on the spelling itself rather than on any one site that wrote it.',
   'For example a schema writing @table(name: "film") and a @reference path element naming film draws one row between them, and a name two schemas both declare draws two rows saying two candidates.',
   'The resolution does not vary by site, so one relation answers for all of them: @table(name:), a @reference path element''s table, its argument-site and @referenceFor siblings, @mutation''s delete target and @routine(name:) all name a table the same way, and a spelling written at five coordinates is one fact and one row. A spelling arrives already split, capture having written the namespace half and the name half beside the value, so both sides of both comparisons are stored folded columns and the match is an equality an index can serve rather than a fold computed per candidate row; an unqualified spelling, whose namespace half is null, matches on its name half alone. The catalog side scopes through the graph''s own sources, so a sibling graph''s tables never resolve here. A routine name is a table spelling too, jOOQ modelling a function result as a catalog table, and what makes a resolved row a function is sql_table.table_type, which a reader meaning that form filters on. Ambiguity is rows and never a decline: a name two schemas both declare is two rows and the count says how many, leaving the reading to the reader. Written once per capture by a stage of the graphitron gatherer where it used to be refilled by the register, the rule stated once and still in SQL.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'One occurrence of an element, a use of a declared field, argument or input field reached along a chain: one row per nested coordinate.',
   'For example Emne.navn.en is en on the type Emne.navn returns, which has no table of its own, and Query.films(filter.cast.firstName:) an input field under the argument filter.',
   'A field of a type with no table of its own, an argument and an input field mean something different under each parent: which table they read, where their path starts, what their parent holds. Stated once per parent chain at a coordinate, every fact that depends on the parent has one row per occurrence, and a reader reads it without choosing among the tables a declaration-keyed row carries. Stored rather than a view because its population is a walk with a first-visit guard over a graph that can cycle, which a view cannot state safely, and because the parent-dependent relations key into it.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_overridden', 'occurrence-overridden', 'graphitron',
   'An occurrence at or under a @condition written with override: true, whose generated predicates that condition stands in for: one row per occurrence carrying one, and per argument or input-field occurrence under one.',
   'For example Query.films(filter:) @condition(..., override: true) on the field draws rows for Query.films, Query.films(filter:) and every input field under filter, and none for a field nested under Query.films.',
   'What an override suppresses, stated once at the occurrence: the list element defects read it, so a list an override owns is not compared as rows, and the condition reader can drop the terms it suppresses without walking parents. Read off the override: written on the directive application, not off graphitron_occurrence_condition, whose copy of it is carried for the transition. Written a depth at a time, an occurrence''s row read off its parent''s, by a stage of its own before the depths.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_overridden_rule', 'occurrence-overridden', 'graphitron',
   'One row the overridden rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_overridden stores: the rule itself, evaluated a depth at a time rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-overrides stage inserts this view''s rows at one depth into graphitron_occurrence_overridden, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_list_element', 'occurrence-list-element', 'graphitron',
   'An input field read once per element of a list above it: one row per input-field occurrence and each argument or input-field occurrence above it whose type is a list.',
   'For example Query.filmActorsByPairs(filter.pairs.actorId:) is read once per element of Query.filmActorsByPairs(filter.pairs:), and Query.filmsByFilter(filter.title:) draws no row.',
   'A filter read through a list of input objects compares each element as one row, so its reader groups the element fields under their list, and a refusal of a field under two lists asks the same question. Stored at the occurrence pair, written a depth at a time from the parent''s rows, so neither reader rebuilds an occurrence''s chain to find the list above it, in SQL by recursion or in code by walking parents.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_list_element_rule', 'occurrence-list-element', 'graphitron',
   'One row the list element rule computes, in the shape graphitron_occurrence_list_element stores: the rule itself, evaluated a depth at a time rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the occurrence-lists stage inserts this view''s rows at one depth into graphitron_occurrence_list_element, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'An input field is read per element of its parent where the parent is a list, and of every list its parent is read per element of, which are the depth above''s stored rows, so each arm is one join and the rule never recurses. Kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the table and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_start_rule', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'One occurrence a chain starts at, in the shape graphitron_occurrence stores at depth 0: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example Film.title on the table-bound Film, and Mutation.createFilm on the root.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads.'),
  ('graphitron_occurrence_step_rule', 'graph-occurrence', 'graphitron',
   'One occurrence one step below an occurrence this reading wrote, before the first-visit guard: the rule the writer applies a depth at a time.',
   'For example Emne.navn.en under Emne.navn, Query.films(filter:) under Query.films, and Query.films(filter.cast:) under Query.films(filter:).',
   'The step, kept in the catalog so what it reads is visible to the gates; the writer applies it one depth at a time and adds the first-visit guard, which needs the types already on the chain and so a walk up it that a view over a cyclic graph cannot state safely.'),
  ('graphitron_fetcher', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'An output field''s fetcher, and whether it produces a result or reads one: one row per field.',
   'For example Query.films selects and so produces, and Film.title reads a column of the row its parent produced.',
   'The datafetcher axis every emitter dispatches on: a field runs something or reads what something ran, and a field neither side explains is an absent subtype row a gate can find. The supertype the fetcher''s facts reference, so the source, the target and the operations key to it rather than each keying the field afresh. Written in the graphitron anchor phase.'),
  ('graphitron_producer', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A field whose fetcher runs something, and what kind of thing it runs: one row per field.',
   'For example Query.films with nothing written is a SELECT, Mutation.addFilm @mutation(typeName: INSERT) an INSERT, and the totalCount of the connection a field returns a COUNT over that field''s statement.',
   'The payload, the carrier and the error channel are facts about a producer, and each was restated by a relation that folded the producer away. Stated once here, at the field, from what was written and where; the rule is graphitron_producer_rule and the anchor inserts its rows.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_write', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'What a write producer writes: its @mutation application, the table it mutates and, for an identifying write, how many rows one input may match; one row per write whose table resolved.',
   'For example Mutation.dropFilms @mutation(typeName: DELETE, multiRow: true, table: "film") deletes from public.film, matching MANY.',
   'A produced type and a mutated type are two facts: a delete mutates a table and produces ids. The mutated table is stated here once, by the precedence the generator writes by, so no reader derives it again from the return or the spelling.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_routine', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'What a routine producer calls: its first @routine application and the database routine that names; one row per routine producer whose routine resolved to one callable.',
   'For example Query.filmsForActor @routine(name: "films_for_actor") calls public.films_for_actor.',
   'The routine a producer calls, resolved once at the field, where the routine views each re-resolved the spelling. Only the first @routine makes the producer; the applications after it are the reference path''s.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_service', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'What a service producer calls: the reference its @service wrote and the one method that resolves to; one row per service producer whose reference resolved.',
   'For example Query.uppercased @service(service: {className: "FilmService", method: "titleUppercase"}) calls FilmService.titleUppercase.',
   'The method a producer calls, keyed to the producer rather than to the reference, so a reader holding a field reaches the method in one join.'),
  ('graphitron_errors_field', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A field whose value is a list of errors: one row per nullable list of a union or an interface whose every member is @error, or of one @error type itself.',
   'For example FilmLookupPayload.errors: [FilmLookupError], the union of FilmLookupNotFound, FilmLookupInvalid and FilmLookupClientFacing.',
   'A fact of the field, stated once whoever returns its type: the outcome a producer catches into reads it, and so do the defects a misshapen one raises.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_outcome', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A producer whose result is an outcome, and the field its errors arrive at: one row per service, write or routine whose target declares exactly one errors field.',
   'For example Query.filmLookup @service returning FilmLookupPayload, whose one errors field is errors: [FilmLookupError], catches what the service throws and hands it to errors.',
   'Catching is the producer''s, at a root or below one: an errors field on a type no producer returns is read off the value like any other. Stated once at the producer, where the payload, the carrier and the error channel each restated it from the type.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_write_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the write rule computes, in the shape graphitron_producer_write stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_producer_write, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_routine_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the routine rule computes, in the shape graphitron_producer_routine stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_producer_routine, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_service_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the service rule computes, in the shape graphitron_producer_service stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_producer_service, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_errors_field_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the errors field rule computes, in the shape graphitron_errors_field stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_errors_field, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_outcome_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the outcome rule computes, in the shape graphitron_producer_outcome stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_producer_outcome, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_result', 'type-result', 'graphitron',
   'A type holds a result of this shape: one row per type and shape.',
   'For example Film @table(name: "film") holds a ROW, a directiveless type nested under one of its fields holds a ROW of film too, and the connection a paginated field returns holds an OBJECT its edges, nodes and page info read.',
   'The closure every fetcher stands on: a result flows down from the producer that made it, through the readers that hand it on, to every type it reaches, and its shape is how a field of the type is fetched. Filled in the graphitron anchor by merging graphitron_result_rule until a round adds nothing; stored, as the rule restates itself from these rows each round.'),
  ('graphitron_result_table', 'result-table', 'graphitron',
   'A ROW a type holds is a row of this table: one row per type and table.',
   'For example a type nested under Film.details holds film''s rows, and a type nested under two tables'' fields holds both.',
   'Where a row result comes from, so a nested type''s fields know the table they read and a nested path has its source.'),
  ('graphitron_result_pivot', 'result-pivot', 'graphitron',
   'A ROW a type holds is the row a @pivot field''s aggregate makes: one row per type and pivot field.',
   'For example Film.translations @pivot(on: "lang_code", value: "title_txt") makes Translations a row with one column per slot.',
   'A pivot''s row is neither a table''s nor nested in its parent''s: each slot is an aggregate over the rows at the end of the pivot field''s path, filtered to the slot''s token.'),
  ('graphitron_result_class', 'result-class', 'graphitron',
   'An OBJECT a type holds is an instance of this class: one row per type and class.',
   'For example a type a @service returns its own record for holds that record, and a carrier graphitron builds, a connection or a write''s payload, has no row.',
   'The class an object result is an instance of, read off graphitron_type_backing for the types that hold one, so a reader holding a type reaches the class in one join.'),
  ('graphitron_result_rule', 'type-result', 'graphitron',
   'One round of the result rule: the results the producers seed and the ones a reader hands on from what graphitron_result already holds, in the shape graphitron_result and its two row subtypes store.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor merges this view''s rows into graphitron_result until a round adds nothing, which is the closure.',
   'The rule as a view beside its tables, so what the closure stores stays checkable against one round of it.'),
  ('graphitron_reader', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A field whose fetcher reads from the result its parent holds, and how: one row per field.',
   'For example Film.title reads a COLUMN of film''s row, and a connection''s edges reads its page through an ACCESSOR.',
   'The other side of the fetcher: a field that runs nothing reads the result its parent holds, and how it reads is a column, an expression or a nested row of a ROW, or an accessor on an OBJECT.'),
  ('graphitron_reader_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the reader rule computes, in the shape graphitron_reader stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_reader, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_claim', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'One claim an element makes, by the element whose fact implies it: one row per element, kind and claimer.',
   'For example Query.filmsByKeys with @lookupKey on two arguments has two LOOKUP rows, one claimed by each argument, and a field carrying both @service and @routine has a SERVICE and a ROUTINE row.',
   'Every family''s claims in one relation, so a reader asking what an element claims reads one place and the vocabulary is stated once. Keyed by what is claimed and by whom, not by the directive that claims it, so another spelling of the same claim changes no row. The priority and the stage are the claiming stage''s to state; the relation decides nothing, each reader picking its own winner. Families with something to add beside the claim keep it in a subtype keyed by the claim.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_claim', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'One producer claim a field has: one row per field, kind and claimer.',
   'For example a field carrying both @service and @routine has two rows of two kinds, which is the conflict the entry defects report, and a field claiming nothing has none.',
   'The producer family of graphitron_claim, under the name its readers spell. A plain filter, so a reader''s graph reaches the claims'' key; a reader asking whether a field claims one kind or two counts distinct kinds.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_claim_rule', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'One producer claim the rule computes, in the shape graphitron_claim stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_claim, at the priority and under the stage ProducerClaims claims.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_binding_claim', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'What one binding claim names: one row per binding claim in graphitron_claim.',
   'For example a field carrying @field(name: "title") has a COLUMN row naming title, and an input field carrying @nodeId(typeName: "Film") @field(name: "film") a NODE_ID row naming Film with film as its target.',
   'The binding family''s subtype of graphitron_claim, keyed by the claim and cascading from it, so the binding rules read what a claim names without the supertype. A @field(name:) beside a @nodeId is that claim''s target and no claim of its own: under a table it contradicts the node''s key and under a class it names the member the decoded value goes to, which is the occurrence''s reader to judge.'),
  ('graphitron_binding_claim_container', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'A @nodeId naming an interface or union that is no node type itself: one row per NODE_ID binding claim naming one, the claim naming the type.',
   'For example an argument carrying @nodeId(typeName: "AddressOccupant"), a union of Customer and Staff, has a row, and one naming Film has none.',
   'Whether an id is of one node type or of several is asked by the decode shape, the container defects and the member rule, each of which joined the claim, the type and the node again and spelled it slightly differently; stated once here, at the claim it details, cascading with it.'),
  ('graphitron_binding_claim_container_rule', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'A @nodeId naming an interface or union that is no node type itself: one row per NODE_ID binding claim naming one, the claim naming the type, in the shape graphitron_binding_claim_container stores.',
   'For example an argument carrying @nodeId(typeName: "AddressOccupant"), a union of Customer and Staff, has a row, and one naming Film has none.',
   'The rule graphitron_binding_claim_container is written from, after the claims it details and the nodes it rules out.'),
  ('graphitron_binding_claim_rule', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'One binding claim the rule computes, with what it names: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_claim and graphitron_binding_claim, at the priority and under the stage BindingClaims claims.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_type_claim_rule', 'claim', 'graphitron',
   'One type claim the rule computes, in the shape graphitron_claim stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_claim, at the priority and under the stage TypeClaims claims.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, on graphitron_argmapping_site_rule''s terms: the table is what readers join, and the rule stays runnable beside it.'),
  ('graphitron_producer_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the producer rule computes, in the shape graphitron_producer stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the graphitron anchor inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_producer, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule as a view beside its table, so an EXCEPT between the two stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('graphitron_field_target', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'A field''s target type, unwrapped from a connection where the field returns one, with the connection beside it: one row per field.',
   'For example films: [Film!]! @asConnection has the target Film and the wrapper the macro minted, and title: String has the target String and no wrapper.',
   'A field has a target type, and a target type is what is bound to a table. Five rules spelled the same COALESCE over the synthesis record, and all five navigated a connection only where the generator had built it, so an author-declared connection fell through to its wrapper and bound nothing. Stated once here, the wrapper''s name and not a flag, so a reader asking about the connection joins it. Total over graphitron_field, minted fields and scalar returns included, so a reader joins this rather than left-joining it. Kind, participants and table are each a join on the target''s name rather than columns here: graphql_type, graphql_poly_member and graphitron_tabletype. A mutation''s target is its payload type; the refetch is the outcome''s LOOKUP field and that field''s own target. Stored rather than left to its rule view because readers join on the target''s name, which as a column an index can lead with.'),
  ('graphitron_field_target_rule', 'graph-field', 'graphitron',
   'One row the field target rule computes, in the shape graphitron_field_target stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_field_target, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it, which is a performance bug rather than a wrong answer.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the stage that runs it, so it is stated once in SQL where ViewReferences and the gates reading stored definitions can see what it reads, and so an EXCEPT between the target and this view stays runnable for as long as both exist.'),
  ('intent_type_domain', 'graph-type', 'sdl',
   'The classification domain''s type members: every named type, of every kind, the generator''s intended traversal reaches from its seeds.',
   'For example a graph whose Query.films returns Film draws a row for Query and one for Film, and a type nothing reachable names draws none.',
   'Named for the assertion, not the graph operation, because the seeds are generator policy rather than neutral schema reachability: root operation bindings, @node carriers, types declaring implements Node, authored @key carriers, and the argument types of directive definitions that survive into the emitted schema, where survivorship is read from each definition''s own source (graphitron''s bundled directive vocabulary is build-time only) rather than from a name set held in Java, so the seeds are a function of the captured document throughout. Every seed is an SDL fact, which is what makes this a one-corpus derivation: the node arm seeds on the declaration alone, deliberately over-approximating node inference, whose other two conjuncts (a @table binding, well-formed node metadata on the bound table) decide what nodehood means and never whether the author declared it. Seeding is monotone, so the superset answers membership correctly, and a type declaring the Relay contract over no table or over defective metadata is a member that gains diagnostics instead of vanishing. Materialized, not a view: the closure over cyclic type graphs has no safe H2 view form (a recursive UNION does not terminate on cycles, and the path-guarded form enumerates simple paths), and the descent rule is graphql-java''s own child semantics rather than something SQL should restate edge kind by edge kind. Written once per capture by a step of the derivation gatherer.'),
  ('graphitron_type_backing', 'type-backing', 'graphitron',
   'A Java class stands for a graph''s type, and how the store knows: one row per type, class and population.',
   'For example a type carrying @table(name: "film") is backed by FilmRecord as BOUND_TABLE, a type a @service method returns a FilmDto for is backed by FilmDto as PRODUCER, and the type of a field read off FilmDto.getActors() by ActorDto as ACCESSOR.',
   'The source object, the class a type''s objects are cast to, stated by the gatherer that owns the directives it is read from. Anchored after the classpath is read: a producer''s resolved method and a member''s delivered class are code_ facts, and a nested type''s class is the class its parent''s member delivers, so the closure follows the schema down from the producers. Three populations because readers ask three questions: which class at all, which a producer grounded rather than a hop reached, and which a table stands for. Each is its own arm and none is preferred; a type answered by two is two rows.'),
  ('graphitron_type_backing_rule', 'type-backing', 'graphitron',
   'One row the type-backing rule computes, in the shape graphitron_type_backing stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example a capture inserts this view''s rows for one graph into graphitron_type_backing, which is the name every reader spells; naming this relation instead asks for on-demand evaluation and gets it.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, on graphitron_field_target_rule''s terms. Recursive over a cyclic graph, which H2 cannot detect a cycle in: the path of type and class pairs a step has visited rides along as an array, and a step never lands on a pair already on it. On a consumer schema of 2345 types the closure walks 136 paths, at most four deep, to 130 backings. Stored by its step because H2 evaluates a recursive view again for every row joined to it.'),
  ('graphitron_argmapping_candidate', 'argmapping-candidate', 'graphitron',
   'What an argMapping right-hand side may name: one row per writable path, under the schema coordinate the directive carrying it sits on.',
   'For example at Mutation.rentFilm(input:) the argument''s own name is one row, every field of the input type below it is another, and the input-prefixed spelling of each of those is a third row marked deprecated, so an author who writes either spelling meets a candidate.',
   'The available member of the argMapping triple: graphitron_ast_argmapping_pair_entry is what an author wrote, this is what a right-hand side may name, and graphitron_argmapping_match is where the two meet. The specification states rules a right-hand side follows, a head naming a position in scope and each later name naming something the value there opens into, and no relation held that set: every reader re-derived it, walking a positional decomposition of the authored path against a second decomposition of the same descent and aligning the two with nested negation. Stating the candidates once turns resolution into an equality and completion into a read of one parent link. Keyed by the coordinate the directive sits on rather than by the container whose members a head may name, which is what lets one relation answer for all three placements without a discriminator. Holding every legal spelling rather than a canonical one is what removes the scope tests two readers spelled by hand: the spelling that repeats the coordinate''s own name is what authors write today, so it is a candidate too, marked deprecated so a warning can prefer the other while both resolve. Where two readings collide the relation keeps the one an author would mean and marks it, an absence that says nothing being the shape this line of work removes. Stored rather than stated as a view because the descent is recursive.'),
  ('sql_schema', 'database-schema', 'jooq',
   'One schema the consumer''s catalog declares, carrying the generated artifacts that belong to the schema rather than to any one of its tables.',
   'For example the public schema of a codegen source, naming the Keys class holding its key constants and the Tables class holding its table constants.',
   'The Keys and Tables classes are per schema, so naming them on sql_table would repeat one value across every table in it. Both are reachable only by loading them off the codegen classpath, so a reader assembling a table or key reference without a live catalog has nowhere else to get them.'),
  ('sql_table', 'database-table', 'jooq',
   'One table the consumer''s catalog declares.',
   'For example public.film, carrying the kind of object it is, the generated jOOQ table class and the record class its rows bind to.',
   'A written table name, the kind an author spells in @table(name:), needs ground to resolve against, and this is that ground: every table jOOQ''s model declares, recorded without resolving anything, since ambiguity is a derivation''s question. It also says which generated classes are a table''s, which no other family does: the classpath census reads the generated jOOQ package''s classes as headers, by name, and which table a class belongs to is this family''s fact.'),
  ('sql_column', 'database-column', 'jooq',
   'One column of one table the consumer''s catalog declares.',
   'For example film.title, a varchar the generated model binds to java.lang.String.',
   'A column is what the schema''s directives name, and it carries two types rather than one, the SQL type the database declares and the Java type jOOQ binds it to. Neither derives from the other by any rule the store could apply, the mapping being the generator''s configured binding, which nothing outside the codegen classpath can resolve afterwards.'),
  ('sql_column_record_accessor', 'database-column', 'jooq',
   'The public no-argument getter this table''s generated record reads one column through.',
   'For example film.title, which FilmRecord reads through getTitle, the Bean Validation property title.',
   'Bean Validation names a record''s values by its getter properties, and nothing else in the store knows them: a record is read and written through the table class''s fields, and the classpath census reads the generated jOOQ package without its members. The name is the one jOOQ''s default generator strategy writes, confirmed on the live record class during the catalog walk, which already holds that class for sql_table_record_supertype; a column whose getter a renaming strategy moved has no row, which is what lets a reader say which columns it cannot check rather than guessing a name. The VALIDATION handler''s per-column check on a jOOQ record parameter reads it, and so does the lint finding naming the columns it misses.'),
  ('sql_enum_binding', 'bound-enum-class', 'jooq',
   'One Java enum class some column of one catalog source binds to.',
   'For example the class generated for the mpaa_rating database type, which film.rating binds to, and equally a hand-written enum a configured converter binds a column to.',
   'The one relation that can answer whether a class is an enum for the generated jOOQ classes, which the classpath census reads only as headers, without their members, a generated enum living in exactly that package. The population follows the question the generator actually asks, Class.isEnum() and nothing else, so it is every enum a column binds to rather than every database enum type: a converter-bound Java enum names no catalog type and would otherwise answer no, in the direction that changes emitted code. Reached through the column walk, so an enum no column binds to is absent rather than denied.'),
  ('sql_enum_binding_literal', 'bound-enum-literal', 'jooq',
   'One constant of one bound enum class, with the value the database stores for it.',
   'For example the constant PG_13 of the class generated for mpaa_rating, whose literal is PG-13.',
   'A @discriminator(value:) names what the discriminating column stores, so where that column binds an enum the value has to be one of the enum''s literals, or the typed comparison binds null and matches no row. The literal is jOOQ''s EnumType.getLiteral(), which only the codegen classpath answers; read here, during the catalog walk that already holds the class, a rule compares a written value against it with no class loaded.'),
  ('sql_constraint', 'table-constraint', 'jooq',
   'One named constraint of one table.',
   'For example film_pkey on public.film, of type PRIMARY KEY, at its position in the table''s uniqueness enumeration.',
   'The supertype: one row per constraint whatever its form, discriminated as the standard''s TABLE_CONSTRAINTS is, so a form the walk does not yet read arrives as a further type value rather than as a new relation. It also carries the two facts nothing else can supply: the Keys-class constant an author types in @reference(key:), which only the codegen classpath resolves, and the position the generated model enumerates its keys in, which is the order a consumer picking one row-identifying key has to pick along.'),
  ('sql_constraint_column', 'constraint-column', 'jooq',
   'One position in one constraint''s column list, naming the column that sits there.',
   'For example position 0 of film_actor''s primary key, naming actor_id.',
   'A constraint''s columns are ordered and are a repeating group, so they are their own relation keyed by position, one relation for all three forms as KEY_COLUMN_USAGE is. A foreign key''s target columns are not copied here; which of the referenced key''s positions each of its columns names is sql_referential_constraint_column''s.'),
  ('sql_primary_key', 'database-table', 'jooq',
   'One table''s primary key, naming the constraint that is it.',
   'For example public.film, whose primary key is the constraint film_pkey.',
   'Keyed by the table rather than by the constraint, because a table has at most one primary key: stating it here makes that cardinality structural instead of a gate query over a type value on sql_constraint. A reader wanting the key''s columns joins on to sql_constraint_column.'),
  ('sql_name_matched_key_column', 'name-matched-key-column', 'jooq',
   'One column of the key that matches a table-valued function''s result to a table by column name.',
   'For example a function result exposing organisasjonskode, matched against the primary key of public.organisasjon, is one row at position 0.',
   'A function result declares no foreign key, so a join leaving one has no constraint to read and the only rule available is the column name. Derived rather than transcribed, on sql_table_record_supertype''s terms: a closure the catalog gatherer computes over rows it has just written, in the family whose facts it is about. Only complete keys are stated, which is what separates this from the intent view it replaces. A key one of whose columns the function does not expose is not a key, so it is absent rather than flagged, and the four consumers that demanded a zero shortfall now join these rows instead of counting them: on the sakila example, four rows where the flagged form held 324. What an author wrote that needed a key which is not here is a fact at that author''s coordinate, a catalog being unable to fail to connect two tables and able only to not connect them. Catalog only, so it carries no graph partition and gates on no directive, which lets one relation serve consumers finding their endpoints in different places. Matching is case-insensitive as the resolver compares, against the arrival''s primary key alone, a unique constraint being no candidate. The position is carried because a consumer building a key tuple builds it in the key''s order. Nothing here says a pair is meaningful: every consumer arrives already holding two ends from somewhere that does.'),
  ('sql_column_spelling', 'column-spelling', 'jooq',
   'One column a written name reaches on a table, folded by case: by its SQL name where any column of the table carries that name, else by its generated jOOQ name.',
   'For example the spelling FILM_ID reaches public.film.film_id by SQL_NAME, with a reach of one.',
   'Which column a written name means is a function of the catalog alone, and it was spelled inline at each rule that asked it: the occurrence column rule and the unresolved and ambiguous column defects each counted sql_column twice per row. Stated once here, those rules join on the folded spelling instead, and a reader that has to resolve a column before its coordinate is an element reads the same rows. Derived rather than transcribed, on sql_name_matched_key_column''s terms: a closure the catalog gatherer computes over rows it has just written, in the family whose facts it is about, with no graph partition. The precedence is the resolver''s: a column''s SQL name before its generated one, the generated one answering only where no SQL name on the table does. Reach is a column rather than a rule inside whoever counts first, so a resolution (reach one) and an ambiguity (reach above one) come off the same rows, as graphitron_tabletype''s arity does, and a spelling no column answers is an absence.'),
  ('sql_referential_constraint', 'table-constraint', 'jooq',
   'One foreign key''s reference to the constraint it resolves against.',
   'For example film_actor''s foreign key to film, referencing the film_pkey constraint of public.film.',
   'The foreign-key-only extension of sql_constraint, split off rather than folded in as nullable columns, so a row exists exactly where a reference does. Referencing a constraint rather than a table is what SQL declares; which referenced column each of the key''s columns names is sql_referential_constraint_column''s. Implicit-path inference is a derivation over these rows, not a fact captured beside them.'),
  ('sql_referential_constraint_column', 'constraint-column', 'jooq',
   'One column of one foreign key, and the position of the referenced key it names.',
   'For example position 0 of reordered_fk_child_parent_fkey, fk_b, names position 1 of reordered_pk_parent_pkey, pk_b.',
   'SQL pairs a foreign key''s Nth column with the Nth column of its REFERENCES list, and that list may order the referenced key''s columns any way it likes, so the pairing is a fact of the key and is captured rather than derived from the two constraints'' positions. jOOQ carries it as ForeignKey.getKeyFields(). The referenced side is a position into the referenced constraint''s own sql_constraint_column rows, not a column name, so the column has one answer, and the foreign keys hold it to the constraint sql_referential_constraint records and to a column that constraint has.'),
  ('sql_table_reference', 'table-reference-pair', 'jooq',
   'One ordered pair of tables at least one foreign key connects, and how many connect it.',
   'For example film_actor to film is one row saying 1, and a pair two foreign keys join is one row saying 2.',
   'How many routes there are between two tables, which a reader that discovers a foreign key rather than naming one has to know before it may take the route at all: exactly one key connecting the pair is a route, and two are an ambiguity nothing here resolves. Counted once per capture over the census rather than per reader, and stated as a relation rather than as a column on sql_referential_constraint, which would repeat one value down every constraint of the pair. Ordered, because a foreign key has a declaring end and a referenced end and a reader asking which table it may reach from where it stands is asking about one of the two directions. Owned by jooq and not by catalog, the gatherer that writes the rows being the one whose stage computes them; fourteen sql_ relations beside it declare catalog and are written here too, which is a pre-existing declaration defect this row declines to copy.'),
  ('sql_index', 'table-index', 'jooq',
   'One index of one table, which is the population @order(index:) and @index resolve against.',
   'For example idx_actor_last_name on public.actor.',
   'The population @order(index:) and @index resolve against, and it is jOOQ''s own: Table.getIndexes() excludes the indexes backing a primary key or a unique constraint, which are in sql_constraint instead. Recording that filter is what makes an author naming a primary key''s index resolve against a documented absence rather than an apparent one.'),
  ('sql_index_column', 'index-column', 'jooq',
   'One position in one index''s column list, naming the column that sits there.',
   'For example position 0 of idx_actor_last_name, naming last_name.',
   'An index is ordered, and a reader that knew only which columns it covered could not tell a leading column from a trailing one, which is the difference between an index a predicate can seek on and one it cannot. Its own relation keyed by position for that reason, as sql_constraint_column is to sql_constraint.'),
  ('sql_routine', 'database-routine', 'jooq',
   'One callable the consumer''s catalog declares.',
   'For example the table-valued function public.films_for_actor, whose generated call surface is the Routines method filmsForActor.',
   'A callable is its own subject rather than columns on sql_table: the standard separates ROUTINES from TABLES, and a routine with no RETURNS TABLE form has a callable and no table at all, so parameters hung off sql_table would have nowhere to go the moment the walk reads one. It takes sql_constraint''s supertype shape, so a form an iteration does not yet read arrives as a further routine_type value. Whether a routine is table-valued is a join to a FUNCTION-typed sql_table row rather than a column here.'),
  ('sql_routine_parameter', 'routine-parameter', 'jooq',
   'One position in one routine''s call-surface parameter list, as the generated method takes it.',
   'For example position 0 of public.films_for_actor, a java.lang.Integer the generated method names pActorId.',
   'A parameter list is what @routine(argMapping:) is matched against, and it is ordered, so one position is one row. It describes one generated method rather than the routine in the abstract, jOOQ generating several call forms per routine, which is why sql_routine names the method these rows belong to. Neither the database''s own parameter names nor its SQL types are here: for a table-valued function jOOQ generates no Routine object, so neither survives into the generated model, and both were left out rather than shipped always-null.'),
  ('sql_node_metadata', 'database-table', 'jooq',
   'One table whose generated jOOQ class publishes node-identity metadata graphitron can use.',
   'For example a film table class stating a type id of film and a key-columns array naming film_id, against a table class stating neither, which has no row here at all.',
   'KjerneJooqGenerator emits two static constants on a table it treats as a node. A row is the pair being usable, a non-empty type id and a non-empty array of columns the table has, judged by the one probe the walk reads too, so whether a class published a node is binary and every reader takes it as found. A pair that is not usable is sql_node_metadata_unreadable''s row and none here: graphitron then falls back to its other tiers, and that relation is what says the attempt was noticed. Under sql_ because the constants ride on the generated package sql_table partitions on and refresh in the same reading.'),
  ('sql_node_key_column', 'node-key-column', 'jooq',
   'One position in the key-columns array of a table whose node metadata graphitron can use, naming the column it resolves to.',
   'For example position 0 of a film table class''s array, naming film_id.',
   'The array is ordered and the encoded identity depends on that order, so a reader recovering the columns from the table''s own key would encode different ids than the ones already issued: the position is the fact and therefore the key. Every entry resolves, an entry that does not making the whole pair unusable, so the column is a foreign key into sql_column.'),
  ('sql_node_legacy_key_column', 'node-legacy-key-column', 'jooq',
   'One position in the legacy key-columns array of a table whose node metadata graphitron can use, naming the column it resolves to.',
   'For example position 2 of an inventory table class''s __NODE_LEGACY_KEY_COLUMNS, naming last_update.',
   'The catalog form of @node(legacyKeyColumns:), for a consumer that states node keys in jOOQ metadata. The position is the fact and therefore the key, being the order of the values inside every id issued under the old key. The foreign key into sql_node_metadata makes a legacy array on a class without a usable identity pair impossible to store, and every entry resolves to a column of the table.'),
  ('sql_node_metadata_unreadable', 'database-table', 'jooq',
   'One table whose generated class declares node-identity metadata graphitron cannot use.',
   'For example a table class stating __NODE_TYPE_ID and no __NODE_KEY_COLUMNS, or a key-columns array naming a column the table does not have.',
   'That an attempt at node metadata was noticed and failed, and not why: the generator configuration that writes the constants is what to fix, and it is the consumer''s, so a vocabulary of the ways it can fail would describe the configuration rather than the schema. Recorded because the failure is not harmless, a type bound to the table encoding its ids from the fallback tiers instead, which is what the NODE_METADATA_UNREADABLE warning tells the author at the type''s @table.'),
  ('sql_node_legacy_key_unreadable', 'database-table', 'jooq',
   'One table whose generated class declares a legacy node key-columns array graphitron cannot use.',
   'For example a table class stating __NODE_LEGACY_KEY_COLUMNS with a null entry, or stating it with no usable __NODE_TYPE_ID and __NODE_KEY_COLUMNS beside it.',
   'Separate from sql_node_metadata_unreadable because the legacy array is an independent axis: a malformed one must not send the table''s identity to the fallback tiers. Recorded because ignoring it is not harmless, every stored id in the old shape then failing to decode at runtime, which is why the defect it raises is an error where the identity pair''s is a warning.'),
  ('code_scalar_constant', 'scalar-type-field', 'code',
   'One constant an author may name in @scalarType(scalar:), and the Java type its scalar coerces to.',
   'For example a DATE_TIME field coercing to java.time.OffsetDateTime.',
   'The first arm of a family whose shape is one gatherer per thing an author writes, rather than one index of every class on the classpath. An index answers what the classpath holds and leaves every reader to re-filter it; this answers what may be written at one directive, and the filter is the arm''s own admission rule. The admission is a classfile fact, a public static field whose declared type is exactly GraphQLScalarType, so the candidate set is read from bytes. The input type is not, and the column says so: it is reached by loading the class, the coercing being a live object rather than a signature. Not reactor-limited, alone among the arms but for the throwables, because the constants an author names are a library''s and the premise that consumer vocabulary lives in reactor source was falsified outright by @scalarType(scalar: "graphql.scalars.ExtendedScalars.Date").'),
  ('store_class_file', 'class-file', 'classpath-source',
   'One class file the classpath reading found, at the entry it was found under and the path it sits at: the unit a reading opens, and therefore the unit it can decline to open again.',
   'For example no/sikt/example/FilmService.class under a consumer''s target/classes is one row, and a file a later compile removed is no row at all.',
   'The entry above it cannot carry this. In a reactor every classpath entry is a target/classes directory, which has no stamp of its own, so an entry-grained invalidation reads as changed on every build and the families below are re-read whole. The file is the grain the reading actually works at, and it is already the grain those families key themselves by, so what hangs off a class file can hang off this row and go when it goes. Store-wide rather than per graph, on the families'' own terms: nothing read out of a class file varies by which graph asked, so a second graph reading the same entry finds the work done.'),
  ('code_method', 'class-method', 'code',
   'One public method of a class the reactor built, which is the population every directive naming Java draws its candidates from.',
   'For example filmsByRating(DSLContext, String) on a consumer''s FilmService.',
   'The shared half of the family, and the correction that makes the arms mean one thing each. Whether a method may be named at @service and whether it may be named at @condition are two questions and two relations; what it returns, what it takes and what it throws are properties of the declaration and do not vary by the coordinate somebody reached it through, so holding them per arm held one fact twice and let two copies disagree. The population is the reactor''s public methods, which is what keeps this from being the census it would otherwise resemble: a census answers for the whole classpath and has to, because it does not know who is asking; this answers for the code a build compiles, because nothing else may be named.'),
  ('code_method_parameter', 'method-parameter', 'code',
   'One position in a method''s parameter list: the type bound there, the name it is bound under, and what the type alone says the position is for.',
   'For example position 0 of filmTitleContains, named film and playing the TABLE_CONCRETE role.',
   'A method''s parameters are the method''s own fact, so they are held once and the arms read them. Written rather than derived because a descriptor states types and nothing else: the name a binding targets is in the MethodParameters attribute, and the role is an assignability question the descriptor cannot answer. The role vocabulary is four values and they are exclusive because no type satisfies two; it is one column rather than one per arm because a position typed as a jOOQ table is typed that way whether the method is reached at @condition or anywhere else, and the arm''s reading of what that means is the arm''s. Keyed on position and not on name, since the name is exactly the part a classfile may omit.'),
  ('code_graph_class', 'graph-class', 'code',
   'The class a graph''s name resolves to: one row per class name any entry on the graph''s classpath declares, naming the entry whose copy the graph sees.',
   'For example a FilmService on the consumer''s own output and on a library jar listed after it is one row naming the output. The sakila example graph holds 5,403 rows.',
   'Graphs on different classpaths share a store, and an entry''s rows are a function of that entry alone, so one class name declared by two entries is two code_class rows and which one a graph sees is a question about the graph: the first entry on its classpath declaring the name, as the JVM would load it. Every rule that resolves a name read that predicate inline, an anti-join over the graph''s entries per candidate, or settled for the first entry by name, which classpath order does not decide. Stated once here, the readers join it on its key. The whole visible set rather than the names some reader asks about, because the readers are many and their questions are not known to the gatherer that owns the classpath. Written by the code gatherer''s anchor stage, after its own reading and the store and classpath-source gatherers it depends on, and rewritten for the graph on every capture, since the order is the graph''s and moves under entries that did not change.'),
  ('code_graph_class_rule', 'graph-class', 'code',
   'One row the graph class rule computes, in the shape code_graph_class stores: the rule itself, evaluated on demand rather than read off disk.',
   'For example the code gatherer''s graph-classes stage inserts this view''s rows for one graph into code_graph_class, which is the name every reader spells.',
   'The rule, kept in the catalog rather than in the step that runs it, on graphitron_field_target_rule''s terms, so the stage and the rule can be compared row for row on every fact document.'),
  ('code_class', 'classpath-class', 'code',
   'One class the reading read, and the file under the entry it was read out of.',
   'For example a consumer''s FilmService on the reactor''s own output, beside a jOOQ interface on a declared jar.',
   'The anchor the rest of the family keys a class to. Three readers wanted it independently and none of them wants anything else about a class: an editor asking whether a name an author typed is one the classpath has, a completion listing what there is, and a condition route telling "this class declares no such method" apart from "this class is not here at all", which a relation of candidates cannot say by itself. Carries no kind and no rendering, because a class''s declared form is already the decision code_read_slot.origin records and no reader has wanted the evidence behind it. Distinct from code_type on population rather than on subject: a type is what a signature names, so it holds java.util.List<Film> which is no class, and a class the reading read that no signature mentions is here and not there. It carries the file the class came out of because that tie is established by reading rather than implied by the path, which is also why the file is keyed by path and not by class name.'),
  ('code_type', 'declared-type', 'code',
   'One type the signatures of an entry''s methods mention, as the source wrote it.',
   'For example java.util.List<no.sikt.example.Film>, beside the plain java.lang.String and the int that other positions carry.',
   'The relation the sites point at, and the reason a parameter is a binding rather than a description. What a type is and what it resolves to do not change with the position carrying it, so stating them per position stated one fact as many times as the signature mentioned it: measured over this module''s main sources, thirteen hundred positions carry two hundred types. Keyed by the spelling because the spelling is the identity, type arguments and packages kept, which is also what tells apart the three silences a resolution cannot: a primitive, an array and a type variable all resolve to no class and are told apart here by their names. Source-keyed like everything else in the family, a type with no entry having no lifecycle to be swept on.'),
  ('code_type_element', 'declared-type', 'code',
   'What a type resolves to once its delivery containers are peeled off, for the types that resolve to a class at all.',
   'For example java.util.List<no.sikt.example.Film> resolving to Film, many of them.',
   'A field backed by a method is backed by what that method finally hands back, and a return type is a tree rather than a name. Stated at capture because the alternative is in the store and measurable: a view peeling containers by self-joining a type-reference relation once per level, unrolled to a fixed depth because SQL has no loop, which bounds what it can answer as well as costing what it costs. Its own relation and not two columns on the type, because a void, a primitive, an array and a type variable name no class, and a placeholder would make four different silences look like one answer.'),
  ('code_type_element_argument', 'declared-type-argument', 'code',
   'One type argument of the element a type delivers, by its position, where it names a class exactly.',
   'For example java.util.List<org.jooq.Row2<java.lang.Integer, java.lang.String>> delivering Row2, whose argument 0 is Integer and argument 1 String.',
   'What a slot taking a node id''s key as a tuple has to agree with: a RecordN or RowN is the key only where its arguments are the key columns'' types in key order. Stated at capture beside the peel that finds the element, since the peel already holds the element''s place in the declared type and a relation of every type reference would make each reader find it again.'),
  ('code_read_slot', 'class-method', 'code',
   'One member name a class offers an author, the method that reads it and what reading it yields.',
   'For example a Film record offering title through its title() accessor, or a FilmDto offering it through getTitle().',
   'What @field(name:) resolves against on a type whose backing is a class rather than a table. Two arms and the discriminator is on the class rather than the member: a record answers with its components and anything else with its getters, which is decided where the class''s declared form is known and stored as the answer. Keyed by the accessor because the accessor is what is unique; the name an author writes is not, a class spelling one property two ways offering it twice. It names what reading the member yields rather than reaching it through the accessor''s own row, which is the shape the write side settled first and for two reasons that hold here too: every reader of a slot wants the type and none of them wants anything else the method has, and an inherited accessor has no row under the class offering it, so a slot typed only through one could not exist. What a class offers includes what it inherits, the emitter reaching an inherited accessor through the subclass without caring which file it came from, and the declaring class is carried so a jump to the member''s source lands in the file that has it. A record''s accessors are ordinary public methods and so is everything else a record generates, so the components the Record attribute names are what tells an accessor from a toString.'),
  ('code_constructor', 'class-constructor', 'code',
   'One public constructor of a class declaring an instance @service method: one row per class and constructor, overloads told apart by descriptor.',
   'For example SampleInstanceService(DSLContext), the one public constructor of a class whose titleByFilmId an instance @service names.',
   'What a generated fetcher calls to get the object an instance @service method runs on, so the holder a field needs is chosen from what the class offers rather than by reflecting on it when the schema is classified. Read only for the classes the reading already found an instance service method on, kept apart from the methods as the census keeps them, since a constructor is named by nothing an author writes.'),
  ('code_constructor_buildable', 'class-constructor', 'code',
   'One public constructor the run can call to make an object an instance service method runs on: every parameter the jOOQ context, at most once.',
   'For example SampleInstanceService(DSLContext) and a no-argument constructor are buildable, and one taking a String is not.',
   'Which constructors the generated code can call, stated once for the holder rule and the defect that reports a class with none, so the two cannot disagree. A view: its readers each look up one class, and nothing constrains it.'),
  ('code_constructor_parameter', 'constructor-parameter', 'code',
   'One position in a public constructor''s parameter list: the type bound there, the name it is bound under, and what the type alone says the position is for.',
   'For example position 0 of SampleInstanceService(DSLContext), named ctx and playing the DSL_CONTEXT role.',
   'A constructor''s parameters are its own fact, held once beside it, on code_method_parameter''s terms and decided by the same role rule, so a holder''s positions and a method''s mean one thing by one vocabulary. Only what a holder needs is kept: the name a context key binds to, the type and its erasure, and the role.'),
  ('code_construction', 'classpath-class', 'code',
   'How a value of one class is made, for the classes a generator has to make one of.',
   'For example a FilmInput record, made by calling its canonical constructor with every component at once.',
   'Reading a value and writing one are different questions and this family answers them separately, because the direction a type is reached from decides which it owes. A type at a result position is only ever read, so what it owes is accessors, which code_read_slot holds. A type at a parameter position has to be made before it can be passed, so what it owes is a constructor and the members that go in through it. The two are not mirrors: a read is per member and a construction is per object, which is why the arms sit here rather than on the members. Captured rather than reflected because the generator reflects for it today, at six sites, to decide a fork it then emits code for.'),
  ('code_write_slot', 'construction-argument', 'code',
   'One member an author can fill when a value of the class is made, and the call that fills it.',
   'For example a FilmInput''s title, filled at argument zero of its canonical constructor.',
   'What @field(name:) resolves against on an input, which is not what it resolves against on an output. An output field is read off an accessor and an input field is written through a constructor argument or a setter, and a class can offer one and not the other: a DTO with a getter and no setter answers an output field and cannot answer an input one. Keyed by the call and the argument rather than by the name, on code_read_slot''s terms and for one more reason of its own: at POSITIONAL one call carries every member, so the argument is the only thing telling them apart.'),
  ('code_method_exception', 'method-exception', 'code',
   'One exception a method declares it throws.',
   'For example filmsByRating declaring java.io.IOException.',
   'Captured because two readers ask for it and neither could be answered without it: the @error channel-coverage check asks whether every exception a producing method declares is covered by a handler on the field''s channel, and @condition''s admission refuses a set of same-named declarations that disagree on the clause. Held on the method rather than per arm because a throws clause is a property of the declaration, which is the whole of this half of the family.'),
  ('code_service_method', 'class-method', 'code',
   'One method an author may name in @service(service:).',
   'For example a public static filmsByRating(DSLContext, String) on a consumer''s FilmService.',
   'The arm over the directive that names the most Java, and the one whose admission is least about shape. @service resolution picks by name from the declaring class and applies no filter at all, so what makes a method a candidate is stated here rather than read off the generator: the reactor built it, it is public, and it is not already an answer to a different directive. A method returning exactly org.jooq.Condition is the condition arm''s and a static one-parameter method taking a jOOQ table and returning org.jooq.Field is the lifter arm''s, and neither answers what may be called for a field''s value. Everything else the generator asks, that the return matches the field''s declared type and that an instance method''s class can be constructed, is a judgement about one application, and the refusal should name the method rather than behave as though it did not exist.'),
  ('code_condition_method', 'class-method', 'code',
   'One method an author may name in @condition(condition:).',
   'For example a public static filmTitleContains(Film, String) returning an org.jooq.Condition.',
   'Admission is the return type read off the classfile and nothing else: org.jooq.Condition, matched un-erased so a consumer''s own Condition type cannot pass, or org.jooq.Field with java.lang.Boolean as its type argument, read off the signature so a raw or non-boolean Field stays out. The second is not what the manual documents; it is what the generator accepts, checking no return type at all, and a consumer schema names it, so leaving it out reported coordinates that generate as unresolved. What the generator does with a named method beyond that, how many parameters it wants and which of them carries the table, is a judgement about one directive application rather than about candidacy, so none of it narrows this population. Reactor-scoped because a condition is consumer code by construction, and the entry origin already says which entries the reactor built, so the scope is a predicate rather than a second classpath.'),
  ('code_external_field_method', 'class-method', 'code',
   'One method an author may name in @externalField(reference:).',
   'For example a public static titleUpper(Film) returning an org.jooq.Field.',
   'The whole of this directive''s contract is a fact about the method, which is what lets the relation be the candidate set rather than an approximation of it: the generator admits a lifter that is public and static, takes exactly one parameter typed as a jOOQ table, and returns org.jooq.Field. Every clause is readable before any schema is consulted, so every clause is an admission rule here and none of them is a column. The one clause left out is the only one that is not about the method, whether the table it lifts from is the table the field was written on, and a reader answers that from the position''s own role.'),
  ('code_throwable', 'classpath-class', 'code',
   'One throwable on the classpath, which is what an author may name as an @error handler''s exception.',
   'For example org.jooq.exception.IntegrityConstraintViolationException, read from the jOOQ jar.',
   'The one arm whose corpus cannot be the reactor. The five method arms reach a class by reading a signature that names it, and the exceptions an author maps are precisely the ones no signature names: jOOQ throws unchecked past the method, so no throws clause mentions them and no reactor walk arrives at one. Harvesting every throwable instead is affordable because the population is small and self-limiting, one class in forty-eight over a real compile classpath, and it is bounded further by that classpath being the compile scope every mojo resolves, so a test-scoped library never reaches it. Admission is a chain and not a name test: the class extends its way to java.lang.Throwable, which is read from classfiles where the chain stays on the classpath and by loading the class where it leaves it, so a chain that cannot be closed admits nothing rather than guessing.'),
  ('code_throwable_supertype', 'class-ancestor', 'code',
   'Every type a throwable is, itself excluded: the superclass chain above it and every interface it or they implement, transitively.',
   'For example IntegrityConstraintViolationException being a DataAccessException, a RuntimeException, an Exception, a Throwable and an Object.',
   'The closure and not the edges, for the reason sql_table_record_supertype holds one: the chain above a throwable runs through java.lang, which is no classpath entry and has no rows here, so the store would hold no edges a closure could be recomputed from and a recursive join would stop at the first JDK ancestor. What reads it is the ranking the harvest owes a reader. Whether a throwable is checked is this relation''s answer rather than a column, java.lang.RuntimeException or java.lang.Error appearing in the closure being exactly what unchecked means, and the same join sorts a specific exception under the family a handler matches on, which is what a DATABASE handler asks when it looks for a java.sql.SQLException in a cause chain.'),
  ('java_file', 'source-file', 'java-source',
   'One .java file whose declarations this store holds, and the stamp they were read at.',
   'For example a consumer''s FilmService.java, under the root it was walked from and stamped with the content hash it was parsed at.',
   'The family''s refresh unit, which is what makes an edit cost one parse rather than a workspace walk: a file is retained while it still hashes to its stamp and rewritten whole when it does not. Its own relation rather than a store_source row because store_source is a capture round''s read set and a .java file is read by neither the SDL walk nor the classpath scan, so this family carries its own freshness bookkeeping and leaves that taxonomy closed.'),
  ('java_class_declaration', 'source-class-declaration', 'java-source',
   'One class, interface, enum, record or annotation declaration written in a source file, at the position the parse read it.',
   'For example the FilmService declaration at line 12 of that file, carrying the doc comment written above it.',
   'Where a declaration is written and what its doc comment says, which the classfile census cannot answer, least of all for the half that matters most: the census reads the generated jOOQ package only as headers, without their members, and that is exactly where a jump from @table or @field(name:) has to land. Keyed by file and name rather than by name alone, because two files declaring one fully-qualified name is malformed Java that a parse still reads, and a name-keyed relation would have to pick one of them.'),
  ('java_method_declaration', 'source-method-declaration', 'java-source',
   'One method declaration on a declared class.',
   'For example the first findFilm declaration on FilmService at ordinal 0, beside a second overload of the same name at ordinal 1.',
   'One row per declaration rather than per resolvable name, so a consumer asking for a name gets as many rows as the class declares and the count is the resolution outcome; that replaces an index which dropped colliding keys into a side set of ambiguous ones and kept a first-declaration-wins view beside it. Arity rather than parameter types, an unattributed parse resolving a type to the unqualified name as written, so the arity is the part that is a fact.'),
  ('java_field_declaration', 'source-field-declaration', 'java-source',
   'One field declaration on a declared class, meaning a variable whose immediate encloser is the class.',
   'For example the FILM_ID constant on a generated Film table class, which is how a column''s own declaration becomes reachable.',
   'A column''s declaration is reachable only here: the generated jOOQ table classes'' column constants are fields at this grain and the classfile census reads that package without its members. Enum constants are fields at this grain too. Parameters and locals are absent, their encloser not being a class, and a field name is unique within a class, so no ordinal sits beside it.'),
  ('javac_diagnostic', 'compile-diagnostic', 'compile',
   'One javac diagnostic from the latest compile round over a graph''s emitted sources.',
   'For example an ERROR at line 42 of a generated FilmResolver.java, carrying the compiler''s own code and rendered message.',
   'The compile oracle''s verdict on what a run emitted, which nothing in the store can derive: whether javac accepts the output is a fact about the compiler rather than about the schema. Graph-keyed and graph-private, a sibling graph''s compile errors being its internals rather than its schema contract, and a round replaces the graph''s rows wholesale so the relation''s content is exactly the published round. Only a dev session ever writes here: in the batch pipeline javac runs in the consumer''s own build after the generator exits, so a batch run''s partition stays empty rather than claiming what it cannot know.');

